(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1d.b26b67baf4.js"]={"18":[{"id":"sdl18_q01","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cell type constitutes approximately 90–95% of all cells found in mature bone tissue and serves as the primary sensor of mechanical strain and regulator of local mineral chemistry?","choices":{"A":"Osteoblasts, which synthesize and secrete osteoid and remain the dominant cell population in cortical bone throughout adulthood","B":"Osteoclasts, which are large multinucleated resorptive cells that account for the majority of cellular volume in cortical bone","C":"Periosteal stem cells, which reside in the inner cambium layer and represent the most abundant progenitor population in mature bone","D":"Chondroblasts, which persist within cortical bone after endochondral ossification and maintain the calcified matrix in adult life","E":"Osteocytes, former osteoblasts embedded within the mineralized matrix and linked to neighboring cells by canalicular processes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Osteocytes make up 90–95% of all bone cells. They derive from osteoblasts that become trapped within the matrix they secrete and differentiate into long-lived mechanosensors connected by canalicular processes. Osteoblasts represent only 4–6% of bone cells and reside on active formation surfaces. Osteoclasts are a small fraction derived from hematopoietic precursors. Periosteal stem cells and chondroblasts are not significant resident populations in mature cortical bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteocytes are the HY \"most abundant bone cell\" — classic single-best-answer. Also know that osteocyte death (lacunar necrosis) is the hallmark of avascular necrosis. COMLEX: Osteocytes respond to Wolff's Law (bone remodels in response to mechanical loading); loss of loading leads to disuse osteoporosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types","whyNot":{"A":"Osteoblasts represent only 4–6","B":"Osteoclasts are a small fracti","D":"Periosteal stem cells and chon"}},{"id":"sdl18_q02","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An osteoblast synthesizes and secretes osteoid prior to its mineralization into mature bone matrix. Which organic component constitutes the primary structural protein within this pre-mineralized scaffold?","choices":{"A":"Type I collagen, which provides the tensile framework upon which hydroxyapatite crystals are subsequently deposited to form mineralized bone","B":"Type II collagen, which is the principal structural protein of hyaline cartilage and is degraded before bone replaces the cartilaginous template","C":"Type III collagen, which is the dominant structural protein of soft connective tissues such as skin and arterial walls in the body","D":"Elastin, which provides elastic recoil and flexibility to connective tissues and is a minor component of ligaments throughout the skeleton","E":"Fibronectin, which anchors cells to the extracellular matrix and facilitates cell migration during bone formation and fracture repair"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osteoid — the pre-mineralized organic matrix secreted by osteoblasts — is composed predominantly of Type I collagen along with non-collagenous proteins such as osteocalcin and osteopontin. Type II collagen is specific to cartilage. Type III collagen is found in soft tissues. Elastin and fibronectin are minor matrix components that do not form the primary structural scaffold of bone. Hydroxyapatite subsequently crystallizes on and between the collagen fibers to create the composite mineral-organic structure of mature bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteogenesis imperfecta is caused by mutations in Type I collagen (COL1A1/COL1A2) — directly linking collagen type to bone disease. COMLEX: \"Blue sclerae\" in OI occurs because the thin collagen allows underlying choroid to show through — a classic clinical pearl often paired with bone fragility questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types","whyNot":{"E":"Elastin and fibronectin are mi"}},{"id":"sdl18_q03","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"During bone resorption, osteoclasts must first dissolve the inorganic mineral phase of bone matrix before the organic matrix can be degraded. Which mechanism accounts for demineralization of the hydroxyapatite crystals?","choices":{"A":"Release of alkaline phosphatase, which catalyzes hydrolysis of phosphate ester bonds within the hydroxyapatite lattice to release calcium and phosphate ions","B":"Secretion of osteocalcin, which chelates calcium ions from the crystal surface through high-affinity gamma-carboxyglutamate residues in the acidic resorption lacuna","C":"Secretion of collagenase and gelatinase enzymes at the ruffled border, which directly degrade the hydroxyapatite mineral phase before the underlying organic matrix is lysed","D":"Secretion of hydrogen ions via an apical proton pump, which creates an acidic microenvironment beneath the ruffled border that dissolves hydroxyapatite crystals","E":"Production of reactive oxygen species by the osteoclast NADPH oxidase, which oxidatively fragment the hydroxyapatite crystal lattice to release mineral ions"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Osteoclasts seal a resorption lacuna against the bone surface using an actin ring, then deploy a ruffled border that secretes H+ via a vacuolar-type H+-ATPase (proton pump). The resulting acidic pH dissolves hydroxyapatite, releasing Ca2+ and PO43−. After demineralization, cathepsins and matrix metalloproteinases degrade the organic matrix. Alkaline phosphatase is an osteoblast marker, not an osteoclast mechanism. Osteocalcin and reactive oxygen species are not the primary demineralization mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Proton pump inhibitors and carbonic anhydrase II inhibitors can impair osteoclast function — carbonic anhydrase II deficiency causes osteopetrosis. COMLEX: Bisphosphonates bind hydroxyapatite and are ingested by osteoclasts, inducing apoptosis — clinically relevant for osteoporosis and Paget disease management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types","whyNot":{"B":"Osteocalcin and reactive oxyge","C":"After demineralization, cathep"}},{"id":"sdl18_q04","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the RANKL-mediated pathway of bone remodeling, which cell type produces receptor activator of nuclear factor kappa-B ligand in response to mechanical and hormonal stimuli, thereby driving osteoclastogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Osteoblasts, which express RANKL that binds RANK on monocyte precursors and drives their fusion into mature multinucleated osteoclasts","B":"Osteoclasts, which produce RANKL in an autocrine fashion to sustain their own activation and amplify bone resorption at sites of high turnover","C":"Parafollicular cells of the thyroid, which release RANKL in response to elevated serum calcium as part of the calcitonin regulatory feedback loop","D":"T lymphocytes, which are the exclusive systemic source of RANKL and drive osteoclastogenesis only under conditions of inflammatory joint disease","E":"Osteocytes, which are solely responsible for RANKL secretion due to their embedded position and direct exposure to mechanical strain signals"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osteoblasts are the principal producers of RANKL in physiologic bone remodeling. RANKL binds RANK on osteoclast precursors, initiating a signaling cascade that drives their differentiation into mature bone-resorbing osteoclasts. While T lymphocytes can produce RANKL in inflammatory conditions (e.g., rheumatoid arthritis), osteoblasts are the physiologic source. Parafollicular cells produce calcitonin, not RANKL. Osteoclasts and osteocytes are not primary RANKL producers under normal conditions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The RANKL/RANK/OPG axis is a classic high-yield pathway — denosumab is a monoclonal antibody against RANKL used to treat osteoporosis. COMLEX: Understand that PTH paradoxically promotes bone resorption (via RANKL upregulation) with chronic elevation but stimulates formation with intermittent dosing — teriparatide exploits this.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance","whyNot":{"B":"Osteoclasts and osteocytes are","C":"Parafollicular cells produce c","E":"Osteoclasts and osteocytes are"}},{"id":"sdl18_q05","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Osteoprotegerin is a key regulatory glycoprotein in the control of bone remodeling. Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which osteoprotegerin limits bone resorption?","choices":{"A":"Osteoprotegerin is secreted by osteoclasts and directly inhibits calcitonin binding at its receptor on the osteoclast surface, reducing the anti-resorptive hormonal response","B":"Osteoprotegerin is secreted by osteoblasts and acts as a soluble decoy receptor that binds RANKL, keeping it from engaging RANK on osteoclast precursors","C":"Osteoprotegerin is a transmembrane enzyme on osteocytes that phosphorylates and inactivates RANKL before it can reach circulation and bind distant osteoclast precursors","D":"Osteoprotegerin is a circulating protease derived from hepatocytes that cleaves PTH in the bloodstream, reducing upstream stimulation of the RANKL-dependent resorption cycle","E":"Osteoprotegerin is produced by T lymphocytes and blocks vitamin D receptors on intestinal epithelium to suppress calcium absorption and reduce systemic calcium-driven remodeling"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Osteoprotegerin (OPG) is a member of the TNF receptor superfamily secreted by osteoblasts. It acts as a decoy receptor by binding RANKL with high affinity, preventing RANKL from engaging its functional receptor RANK on osteoclast precursors. This OPG/RANKL ratio is the key determinant of osteoclastogenesis — high OPG favors bone formation, low OPG favors resorption. It is not produced by osteoclasts, T lymphocytes, or hepatocytes, and it has no direct effect on calcitonin or vitamin D receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The OPG/RANKL ratio is a critical concept — estrogen increases OPG production, which is why postmenopausal estrogen loss leads to decreased OPG, increased RANKL-driven resorption, and osteoporosis. COMLEX: Denosumab mimics OPG by targeting RANKL; contrast with bisphosphonates (induce osteoclast apoptosis directly).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance","whyNot":{"A":"It is not produced by osteocla","D":"It is not produced by osteocla","E":"It is not produced by osteocla"}},{"id":"sdl18_q06","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient has an elevated serum calcium level following excessive vitamin D supplementation. Which cell type responds to this hypercalcemia and what hormone do they release to restore mineral homeostasis?","choices":{"A":"Chief cells of the parathyroid gland, which detect elevated serum calcium and release parathyroid hormone to stimulate osteoblast-mediated bone formation and restore balance","B":"Osteoblasts on endosteal surfaces, which detect local hypercalcemia and secrete an autocrine calcitonin-like peptide that suppresses adjacent osteoclast activity","C":"Parafollicular cells of the thyroid gland, which secrete calcitonin in response to elevated serum calcium to directly inhibit osteoclast activity and reduce bone resorption","D":"Alpha cells of the pancreatic islets of Langerhans, which respond to elevated serum calcium by releasing calcitonin alongside glucagon to promote calcium deposition in bone","E":"Enterochromaffin cells of the duodenal mucosa, which release calcitonin in response to dietary calcium load to limit further intestinal absorption and prevent systemic hypercalcemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Calcitonin is produced exclusively by the parafollicular (C) cells of the thyroid gland in response to elevated serum calcium. Calcitonin inhibits osteoclast activity, reducing bone resorption and lowering blood calcium. Chief cells of the parathyroid produce PTH (released in response to hypocalcemia, not hypercalcemia). Pancreatic alpha cells produce glucagon; osteoblasts and enterochromaffin cells do not produce calcitonin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from C cells and produces excess calcitonin — serum calcitonin is used as a tumor marker. MEN2A/MEN2B involve RET mutation and include medullary thyroid carcinoma. COMLEX: In clinical practice, calcitonin is used therapeutically for Paget disease and hypercalcemia of malignancy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance","whyNot":{"B":"Pancreatic alpha cells produce","E":"Pancreatic alpha cells produce"}},{"id":"sdl18_q07","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In a mature long bone, which statement best describes the direction of blood flow through the bone tissue and the main route by which vessels cross the compact bone?","choices":{"A":"Flow is centrifugal, from the marrow cavity out through the cortex to periosteal veins, entering mainly via Volkmann canals","B":"Flow is centripetal, from periosteal arteries inward to the marrow, with Haversian canals as the main route of entry across the cortex","C":"Flow is longitudinal, from the epiphysis toward the diaphysis along the marrow cavity, crossing the cortex through the growth plate","D":"Flow is circular within each osteon, with blood passing from canaliculi into the lacunae and back without leaving the cortex","E":"Flow is bidirectional and equal, with half of cortical blood entering from the marrow and half from the periosteum at all ages"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pawlina describes blood flow in bone as essentially centrifugal: blood moves from the marrow cavity into and through the bone tissue and leaves through periosteal veins. Volkmann canals provide the major route for vessels to pass through compact bone, and smaller vessels enter the Haversian canals, each holding an arteriole and venule or a single capillary. Periosteal arteries give a lesser supply to the outermost compact bone. A centripetal pattern reverses this. Flow does not run along the bone through the growth plate. Canaliculi carry tissue fluid and osteocyte processes, not blood. The marrow-derived supply dominates, so the contribution is not an equal split. Bone tissue also lacks lymphatic vessels; lymphatic drainage occurs from the periosteum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Long bone supply: nutrient artery (marrow, inner cortex), metaphyseal and epiphyseal arteries, periosteal arteries (outer cortex); flow is centrifugal. COMLEX: Periosteal stripping and intramedullary reaming each injure part of this supply, which matters for fracture healing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — regions of long bones and their blood supply","whyNot":{"B":"A centripetal pattern reverses","D":"Canaliculi carry tissue fluid ","E":"The marrow-derived supply domi"}},{"id":"sdl18_q08","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with a tibial shaft stress fracture reports severe, well-localized point tenderness over the bone. Which anatomical structure is primarily responsible for the exquisite pain sensitivity of bone to injury and periosteal irritation?","choices":{"A":"The inner cellular layer of the periosteum, which contains osteoprogenitor stem cells and is primarily responsible for nociceptive signaling via its dense afferent innervation","B":"The Haversian canals within cortical bone, which contain abundant sensory nerve fibers running parallel to the osteon and serve as the principal pain-sensing compartment","C":"The outer fibrous layer of the periosteum, which is densely invested with sensory nerve fibers and sympathetic afferents that transmit pain signals upon mechanical deformation or injury","D":"The trabecular surfaces of cancellous bone, which are densely innervated with free nerve endings and are the primary source of nociception in long bone injuries","E":"The endosteum lining the medullary cavity, which contains the highest concentration of pain-sensitive nerve endings and is predominantly responsible for fracture pain"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The outer fibrous layer of the periosteum is richly innervated with sensory (nociceptive) and sympathetic nerve fibers, making it the most pain-sensitive component of bone. This explains why periosteal elevation, tumors invading the periosteum, or stress fractures cause exquisite localized pain. The inner cellular (cambium) layer contains progenitor cells but sparse innervation. Haversian canals carry small vessels and nerves, but they are not the primary pain-sensing structure. The endosteum and cancellous surfaces have relatively sparse innervation compared to the periosteum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Periosteal pain is why bone metastases are so painful — tumor expansion stretches the periosteum. NSAIDs and bisphosphonates help reduce periosteal nociception. COMLEX: OMM consideration — the periosteum transmits viscerosomatic reflexes; periosteal tenderness at Chapman reflex points reflects visceral dysfunction and is used diagnostically.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — Primary regions of long bones and their anatomical organization","whyNot":{"D":"The endosteum and cancellous s","E":"The endosteum and cancellous s"}},{"id":"sdl18_q09","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the weeks following a long bone fracture, a fibrocartilaginous bridge spans the fracture gap and stabilizes the fragments. What is this structure called, and what process ultimately converts it into bone?","choices":{"A":"The hard callus, which is a directly mineralized woven bone collar that forms within the first 72 hours after fracture by periosteal osteoblast activation at the injury site","B":"The primary union bridge, which is a direct lamellar bone-to-bone junction that forms when fracture ends are held in rigid contact without any intermediate cartilaginous phase","C":"The remodeling zone, which is the mature lamellar bone structure that replaces disorganized woven bone and is classified as the bridging callus on post-fracture imaging","D":"The soft callus, a fibrocartilaginous scaffold that stabilizes the fracture ends and is later replaced by woven bone through endochondral ossification by invading osteoblasts","E":"The periosteal sleeve, which is an avascular fibrous collar that encases and immobilizes the fracture gap and is later resorbed entirely before new bone can be laid down"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bone repair proceeds through: (1) hematoma formation, (2) soft callus formation — a fibrocartilaginous bridge that stabilizes the fracture, (3) hard callus — the soft callus is replaced via endochondral ossification by woven bone, and (4) remodeling — woven bone is replaced by organized lamellar bone over months to years. The hard callus appears weeks later, not within 72 hours. Primary union (no callus) occurs only with rigid fixation and direct cortical contact. The remodeling zone is the final stage, not the bridging callus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fracture repair stages are classic HY: hematoma → fibrocartilaginous callus → bony callus → remodeling. Know that NSAIDs impair early inflammatory phase (hematoma/soft callus), potentially slowing healing. COMLEX: Smoking and diabetes impair bone repair — smoking reduces osteoblast activity and impairs angiogenesis needed for callus vascularization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — Primary regions of long bones and their anatomical organization"},{"id":"sdl18_q10","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following bones is formed via intramembranous ossification, in which mesenchymal cells differentiate directly into osteoblasts without requiring a cartilaginous intermediate template?","choices":{"A":"The femur, which forms via endochondral ossification in which a hyaline cartilage model is progressively replaced by bone through vascular invasion and osteoblast activity","B":"The skull, in which mesenchymal cells condense into nodules and differentiate directly into osteoblasts that deposit a collagen-proteoglycan matrix that then calcifies","C":"The tibia, which undergoes endochondral ossification from a primary center in the diaphysis that extends toward epiphyseal growth plates at both ends of the bone","D":"The humerus, which develops through endochondral ossification identical to other long bones of the appendicular skeleton, requiring a cartilaginous template at all stages","E":"The vertebral bodies, which are formed through endochondral ossification from somite-derived cartilaginous precursors during embryonic vertebral column development"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Intramembranous ossification forms flat bones including the skull (calvaria), mandible, clavicle, and some facial bones. In this process, mesenchymal cells differentiate directly into osteoblasts — no cartilage template is needed. Endochondral ossification forms long bones (femur, tibia, humerus) and vertebral bodies, which require a cartilage scaffold that is progressively replaced by bone through vascular invasion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cleidocranial dysostosis involves defective intramembranous ossification of the clavicle and cranial vault (RUNX2 mutation) — can present with absent clavicles allowing shoulder hyperflexion. COMLEX: Fontanelles represent areas of delayed intramembranous ossification — the anterior fontanelle closes by 18 months; delayed closure suggests hypothyroidism or rickets.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — Limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation via mesenchymal differentiation","whyNot":{"C":"Endochondral ossification form"}},{"id":"sdl18_q11","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"During endochondral ossification of a long bone, where does the primary ossification center first appear, and which event triggers the initial wave of bone matrix deposition at that location?","choices":{"A":"The epiphysis, where hypertrophied chondrocytes first calcify the surrounding matrix and attract osteoblast precursors to initiate the primary ossification sequence","B":"The growth plate, where chondrocyte proliferation produces the initial calcified template that osteoblasts later colonize to establish the primary ossification center","C":"The periosteum, where mesenchymal progenitors first differentiate into osteoblasts and lay down a periosteal collar that anchors the primary ossification process within the diaphysis","D":"The diaphysis, where vascular connective tissue invades the calcified cartilage model and introduces osteoblasts that deposit bone matrix onto the cartilaginous scaffold","E":"The metaphysis, where blood vessel invasion first occurs near the flared ends of the bone and establishes the ossification center before extending into the diaphyseal shaft"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The primary ossification center forms in the diaphysis (shaft) of the long bone. Mesodermal mesenchymal cells first differentiate into chondrocytes that form the cartilage model. Chondrocytes hypertrophy and secrete a matrix that calcifies. The perichondrium then becomes periosteum as a bone collar forms. Vascular connective tissue subsequently invades from the periosteal region, bringing osteoblasts that deposit bone matrix onto the calcified cartilage scaffold. Secondary ossification centers later appear in the epiphyses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Achondroplasia (FGFR3 gain-of-function mutation) disrupts endochondral ossification at the growth plate, causing the characteristic short-limb dwarfism — long bones are short but membranous bones of the skull are normal. COMLEX: Ossification centers appear at predictable developmental ages — important for reading pediatric radiographs and assessing bone age.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — Limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation via mesenchymal differentiation","whyNot":{"A":"Secondary ossification centers"}},{"id":"sdl18_q12","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Early in endochondral ossification of a long bone, a thin layer of bone is laid down around the middle of the cartilage model. What happens to the connective tissue layer that covers the diaphysis at this stage?","choices":{"A":"It becomes the endosteum lining the marrow cavity, after the cartilage beneath it is resorbed by osteoclasts","B":"It becomes the epiphyseal growth plate, which migrates toward the ends of the bone as the diaphysis lengthens","C":"The perichondrium becomes the periosteum once bone is deposited beneath it around the diaphysis of the model","D":"It becomes articular cartilage, which lacks a perichondrium so that it can exchange water with synovial fluid","E":"It degenerates by apoptosis, because a covering layer is no longer needed once the matrix of the model calcifies"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course slide on endochondral ossification describes the sequence: mesenchymal cells become chondrocytes that proliferate, hypertrophy, and secrete matrix; the matrix calcifies; a thin layer of bone is deposited under the perichondrium surrounding the diaphysis, and the perichondrium becomes the periosteum. Vascular connective tissue then invades from the periosteum, bringing hemopoietic cells and osteoblasts that deposit bone on the calcified cartilage. The endosteum lines the marrow cavity but does not come from this covering layer. Growth plates are the epiphyseal cartilage plates at the diaphyseal-epiphyseal junctions. Articular cartilage covers joint surfaces and is not formed from the diaphyseal covering. The covering layer persists as periosteum and supplies osteoprogenitor cells throughout life.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Endochondral ossification (long bones, vertebrae): cartilage model → bone collar under perichondrium (→ periosteum) → vascular invasion → primary center in diaphysis → secondary centers in epiphyses → growth at physes. Intramembranous: flat bones of skull, direct from mesenchyme. COMLEX: Achondroplasia (FGFR3) affects endochondral growth.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — achondroplasia and endochondral growth plate failure","whyNot":{"B":"Growth plates are the epiphyse","D":"Articular cartilage covers joi","E":"The covering layer persists as"}},{"id":"sdl18_q13","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with known osteogenesis imperfecta presents with his third long bone fracture in two years. Bone biopsy reveals a reduced ratio of osteoid to mineralized matrix and an abundance of large multinucleated cells actively excavating the bone surface. These multinucleated cells are derived from which embryological lineage?","choices":{"A":"Mesenchymal stromal cells of the inner periosteum, which differentiate sequentially into osteoblasts and subsequently fuse into multinucleated giant cells when stimulated by inflammatory cytokines","B":"Hematopoietic stem cells of the granulocyte-macrophage colony-forming unit lineage, which give rise to monocyte precursors that fuse into multinucleated osteoclasts under RANKL stimulation","C":"Neural crest cells migrating from the dorsal neural tube, which provide the progenitor population for all bone-resorbing cells in the craniofacial and axial skeletal compartments","D":"Lateral plate mesodermal cells that differentiate into myoblasts and subsequently transdifferentiate into osteoclast precursors in response to mechanical unloading signals","E":"Endothelial progenitor cells derived from the aorta-gonad-mesonephros region, which give rise to the monocyte-macrophage lineage that fuses into multinucleated bone-resorbing cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Osteoclasts are derived from the hematopoietic granulocyte-macrophage colony-forming unit (GM-CFU) lineage — the same lineage that gives rise to monocytes and macrophages. Monocyte precursors are recruited to bone surfaces and fuse into multinucleated osteoclasts under RANKL and M-CSF stimulation. In osteogenesis imperfecta, defective Type I collagen is produced by osteoblasts (mesenchymal origin), while osteoclasts are hematopoietic-derived and function normally or are even hyperactive. Mesenchymal stromal cells give rise to osteoblasts, chondrocytes, and adipocytes — not osteoclasts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know both lineages — osteoblasts from mesenchymal stromal cells, osteoclasts from GM-CFU/monocyte lineage. Paget disease involves dysregulated osteoclast activity (measles virus-triggered). COMLEX: In multiple myeloma, plasma cells secrete RANKL and inhibit OPG, driving osteoclast activity and the punched-out lytic lesions on skull X-rays.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types"},{"id":"sdl18_q14","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An astronaut returns from 6 months aboard the International Space Station and undergoes DEXA scanning, which shows a 1.5% decrease in lumbar spine bone mineral density. The loss of mechanical loading in microgravity primarily disrupts the function of which bone cell type, leading to the observed reduction in bone formation signaling?","choices":{"A":"Osteoblasts, which are the primary mechanosensors of bone and directly reduce their collagen secretion when gravity-mediated compressive forces are absent from the bone surface","B":"Periosteal stem cells, which rely on repetitive impact loading to maintain their proliferative state and undergo apoptosis during prolonged microgravity exposure","C":"Osteoclasts, which sense the lack of mechanical deformation through integrins on their ruffled border and downregulate their resorptive activity in a microgravity environment","D":"Osteocytes, which sense strain through their canalicular network; with unloading they increase sclerostin, suppressing Wnt-driven bone formation and favoring net resorption","E":"Haversian canal endothelial cells, which detect fluid shear stress changes caused by reduced loading and decrease vascular growth factor secretion required for bone cell maintenance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Osteocytes are the primary mechanosensors of bone. Embedded within the canalicular network, they detect fluid shear stress caused by mechanical loading and regulate bone remodeling accordingly. Under normal loading, osteocytes suppress sclerostin (which inhibits Wnt/bone formation signaling). In microgravity, absent mechanical stimulus leads to increased sclerostin production, reduced Wnt signaling, decreased osteoblast activity, and net bone loss. Osteoblasts produce matrix but are not the primary sensors. Osteoclasts increase activity in disuse osteoporosis but are not the primary sensing cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sclerostin is produced by osteocytes and inhibits Wnt/beta-catenin signaling, suppressing bone formation — romosozumab is a sclerostin inhibitor approved for severe osteoporosis. COMLEX: Wolff's Law states that bone remodels in response to mechanical stress — disuse osteoporosis from bed rest or paralysis is a common clinical manifestation tested on COMLEX.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types","whyNot":{"A":"Osteoblasts produce matrix but","C":"Osteoclasts increase activity "}},{"id":"sdl18_q15","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A histology laboratory slide of cortical bone shows a central canal surrounded by concentric rings of mineralized lamellae with osteocytes in lacunae connected by fine radiating channels. A student is asked to identify the contents of the central canal of this structural unit. Which of the following is correct?","choices":{"A":"The central Haversian canal contains a marrow cavity with hematopoietic cells and osteoclast precursors that replenish the bone-resorbing cell population throughout adult life","B":"The central Haversian canal contains lymphatic channels exclusively, which drain interstitial fluid from the bone lacunae and prevent accumulation of metabolic waste products","C":"The central Haversian canal contains unmineralized osteoid and osteoblasts that continuously deposit new matrix in a centrifugal direction, expanding the osteon over time","D":"The central Haversian canal is an empty conduit that allows transmission of mechanical strain waves between adjacent osteons in the cortical bone structure","E":"The central Haversian canal contains blood vessels and nerve fibers that supply the osteocytes within the surrounding concentric lamellae via diffusion through the canalicular network"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The osteon (Haversian system) is the basic structural unit of cortical bone, consisting of concentric lamellae of mineralized matrix surrounding a central Haversian canal. The canal contains one or two small blood vessels (capillaries/arterioles) and nerve fibers. Nutrients and metabolic products diffuse to and from osteocytes through the canalicular network connecting lacunae. Volkmann's canals run perpendicular to Haversian canals, connecting them to the periosteal surface. The marrow cavity is a separate medullary compartment, not within individual osteons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the osteon hierarchy — Haversian canal (vessels/nerves) → lamellae → lacunae (osteocytes) → canaliculi (connections). Paget disease shows disorganized \"mosaic\" lamellae replacing normal osteons on histology. COMLEX: Osteoporosis thins cortical bone and reduces the number of intact osteons — this explains the increased fracture risk despite apparently normal-looking radiographs until significant mass is lost.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types","whyNot":{"A":"The marrow cavity is a separat"}},{"id":"sdl18_q16","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with stage 3 chronic kidney disease has a serum calcium of 7.1 mg/dL and an elevated PTH of 320 pg/mL on routine labs. Her provider explains that the elevated PTH is acting on bone to raise serum calcium. Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which PTH increases serum calcium through bone?","choices":{"A":"PTH stimulates osteoblasts to raise RANKL and lower OPG, shifting the balance toward osteoclast activation and resorption that releases calcium and phosphate into circulation","B":"PTH directly activates osteoclasts by binding PTH receptors on their surface, causing immediate secretion of hydrogen ions and collagenase without requiring osteoblast intermediary signaling","C":"PTH stimulates osteocytes to release sclerostin, which inhibits Wnt signaling in osteoblasts and thereby promotes unopposed osteoclast-mediated bone resorption over time","D":"PTH induces osteoblasts to produce calcitonin locally, which paradoxically stimulates osteoclast activity through a non-canonical intracrine signaling pathway within bone","E":"PTH suppresses OPG expression in T lymphocytes, reducing the circulating anti-resorptive signal and allowing systemic RANKL levels to drive osteoclast differentiation throughout the skeleton"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PTH acts on PTH/PTHrP receptors expressed on osteoblasts (not directly on osteoclasts). PTH upregulates RANKL expression and downregulates OPG on osteoblasts, creating a pro-resorptive environment that activates osteoclast precursors. The resulting osteoclast activity releases Ca2+ and PO43− from hydroxyapatite. This is the dominant mechanism of PTH-mediated hypercalcemia. PTH does not directly activate osteoclasts (they lack PTH receptors in significant numbers). Sclerostin and calcitonin effects described are incorrect in this context.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyperparathyroidism (primary = parathyroid adenoma) causes hypercalcemia, hypophosphatemia, increased PTH — classic \"bones, groans, moans, stones.\" Renal osteodystrophy (secondary hyperparathyroidism) results from low vitamin D + hyperphosphatemia → low Ca → high PTH. COMLEX: Distinguish primary, secondary, and tertiary hyperparathyroidism — CKD drives secondary; autonomous gland develops into tertiary.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance","whyNot":{"B":"PTH does not directly activate","C":"Sclerostin and calcitonin effe","D":"Sclerostin and calcitonin effe"}},{"id":"sdl18_q17","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with a T-score of −2.7 at the femoral neck is started on alendronate for osteoporosis. Her physician explains that this medication directly targets the osteoclast to reduce bone resorption. Which of the following correctly describes the cellular mechanism by which bisphosphonates inhibit osteoclast function?","choices":{"A":"Bisphosphonates bind RANKL and function as decoy receptors, preventing RANKL from engaging RANK on osteoclast precursors in a mechanism identical to that of osteoprotegerin","B":"Bisphosphonates are incorporated into the hydroxyapatite crystal lattice and increase mineral resistance to acid dissolution, physically preventing osteoclasts from creating resorption lacunae","C":"Bisphosphonates directly bind the PTH receptor on osteoblasts, preventing RANKL upregulation and thereby reducing downstream osteoclast activation throughout the remodeling cycle","D":"Bisphosphonates enter osteocytes through canalicular channels and inhibit sclerostin secretion, indirectly increasing Wnt-mediated osteoblast formation to outpace osteoclast resorption","E":"Bisphosphonates bind hydroxyapatite at resorption sites, are taken up by osteoclasts, block farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate pathway, and trigger osteoclast apoptosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bisphosphonates (e.g., alendronate, risedronate) are pyrophosphate analogs that bind avidly to hydroxyapatite at sites of active bone resorption. When osteoclasts resorb the bone surface, they ingest the bisphosphonate. Inside the cell, nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate pathway, impairing prenylation of signaling proteins required for osteoclast cytoskeletal function and survival, ultimately triggering apoptosis. Denosumab — not bisphosphonates — binds RANKL. Bisphosphonates have no direct action on PTH receptors, osteocytes, or sclerostin pathways.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bisphosphonates inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase (mevalonate pathway) — same pathway inhibited by statins. Side effects include esophageal irritation, osteonecrosis of the jaw, and atypical femoral fractures with long-term use. COMLEX: Bisphosphonates are also used in Paget disease and osteogenesis imperfecta to reduce excess osteoclast activity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance","whyNot":{"A":"Denosumab — not bisphosphonate","C":"Bisphosphonates have no direct","D":"Bisphosphonates have no direct"}},{"id":"sdl18_q18","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old man is found to have a serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D of 12 ng/mL and a serum calcium of 8.0 mg/dL. He is started on vitamin D supplementation. At which anatomical site does vitamin D primarily exert its calcium-raising effect, and what is the mechanism?","choices":{"A":"The proximal renal tubule, where activated vitamin D binds to receptors on tubular cells and directly promotes calcium reabsorption from the tubular filtrate into the peritubular capillaries","B":"The osteoclast surface, where activated vitamin D binds receptors and directly stimulates RANKL-independent bone resorption to mobilize calcium stores into the circulation","C":"The small intestine, where activated vitamin D binds nuclear receptors in enterocytes to upregulate calbindin and calcium transport proteins, increasing active transcellular calcium absorption","D":"The parathyroid gland, where activated vitamin D binds chief cell receptors and directly stimulates PTH synthesis and secretion to drive downstream calcium mobilization from bone","E":"The large intestine, where activated vitamin D stimulates passive paracellular calcium absorption through the colonic epithelium without requiring active transport proteins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The primary site of vitamin D action to raise serum calcium is the small intestine (duodenum and jejunum). Active vitamin D (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3, calcitriol) binds vitamin D receptors in enterocytes and upregulates calbindin and other calcium transport proteins, dramatically increasing transcellular calcium absorption. Vitamin D also increases renal calcium reabsorption and enhances PTH-induced bone resorption as secondary mechanisms, but the gut effect is primary. Vitamin D actually suppresses PTH synthesis (feedback inhibition) rather than stimulating it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vitamin D deficiency → rickets in children (bowed legs, rachitic rosary) and osteomalacia in adults (bone pain, pseudo-fractures). Know the activation pathway: liver (25-hydroxylation) → kidney (1-alpha-hydroxylation to calcitriol). COMLEX: Granulomatous diseases (sarcoidosis, TB) cause hypercalcemia by producing 1-alpha-hydroxylase in macrophages, creating excess calcitriol.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance","whyNot":{"A":"Vitamin D also increases renal","D":"Vitamin D actually suppresses "}},{"id":"sdl18_q19","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old male soccer player sustains a closed mid-shaft tibial fracture. Six weeks later, plain radiographs show a calcified bridge spanning the fracture gap. The orthopedic surgeon explains that this represents normal progression of fracture healing. Which phase of bone repair does this radiographic finding represent?","choices":{"A":"The hematoma phase, in which organized clot at the fracture site has begun to mineralize, forming a calcified scaffold that will eventually be remodeled into compact bone","B":"The soft callus phase, in which fibrocartilaginous tissue spans the fracture gap and is visible on radiograph due to early calcium deposition within the cartilaginous matrix","C":"The remodeling phase, in which lamellar bone has replaced disorganized woven bone and the medullary canal has been restored to its normal architecture throughout the fracture zone","D":"The hard callus phase, in which the fibrocartilaginous soft callus has been replaced by woven bone through endochondral ossification, producing the calcified bridge visible on radiograph","E":"The primary union phase, in which direct bone-to-bone contact between rigidly immobilized fracture ends has allowed osteons to bridge the gap without any callus formation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fracture repair proceeds through four overlapping stages: (1) hematoma formation and inflammation (up to 3-7 days, not visible on radiograph), (2) soft callus formation (about 2 weeks, fibrocartilaginous, radiolucent on X-ray), (3) hard callus formation (6+ weeks, woven bone replaces fibrocartilage through endochondral ossification — this is the calcified bridging callus visible on radiograph), and (4) remodeling (months to years, woven bone replaced by lamellar bone) (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 2). Primary union occurs only with rigid fixation and compressed cortical contact — no callus forms in that scenario.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The 4 stages of fracture repair are high-yield: hematoma → soft callus → hard callus → remodeling. Know that smoking, steroids, and NSAIDs (especially in the early phases) impair healing. COMLEX: Delayed union vs. non-union — non-union occurs when the fracture fails to heal by expected time, often requiring surgical intervention; pseudarthrosis is a fibrous non-union that creates a false joint.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — Primary regions of long bones and their anatomical organization"},{"id":"sdl18_q20","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has a closed tibial shaft fracture. Tissue taken from the fracture gap 5 days after injury is examined under the microscope. Which description best fits the repair stage expected at this time?","choices":{"A":"Woven bone trabeculae lined by osteoblasts bridging the fragments, forming a hard callus that allows full weight bearing","B":"Hyaline cartilage undergoing calcification and endochondral replacement across the whole gap, forming a mature soft callus","C":"Lamellar compact bone with a restored marrow cavity, with osteoclasts removing the last remnants of hard callus","D":"Dense regular collagen bundles aligned along the bone axis, laid down by tenocytes that migrate from nearby tendons","E":"A fracture hematoma with neutrophils and macrophages, necrotic bone ends, and early granulation tissue replacing the clot"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pawlina describes the first stage of indirect fracture healing as an acute inflammatory phase lasting about 1 week: a fracture hematoma forms around the bone ends, the ends of the fragments undergo necrosis, cytokines draw in neutrophils followed by macrophages, fibroblasts and capillaries proliferate, and the hematoma is gradually replaced by granulation tissue. At day 5 this is the expected picture. The fibrocartilaginous soft callus forms at about 2 to 3 weeks, and the bony hard callus needs about 3 to 4 months. Remodeling into lamellar bone with a restored marrow cavity takes months to years. Fracture repair does not use tendon-type dense regular collagen from tenocytes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fracture healing: hematoma and inflammation (~1 week) → granulation tissue → fibrocartilaginous soft callus (2–3 weeks) → bony hard callus (3–4 months) → remodeling (months–years). COMLEX: Inflammation is required for healing; stable fixation speeds union.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — NSAID inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis","whyNot":{"A":"The fibrocartilaginous soft ca","B":"The fibrocartilaginous soft ca"}},{"id":"sdl18_q21","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman had a femoral shaft fracture that united 14 months ago. Serial radiographs over the past year show the bulky callus shrinking, the cortex becoming smoother and denser, and the marrow cavity reappearing across the old fracture site. Which process explains these changes?","choices":{"A":"Formation of a fibrocartilaginous soft callus that is just now stabilizing the fragments enough to allow weight bearing","B":"Delayed union with resorption of the callus by infection, because callus should never shrink after union has occurred","C":"Remodeling, in which osteoclasts and osteoblasts convert woven callus into lamellar bone and restore the original shape","D":"Intramembranous ossification of a new cartilage model, which replaces the hard callus with a second, larger callus","E":"Disuse osteoporosis of the healed segment, in which cortical bone is lost without replacement after immobilization"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pawlina explains that the hard callus gives mechanical stability but does not restore the properties of normal bone. Remodeling converts the woven bone of the callus into mature lamellar bone; osteoclasts remove the remnants of the hard callus, the marrow cavity is restored, and the bone gradually returns to its original shape over months to years. Shrinking callus with a denser, smoother cortex and a reappearing marrow cavity after union is remodeling. The soft callus forms at about 2 to 3 weeks, not after a year. Callus shrinkage after union is expected and does not signal infection. Intramembranous ossification does not use a cartilage model. Disuse loss would thin the cortex, not smooth and densify it while restoring the canal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Woven (immature) bone → lamellar (mature) bone through coupled osteoclast resorption and osteoblast formation; bone remodels along lines of stress (Wolff's law). COMLEX: Remodeling continues for months to years after clinical union.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — remodeling potential of pediatric bone","whyNot":{"A":"The soft callus forms at about","E":"Disuse loss would thin the cor"}},{"id":"sdl18_q22","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A medical student studying embryology traces the sequence of events in intramembranous ossification of the calvaria. After osteoblasts deposit collagen-proteoglycan matrix and calcium salts begin to bind and solidify the matrix, which structural formation is the next observable event in this process?","choices":{"A":"A hyaline cartilage template forms around the calcified matrix to provide mechanical stability before vascular invasion occurs and osteoblasts replace the cartilage with lamellar bone","B":"Osteoclasts are recruited to the calcified region to resorb the initial disorganized spicules, and osteoblasts then deposit organized lamellar bone in a centripetal direction","C":"Chondrocytes hypertrophy and secrete alkaline phosphatase into the calcified matrix, creating additional mineralization fronts that accelerate spicule formation and bone collar development","D":"Growth plate chondrocytes proliferate at the margin of each calcified center and drive longitudinal expansion of the flat bone anlage through endochondral ossification","E":"Calcified spicules radiate outward from ossification centers, and trapped osteoblasts within the solidifying matrix differentiate into osteocytes as the region becomes enclosed by the periosteum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In intramembranous ossification, after osteoblasts deposit collagen-proteoglycan matrix and calcium salts bind and solidify it: (1) calcified spicules radiate outward from the ossification center, (2) osteoblasts trapped within the solidifying matrix become osteocytes, and (3) the entire region — consisting of centers with radiating spicules — becomes enclosed within the periosteum. No cartilage template forms at any stage. Osteoclasts participate later in remodeling but are not the next immediate event. Growth plates and chondrocyte proliferation are features of endochondral, not intramembranous, ossification.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intramembranous ossification produces woven bone initially (rapidly deposited, disorganized collagen) that is later replaced by lamellar bone during remodeling — the same sequence occurs in fracture repair (soft callus → hard callus → remodeling). COMLEX: Fontanelles — the soft spots on a newborn's skull — are areas where intramembranous ossification is incomplete; the anterior fontanelle is the last to close at approximately 18 months.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — Limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation via mesenchymal differentiation","whyNot":{"B":"Osteoclasts participate later ","C":"Growth plates and chondrocyte "}},{"id":"sdl18_q23","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy falls from his bicycle and sustains a Salter-Harris Type II fracture of the distal radius. The pediatric orthopedist explains to his parents that this injury is concerning because it involves the growth plate. Which feature of endochondral ossification makes growth plate injury particularly significant in skeletally immature patients?","choices":{"A":"Longitudinal growth occurs by chondrocyte proliferation and hypertrophy at the growth plate, so disruption here can cause premature physeal closure with limb length discrepancy or angular deformity","B":"The growth plate is the site where intramembranous ossification transitions to endochondral ossification, and injury to this zone eliminates the only mechanism by which flat bones can increase their surface area","C":"All blood supply to the epiphysis travels exclusively through the growth plate cartilage, and fracture at this level causes immediate avascular necrosis of the entire epiphyseal ossification center","D":"The growth plate is the primary location of osteoclast activity in growing bone, and its disruption leads to unchecked osteoblast activity and paradoxical overgrowth of the affected limb","E":"Periosteal stem cells are exclusively located within the growth plate zone, and injury to this region eliminates all appositional growth capacity throughout the remainder of skeletal development"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In endochondral ossification, longitudinal bone growth is driven by chondrocyte proliferation in the zone of proliferation and hypertrophy within the epiphyseal growth plate. Ossification of these cartilage columns at the metaphysis progressively lengthens the bone. The Salter-Harris classification describes fractures involving the physis — injuries to the germinal zone of the growth plate (Salter-Harris III, IV, V) carry the highest risk of premature physeal closure, which can result in limb length discrepancy or angular deformity. Blood supply to the epiphysis comes from epiphyseal arteries that are separate from the growth plate vessels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salter-Harris classification is HY: Type I (through physis, \"SALTER\" mnemonic — Straight), II (through physis and metaphysis — most common, best prognosis), III (physis and epiphysis), IV (all three), V (crush — worst prognosis). COMLEX: Always obtain comparison X-rays for pediatric wrist injuries — the physis can be confused with a fracture line.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — Limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation via mesenchymal differentiation"},{"id":"sdl18_q24","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A first-year medical student is reviewing fetal bone development. She notes that the femur and the skull each begin as collections of mesenchymal cells but develop through entirely different ossification mechanisms. Which single feature most fundamentally distinguishes endochondral ossification of the femur from intramembranous ossification of the calvaria?","choices":{"A":"Endochondral ossification produces only cancellous bone whereas intramembranous ossification exclusively generates cortical bone, reflecting their distinct structural requirements in the adult skeleton","B":"Endochondral ossification requires a hyaline cartilage intermediate replaced by bone after vascular invasion, whereas intramembranous bone forms directly from mesenchymal cells with no cartilage phase","C":"Endochondral ossification is driven entirely by neural crest cell-derived progenitors, whereas intramembranous ossification depends exclusively on lateral plate mesodermal cells for bone formation","D":"Endochondral ossification forms bones that contain only red marrow throughout life, whereas intramembranous ossification forms bones that contain exclusively yellow marrow after puberty","E":"Endochondral ossification proceeds without periosteal formation, relying solely on the perichondrium, whereas intramembranous ossification forms a periosteum that is the exclusive source of appositional growth"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The defining difference between the two mechanisms is the presence or absence of a cartilage template. In endochondral ossification, mesenchymal cells first differentiate into chondrocytes, forming a hyaline cartilage model that is then progressively replaced by bone through vascular invasion and osteoblast activity. In intramembranous ossification, mesenchymal cells differentiate directly into osteoblasts — no cartilage is produced at any stage. Both processes can produce cortical and cancellous bone; marrow composition and periosteum formation are not the distinguishing features. The femur is mesodermal and the skull has both mesodermal and neural crest contributions, but germ-layer origin is not the defining feature of the ossification mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Achondroplasia (FGFR3 gain-of-function) specifically impairs endochondral ossification at the growth plate — long bones are short, but the vault of the skull (intramembranous) is of normal size, producing the characteristic macrocephaly appearance. This distinction is a frequent NBME question. COMLEX: Achondroplasia is the most common form of short-limb dwarfism; foramen magnum stenosis causing myelopathy is a serious complication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — Limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation via mesenchymal differentiation","whyNot":{"D":"Both processes can produce cor","E":"Both processes can produce cor"}}],"19":[{"id":"sdl19_q01","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the primary pathophysiologic mechanism of osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Progressive depletion of proteoglycan from articular cartilage reduces its viscoelastic properties, leading to fibrillation, chondrocyte apoptosis, and reactive subchondral bone changes","B":"Autoimmune CD4+ T-cell infiltration of the synovium drives pannus formation, which progressively erodes articular cartilage and underlying subchondral bone via matrix metalloproteinase secretion","C":"Deposition of calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals within articular cartilage triggers recurrent acute inflammatory episodes that produce progressive joint space narrowing and fibrillation","D":"Systemic elevation of TNF-alpha and IL-1beta drives neutrophil influx into the joint space, producing enzymatic cartilage degradation and periarticular bone remodeling across multiple joints","E":"Uric acid crystal deposition in the periarticular soft tissues and synovium triggers a local inflammatory cascade that produces chronic joint space narrowing and cartilage surface erosion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"OA begins with chondrocyte dysfunction and loss of proteoglycans from the extracellular matrix, reducing cartilage resilience. This leads to fibrillation, fissuring, and eventual full-thickness cartilage loss. Secondary reactive changes — subchondral sclerosis, cyst formation, and osteophyte formation — follow. Pannus, CPPD, TNF-alpha, and uric acid describe RA, pseudogout, systemic inflammatory arthritis, and gout, respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA = loss of proteoglycans → cartilage fibrillation → joint space narrowing + osteophytes + subchondral sclerosis (no periarticular osteopenia). COMLEX: OA is the most common musculoskeletal complaint in primary care; OMM can address somatic dysfunction and muscle imbalance contributing to joint loading.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 4 — Exercise, physical conditioning, and aging all impact joint health; OA is the prototypical age-driven joint failure syndrome","whyNot":{"B":"Pannus, CPPD, TNF-alpha, and u","C":"Pannus, CPPD, TNF-alpha, and u","D":"Pannus, CPPD, TNF-alpha, and u","E":"Pannus, CPPD, TNF-alpha, and u"}},{"id":"sdl19_q02","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following statements best describes the epidemiology of osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Rheumatoid arthritis is the most prevalent joint disease globally, affecting 10 to 15% of the adult population, with peak onset in adults between the ages of 40 and 60 years","B":"Gout is the most common form of inflammatory arthritis and the most prevalent joint disease in men over age 50, with lifetime prevalence exceeding 50% as serum uric acid rises","C":"Psoriatic arthritis is the most frequently diagnosed joint disorder in middle-aged adults, accounting for the largest proportion of primary care musculoskeletal visits annually in the United States","D":"Ankylosing spondylitis is the most prevalent joint disease affecting the axial skeleton, with a radiographic prevalence of 20 to 25% in adults over age 40 in large population studies","E":"Osteoarthritis is the most common form of arthritis worldwide, with prevalence increasing sharply after age 50 and radiographic changes present in the majority of adults over age 65"},"correct":"E","explanation":"OA is the most common arthritis globally. It affects about 70% of the general population over 65 (SDL 5 reading, Symptom to Diagnosis), though symptoms do not always correlate with imaging. Risk factors include age, obesity, prior joint injury, and female sex. RA is far less common (about 2% of persons older than 60 in the same source). Gout is more common than RA but far less than OA, and AS is less common still.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know OA epidemiology — age is the strongest risk factor; obesity drives knee OA specifically. COMLEX: OA is a named COMLEX Clinical Presentation 8 (musculoskeletal) condition; OMM + weight loss + exercise are first-line osteopathic management principles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 4 — Exercise, physical conditioning, and aging all impact joint health; OA is the prototypical age-driven joint failure syndrome","whyNot":{"A":"RA is far less common (about 2","B":"Gout is more common than RA bu","D":"Gout is more common than RA bu"}},{"id":"sdl19_q03","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to OARSI guidelines, which of the following best describes the first-line approach to managing symptomatic osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Opioid analgesics are preferred over NSAIDs as first-line pharmacologic therapy for OA pain, particularly in elderly patients with elevated cardiovascular risk who cannot tolerate COX inhibition","B":"Exercise therapy and weight loss are the foundational first-line interventions, with NSAIDs added for pain control when nonpharmacologic measures alone provide insufficient symptomatic relief","C":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injections are recommended as the initial intervention for all patients with symptomatic knee OA before attempting any pharmacologic or nonpharmacologic trial","D":"Surgical joint replacement is appropriate as the primary management strategy for patients with radiographic OA and pain, regardless of the duration or response to conservative measures","E":"Disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs targeting synovial inflammation, such as methotrexate and hydroxychloroquine, are the first-line pharmacologic treatment for all symptomatic OA patients"},"correct":"B","explanation":"OARSI and ACR guidelines place exercise and weight loss at the top of OA management — they reduce pain and improve function with minimal risk. NSAIDs (topical or oral) are the preferred analgesics when pharmacotherapy is needed. Intra-articular injections are reserved for inadequate response to first-line therapy. Opioids and DMARDs have no role in standard OA management. Surgery is last resort.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA management ladder — exercise/weight loss → NSAIDs/topical agents → intra-articular injections → joint replacement. COMLEX: OMM addresses muscle imbalance and somatic dysfunction that contribute to abnormal joint loading; integrative approaches (acupuncture, OMM) are COMLEX-favored first steps.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 4 — Exercise, physical conditioning, and aging all impact joint health; OA is the prototypical age-driven joint failure syndrome","whyNot":{"A":"Opioids and DMARDs have no rol","D":"Surgery is last resort.","E":"Opioids and DMARDs have no rol"}},{"id":"sdl19_q04","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the preferred imaging modality when neurological symptoms accompany cervical spondylosis?","choices":{"A":"Plain radiographs with flexion-extension views are the gold standard because they detect dynamic instability and cord compression during movement that static MRI cannot assess at rest","B":"CT myelography is the first-line study for all cases of cervical spondylosis with neurological symptoms because it provides superior visualization of bony osteophytes and foraminal stenosis","C":"MRI is preferred because it directly visualizes disc morphology, spinal cord signal changes, nerve root compression within the foramina, and canal dimensions without radiation exposure","D":"Nuclear bone scan is the preferred study for cervical spondylosis because it identifies active metabolic bone turnover at degenerated levels that radiographs and MRI cannot adequately detect","E":"Ultrasound of the cervical spine is the recommended initial imaging for cervical spondylosis because it provides dynamic visualization with low cost and absence of ionizing radiation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MRI is the imaging of choice when cervical spondylosis produces neurological symptoms, as it shows cord signal (T2 hyperintensity = myelomalacia), disc morphology, and nerve root compression. Plain radiographs diagnose and monitor spondylosis (narrowing, osteophytes) but cannot visualize cord or soft tissue. CT myelography is reserved for MRI-contraindicated patients. Bone scan and ultrasound have no role in standard evaluation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical spondylosis = disc + osteophyte + facet arthropathy. X-ray shows it; MRI defines cord/root involvement. COMLEX: Cervical somatic dysfunction at C5-C6 is a common OMM indication in cervical spondylosis patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Bone remodeling and RANKL/OPG balance govern vertebral osteophyte formation, the structural hallmark of cervical spondylosis","whyNot":{"A":"Plain radiographs diagnose and","B":"CT myelography is reserved for","D":"Bone scan and ultrasound have ","E":"Bone scan and ultrasound have "}},{"id":"sdl19_q05","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the approximate prevalence of radiographic cervical spondylosis by age 65, and how does this correlate with symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Cervical spondylosis is radiographically present in approximately 40% of individuals by age 65, with clinical symptoms developing in roughly half of those with positive imaging findings","B":"Radiographic cervical spondylosis is present in approximately 95% of individuals by age 65, but the degree of radiographic change correlates poorly with clinical symptoms","C":"By age 65, radiographic cervical spondylosis is detectable in fewer than 60% of adults, and the presence of osteophytes strongly predicts future development of radiculopathy or myelopathy","D":"Cervical spondylosis develops radiographically in approximately 75% of individuals by age 65, with the C2-C3 level most commonly affected due to its high segmental mobility","E":"Cervical spondylosis affects approximately 50% of adults over age 65, with symptomatic disease developing in the majority of those with radiographic disc space narrowing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Radiographic cervical spondylosis — disc space narrowing, osteophytes, endplate sclerosis — is nearly universal by age 65 (~95%). However, there is a marked disconnect between imaging and symptoms; many patients with severe radiographic changes are asymptomatic. This makes imaging findings alone insufficient to guide management decisions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key concept — radiographic spondylosis ≠ clinical disease. Do not treat the image. COMLEX: Cervical spondylosis is a COMLEX-recognized condition where somatic dysfunction contributes to symptom amplification; OMM targets the functional component.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Bone remodeling and RANKL/OPG balance govern vertebral osteophyte formation, the structural hallmark of cervical spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q06","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best characterizes the typical clinical presentation of cervical spondylosis?","choices":{"A":"Acute onset of bilateral arm weakness and bilateral loss of upper extremity reflexes, reflecting simultaneous multilevel nerve root compression across all cervical segments bilaterally","B":"Severe, localized neck pain occurring in discrete episodic attacks separated by completely pain-free intervals of weeks to months, without any associated stiffness or range of motion restriction","C":"Prominent lower extremity signs including bilateral leg weakness and gait difficulty as the dominant presenting features, reflecting early cervical myelopathy before any axial neck pain","D":"Axial neck pain, periscapular aching, and shoulder girdle stiffness with reduced cervical range of motion, often with intermittent upper extremity radiation but without fixed neurological deficits","E":"Cranial nerve deficits including facial numbness and diplopia caused by vertebrobasilar insufficiency from upper cervical disc disease at C1-C2 as the primary presenting complaint"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cervical spondylosis most often presents with chronic axial neck pain, stiffness, and periscapular discomfort. Upper extremity radiation may occur. Fixed neurological deficits (weakness, reflex loss) suggest radiculopathy; bilateral long-tract signs (hyperreflexia, gait instability) suggest myelopathy — both are complications rather than the typical spondylosis presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spondylosis = neck pain + stiffness ± referred shoulder pain. Radiculopathy = adds dermatomal arm pain/weakness/reflex loss. Myelopathy = adds UMN signs + gait disturbance. COMLEX: Cervical somatic dysfunction with restricted sidebending and paraspinal hypertonicity is the OMM target.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Bone remodeling and RANKL/OPG balance govern vertebral osteophyte formation, the structural hallmark of cervical spondylosis","whyNot":{"A":"Fixed neurological deficits (w","C":"Fixed neurological deficits (w"}},{"id":"sdl19_q07","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best characterizes the diagnostic properties of Spurling's test for cervical radiculopathy?","choices":{"A":"Spurling's test has approximately 90% sensitivity and 45% specificity, meaning a negative result effectively rules out cervical nerve root compression in most clinical settings","B":"Spurling's test has approximately 90% specificity and 45% sensitivity, meaning a positive result strongly suggests nerve root compression but a negative result does not reliably exclude it","C":"Spurling's test has approximately 70% sensitivity and 70% specificity, representing a balanced trade-off that makes it equally useful as both a rule-in and rule-out test for radiculopathy","D":"Spurling's test has equal sensitivity and specificity of approximately 80%, and a positive result combined with MRI findings confirms the need for immediate surgical foraminotomy","E":"Spurling's test has approximately 45% specificity for cervical radiculopathy, which limits its diagnostic utility due to high rates of false-positive results in patients with axial neck pain alone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Spurling's test (pushing down on top of the head with the neck extended and the head leaning toward the symptomatic side) is a specific but insensitive test: 90% specific, 45% sensitive (Tintinalli Table 279-2, the SDL 19 course reading). This means a positive test is highly meaningful (rules in radiculopathy), but a negative test does not rule it out. It is best used in conjunction with history, dermatomal examination, and MRI.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spurling = 90% specific, 45% sensitive — use it to RULE IN cervical radiculopathy. COMLEX: Cervical radiculopathy is a named COMLEX Clinical Presentation; OMM including cervical muscle energy and HVLA (with caution in stenosis) addresses associated somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow and cervical radiculopathy share a foraminal compression mechanism producing dermatomal sensory loss"},{"id":"sdl19_q08","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with cervical radiculopathy has numbness in the lateral forearm, thumb, and index finger along with weakness of elbow flexion and forearm supination. Which nerve root is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"C7 radiculopathy produces sensory loss in the medial forearm, ring finger, and little finger with weakness of elbow extension and a diminished triceps reflex at the C7 level","B":"C5 radiculopathy produces sensory loss over the lateral arm and deltoid region with weakness of shoulder abduction and a diminished biceps reflex at the C5 root level","C":"C8 radiculopathy produces sensory loss in the medial forearm with intrinsic hand muscle weakness and diminished grip strength without a reliable deep tendon reflex loss","D":"C6 radiculopathy produces sensory loss in the lateral forearm, thumb, and index finger with weakness of elbow flexion and supination and a diminished brachioradialis reflex","E":"T1 radiculopathy produces sensory loss over the medial arm and axilla with interosseous muscle weakness and bilateral reflex changes from bilateral nerve root involvement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"C6 is compressed at C5-C6 and covers the lateral forearm, thumb, and index finger. Motor: elbow flexion, forearm supination/pronation (course root table). Reflex: brachioradialis. C5 = lateral arm/deltoid, shoulder abduction, biceps reflex. C7 = middle finger, triceps, triceps reflex. C8 = ring/little finger, finger flexion, no reliable reflex.","boardPrep":"Step 1 HY nerve root table: C5 = deltoid/biceps reflex; C6 = elbow flexion and forearm supination/BR (or biceps) reflex/thumb-index; C7 = triceps/triceps reflex/middle finger; C8 = hand intrinsics/ring-little. COMLEX: Cervical somatic dysfunction at C5-C6 commonly accompanies C6 radiculopathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow and cervical radiculopathy share a foraminal compression mechanism producing dermatomal sensory loss","whyNot":{"A":"C7 = middle finger, triceps, t","C":"C8 = ring/little finger, finge"}},{"id":"sdl19_q09","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes appropriate initial management of acute cervical radiculopathy without progressive neurological deficits?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical discectomy is indicated when Spurling's test is positive, as nerve root inflammation from disc herniation will not resolve within an acceptable timeframe without decompression","B":"Emergent cervical traction as the primary intervention, applied continuously at 10 to 15 pounds for 24-hour periods until the radicular arm pain has fully subsided","C":"Activity modification, analgesics, a short course of oral glucocorticoids for severe pain, and a soft cervical collar for comfort, with physical therapy and OMM added as symptoms allow","D":"Epidural steroid injection within the first 48 hours is mandatory, as early neural blockade is the only intervention proven to prevent permanent neurological deficits from nerve root inflammation","E":"High-dose intravenous methylprednisolone for 3 days followed by surgical foraminotomy, as conservative measures are uniformly ineffective for acute nerve root inflammation in cervical radiculopathy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The majority of acute cervical radiculopathy resolves with conservative management. First-line includes activity modification, NSAIDs/analgesics, a short collar for comfort, and a brief glucocorticoid taper for severe pain. PT, cervical traction, and OMM are added as tolerated. Epidural injections are reserved for persistent symptoms. Surgery is indicated only for progressive neurological deficits or failure of conservative care.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Most cervical radiculopathy resolves conservatively. Surgery only for motor deficits or failed conservative therapy. COMLEX: OMM is a first-line adjunct — cervical HVLA is relatively contraindicated with significant stenosis, but muscle energy and soft tissue techniques are appropriate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow and cervical radiculopathy share a foraminal compression mechanism producing dermatomal sensory loss","whyNot":{"A":"Surgery is indicated only for ","B":"PT, cervical traction, and OMM","D":"Epidural injections are reserv"}},{"id":"sdl19_q10","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following neurological findings distinguishes cervical myelopathy from uncomplicated cervical radiculopathy?","choices":{"A":"Lower motor neuron signs exclusively, including flaccid paralysis, areflexia, and fasciculations at all levels below the stenotic segment, characterize cervical myelopathy and distinguish it from radiculopathy","B":"Loss of all deep tendon reflexes bilaterally is the hallmark of cervical myelopathy, reflecting global axon degeneration within the central spinal cord at the level of the stenotic segment","C":"Autonomic instability including orthostatic hypotension, anhidrosis, and bowel and bladder incontinence is the earliest and most distinguishing finding of cervical myelopathy from cervical radiculopathy","D":"Strictly unilateral upper and lower extremity weakness with contralateral sensory loss is the classical presentation of cervical myelopathy, reflecting a Brown-Sequard cord hemisection pattern","E":"Upper motor neuron signs — including hyperreflexia below the level of cord compression, spastic gait, positive Hoffman's sign, and positive Babinski sign — indicate spinal cord involvement characteristic of myelopathy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Myelopathy = spinal cord compression → UMN signs: hyperreflexia, spasticity, Hoffman's sign (finger flexion on flicking middle finger), Babinski, clonus, and gait disturbance. Radiculopathy = nerve root compression → LMN signs at the affected root level (weakness, hyporeflexia, dermatomal sensory loss) without long-tract involvement. These are fundamentally distinct clinical syndromes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myelopathy = UMN signs (hyperreflexia, Hoffman's, Babinski, spastic gait) + hand clumsiness. Hoffman's sign = flicking the middle finger nail produces involuntary flexion of thumb/index. COMLEX: OMM (HVLA) is CONTRAINDICATED in cervical myelopathy due to cord vulnerability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — Structural features of synovial and fibrous joints explain how facet arthropathy and ligamentum flavum hypertrophy narrow the cervical canal","whyNot":{"A":"Radiculopathy = nerve root com"}},{"id":"sdl19_q11","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes Lhermitte's sign and its clinical significance?","choices":{"A":"Lhermitte's sign is an electric shock-like sensation down the spine and into the limbs with neck flexion, indicating posterior column dysfunction from cord compression or demyelination","B":"Lhermitte's sign is a pain response elicited by axial compression of the cervical spine in neutral position, indicating facet joint arthropathy at the symptomatic cervical level","C":"Lhermitte's sign refers to reproduction of radicular arm pain with simultaneous ipsilateral neck rotation and extension, equivalent to Spurling's maneuver performed in the supine position","D":"Lhermitte's sign is defined as reproduction of radicular symptoms by tapping the posterior cervical spinous processes, indicating foraminal nerve root impingement at the level tapped","E":"Lhermitte's sign is involuntary finger flexion elicited by flicking the middle finger downward, indicating upper motor neuron dysfunction in the corticospinal tract at or above C5"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lhermitte's sign (neck flexion → electric shock down spine into limbs) indicates posterior column pathology — compressed or demyelinated dorsal column axons generate ectopic impulses when mechanically deformed by flexion. It occurs in cervical myelopathy from stenosis but is also classic for MS. Note: involuntary finger flexion on middle-finger flick = Hoffman's sign, not Lhermitte's.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lhermitte's = neck flexion → electric shock down spine. Seen in: MS, cervical myelopathy, B12 deficiency. Hoffman's = flick middle finger → thumb/index flex = UMN sign. Both appear on boards — do not confuse. COMLEX: Both findings contraindicate cervical HVLA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — Structural features of synovial and fibrous joints explain how facet arthropathy and ligamentum flavum hypertrophy narrow the cervical canal"},{"id":"sdl19_q12","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the treatment approach for cervical spinal stenosis compared to uncomplicated cervical spondylosis?","choices":{"A":"Cervical spinal stenosis is treated exclusively with immediate surgical laminectomy, as conservative measures uniformly fail and delays increase the risk of permanent spinal cord injury","B":"Cervical spinal stenosis is managed with epidural corticosteroid injections as the primary intervention, providing durable cord decompression equivalent to surgical outcomes in most patients","C":"Initial management mirrors cervical spondylosis — NSAIDs, physical therapy, and OMM — but surgical decompression becomes indicated when myelopathic signs develop or progressive neurological deficits occur","D":"Cervical spinal stenosis requires 12 weeks of immobilization in a rigid cervical orthosis as the primary treatment, after which most patients achieve complete neurological recovery without surgery","E":"Cervical spinal stenosis is treated with long-term oral corticosteroids to reduce cord edema, combined with muscle relaxants for associated paraspinal spasm and cervicogenic headache"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Without myelopathy, cervical stenosis is managed like spondylosis: activity modification, NSAIDs, PT, OMM (with caution — no HVLA in significant stenosis). Surgical indication: myelopathy, progressive neurological deficits, or failure of adequate conservative therapy. Epidural injections may help radiculopathy but do not decompress the cord. Immobilization alone is not definitive treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surgical decompression (laminoplasty or laminectomy with fusion) for myelopathy. Conservative management for asymptomatic or radiculopathy-only stenosis. COMLEX: Soft tissue OMM and muscle energy are safe in stenosis; HVLA at the level of stenosis is contraindicated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — Structural features of synovial and fibrous joints explain how facet arthropathy and ligamentum flavum hypertrophy narrow the cervical canal","whyNot":{"B":"Epidural injections may help r","D":"Immobilization alone is not de"}},{"id":"sdl19_q13","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the lifetime prevalence of low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Up to 80% of people experience at least one significant episode of low back pain during their lifetime, making it one of the most prevalent and economically burdensome musculoskeletal conditions","B":"Approximately 30% of adults experience at least one clinically significant episode of low back pain during their lifetime, with the majority recovering fully without any medical intervention","C":"Lifetime prevalence of low back pain is approximately 50%, with the highest incidence in adults aged 25 to 45 and prevalence declining significantly after age 60 as activity levels decrease","D":"Low back pain affects approximately 20% of adults at any given time, and the majority of primary care musculoskeletal visits involve lumbar spine disease as the chief complaint","E":"Approximately 65% of adults experience at least one episode of disabling low back pain, with chronic pain developing in one-third and representing the leading cause of opioid prescribing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"LBP lifetime prevalence is ~80%. It is the leading cause of disability worldwide and a top reason for primary care visits. Most acute episodes resolve within 4-6 weeks with conservative management (80% to 90% of patients, Tintinalli Ch 279). Psychosocial factors (yellow flags) are stronger predictors of chronification than structural findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 80% lifetime prevalence; most acute LBP resolves without imaging. Red flags: age >50, fever, weight loss, cancer history, IV drug use, neurological deficit, bladder/bowel dysfunction — warrant further workup. COMLEX: LBP is the most common indication for OMM referral; somatic dysfunction is a recognized contributor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — Low back pain is a primary indication for integrative medicine; OMM, acupuncture, and exercise are COMLEX-recognized management strategies"},{"id":"sdl19_q14","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the hallmark clinical presentation of cauda equina syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Unilateral sciatica with an absent Achilles reflex and foot drop from single-level L5-S1 nerve root compression that resolves fully with epidural steroid injection within 2 to 3 weeks","B":"Saddle area anesthesia affecting the perineum, inner thighs, and genitalia, combined with bilateral lower extremity weakness and bladder or bowel dysfunction, representing a surgical emergency","C":"Fever, elevated ESR, and bilateral radicular leg pain from infectious epidural abscess that requires IV antibiotics and not urgent surgical decompression in most immunocompetent patients","D":"Bilateral Hoffman's signs and spastic paraplegia from upper motor neuron involvement caused by thoracic spinal cord compression rather than a lumbosacral cauda equina process","E":"Midline low back pain, bilateral hamstring tightness, and positive bilateral straight leg raise without any neurological deficits or sphincter involvement requiring emergent intervention"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cauda equina syndrome (CES) is caused by massive central disc herniation or other mass compressing the cauda equina at or below the conus. Classic triad: saddle anesthesia, bladder/bowel dysfunction (the course slide lists usually incontinence; retention also occurs), bilateral leg weakness/pain. This is a surgical emergency — emergent MRI and urgent decompression within 48 hours to prevent permanent sphincter loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cauda equina = saddle anesthesia + bladder/bowel dysfunction + bilateral leg weakness = EMERGENCY. Next step = emergent MRI lumbar spine. COMLEX: CES is an absolute contraindication to OMM; immediate surgical referral is required.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — Low back pain is a primary indication for integrative medicine; OMM, acupuncture, and exercise are COMLEX-recognized management strategies"},{"id":"sdl19_q15","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes first-line management of acute mechanical low back pain without red flags or neurological deficits?","choices":{"A":"Complete bed rest for 2 to 3 weeks combined with opioid analgesics, as early mobilization risks converting an acute injury to chronic pain syndrome through sensitization of spinal pain generators","B":"Immediate lumbar MRI to exclude disc herniation, followed by epidural steroid injection within 72 hours to prevent progression of mechanical pain to chronic radiculopathy","C":"Oral corticosteroids for 10 to 14 days with strict activity restriction, as prostaglandin-mediated inflammation is the primary driver of acute mechanical low back pain tissue damage","D":"Urgent referral to spine surgery within the first week, as structural instability from acute muscle and ligament injury requires evaluation for possible surgical stabilization","E":"Relative rest with continuation of tolerated activities, NSAIDs for analgesia, superficial heat application, and early physical therapy; prolonged bed rest is discouraged as it worsens outcomes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acute mechanical LBP management: stay active (bed rest worsens outcomes), NSAIDs/acetaminophen, heat, and early PT. Muscle relaxants may be added short-term for spasm. OMM is appropriate and COMLEX-favored. Opioids are not first-line. Imaging is not indicated in the first 4-6 weeks without red flags. Most cases resolve within 6 weeks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: No imaging for acute LBP without red flags in first 4-6 weeks. NSAIDs > acetaminophen for LBP. Avoid prolonged bed rest. COMLEX: OMM (soft tissue, muscle energy, counterstrain) for lumbar somatic dysfunction is a high-yield first-line intervention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — Low back pain is a primary indication for integrative medicine; OMM, acupuncture, and exercise are COMLEX-recognized management strategies","whyNot":{"B":"Imaging is not indicated in th"}},{"id":"sdl19_q16","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best characterizes the pain pattern of lumbosacral spondylosis?","choices":{"A":"Pain and stiffness are worst upon waking and improve gradually with movement and activity through the day, reflecting joint and disc stiffness that loosens with use","B":"Pain is worst at the end of the workday after sustained activity and loading, and is completely relieved within minutes of lying down flat in the supine position","C":"Pain is intermittent and colicky in character, radiating to the groin, and associated with nausea, distinguishing it from mechanical causes of low back pain","D":"Pain is constant and unrelenting, waking the patient from sleep at night and unresponsive to any positional change, consistent with an underlying destructive or malignant process","E":"Pain occurs exclusively with lumbar flexion and is absent with extension, reflecting anterior disc pathology and differentiating it from posterior element or facet-mediated pain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"LS spondylosis (age-related DDD and facet OA) causes morning stiffness that improves with activity — the classic \"gelling\" phenomenon of degenerative joint disease. Pain may be localized or refer to the buttocks. Night pain, constitutional symptoms, or pain unresponsive to position should raise concern for malignancy or infection (red flags). Colicky radiation to groin suggests renal/urological etiology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Morning stiffness improving with activity = OA/spondylosis pattern. Night pain + weight loss + fever = red flags for malignancy or infection. COMLEX: Lumbar spondylosis commonly presents with restricted spinal motion and somatic dysfunction — OMM is integral to management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Osteoblast and osteocyte dysfunction at vertebral endplates drives the disc space narrowing and sclerosis characteristic of lumbosacral spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q17","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which imaging modality is preferred when lumbosacral spondylosis presents with neurological symptoms such as leg pain or weakness?","choices":{"A":"Plain lumbar radiographs with oblique views are the gold standard when neurological symptoms accompany spondylosis, as they directly visualize foraminal narrowing at each lumbar level","B":"CT myelography is the first-line imaging for all patients with lumbosacral spondylosis and neurological symptoms, surpassing MRI in detecting foraminal stenosis and arachnoid adhesions","C":"Nuclear medicine bone scan is indicated for all patients with lumbosacral spondylosis and radicular symptoms, identifying active metabolic changes at degenerated levels better than cross-sectional imaging","D":"MRI is the preferred study because it simultaneously assesses disc integrity, nerve root compression, vertebral endplate Modic changes, ligamentum flavum hypertrophy, and spinal canal dimensions","E":"Discography is the preferred initial imaging for lumbosacral spondylosis with neurological symptoms, identifying the pain-generating disc level through provocative contrast injection testing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"MRI is the imaging workhorse for lumbar spondylosis with neurological symptoms: it shows disc degeneration (T2 signal loss), herniation, end plate Modic changes, facet hypertrophy, ligamentum flavum thickening, and foraminal/central stenosis. Plain X-rays show alignment, disc height, and osteophytes but cannot visualize soft tissue or nerve roots. CT myelography is used when MRI is contraindicated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI for neurological symptoms with lumbar spondylosis. X-ray for initial spondylosis workup (alignment, disc height, osteophytes). CT if MRI contraindicated. COMLEX: MRI guides surgical planning; OMM is appropriate while awaiting imaging or during conservative management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Osteoblast and osteocyte dysfunction at vertebral endplates drives the disc space narrowing and sclerosis characteristic of lumbosacral spondylosis","whyNot":{"A":"Plain X-rays show alignment, d","B":"CT myelography is used when MR"}},{"id":"sdl19_q18","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the prevalence of lumbosacral spondylosis in the general population?","choices":{"A":"Lumbosacral spondylosis affects fewer than 20% of adults over age 50, with most lumbar degenerative changes representing clinically insignificant incidental imaging findings","B":"Lumbosacral spondylosis has a uniform prevalence of approximately 30% across all adult age groups with no significant increase in prevalence associated with advancing age","C":"Prevalence ranges from 38% to 85% across studied populations, varying with age, imaging modality, and the specific radiographic criteria applied for diagnosis","D":"Lumbosacral spondylosis affects nearly 95% of adults over age 60, mirroring cervical spondylosis prevalence, with most cases being symptomatic and requiring clinical management","E":"Lumbosacral spondylosis is present in fewer than 10% of adults aged 40 to 60 on MRI, increasing only modestly to 25% in those over age 70 in large epidemiologic studies"},"correct":"C","explanation":"LS spondylosis prevalence is wide — 38-85% depending on the population and criteria used — and increases with age. This wide range reflects the spectrum from mild disc height loss to severe multilevel disease. Like cervical spondylosis, radiographic findings often do not correlate with symptoms. Over 50% of adults without low back pain have bulging or degenerative discs on imaging (Foundations Ch 42).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Degenerative changes are common and often asymptomatic — do not over-image or over-treat based on MRI findings alone. Treat the patient, not the picture. COMLEX: Somatic dysfunction at lumbar levels is a distinct finding from spondylosis and should be addressed with OMM regardless of imaging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Osteoblast and osteocyte dysfunction at vertebral endplates drives the disc space narrowing and sclerosis characteristic of lumbosacral spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q19","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which deep tendon reflex is diminished or absent in L4 nerve root compression, and what motor deficit accompanies it?","choices":{"A":"L4 radiculopathy diminishes the Achilles reflex and produces weakness of plantar flexion from S1 myotome involvement, with sensory loss over the lateral foot and little toe","B":"L4 radiculopathy diminishes the brachioradialis reflex and produces weakness of wrist extension, reflecting a mid-cervical level nerve root rather than a lumbar radiculopathy","C":"L4 radiculopathy produces no reliable reflex change because the L4 root is assessed only by sensory testing over the medial calf without any associated deep tendon reflex","D":"L4 radiculopathy diminishes the patellar reflex and produces weakness of knee extension from quadriceps involvement, with sensory loss over the medial leg and ankle","E":"L4 radiculopathy diminishes the biceps reflex and produces weakness of elbow flexion and forearm supination, reflecting musculocutaneous nerve involvement at the C5-C6 level"},"correct":"D","explanation":"L4 myotome: quadriceps (knee extension). L4 dermatome: medial leg and ankle. L4 reflex: patellar tendon reflex. Compressed at L3-L4 disc. Clinically: difficulty rising from a chair or climbing stairs. L5 = dorsiflexion, no reflex. S1 = plantar flexion, Achilles reflex. Mastering the lumbar myotome-reflex-dermatome triad is essential for localization.","boardPrep":"Step 1 HY lumbar root table: L4 = knee extension/patellar reflex/medial leg; L5 = dorsiflexion + great toe extension/no reflex/dorsum foot; S1 = plantar flexion/Achilles reflex/lateral foot. COMLEX: Can't rise from chair = L2/L3/L4; dragging toe = L5; buckling knee = L3/L4.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injury patterns and myotomal mapping are directly applied to lumbosacral radiculopathy localization"},{"id":"sdl19_q20","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the motor deficit and reflex finding in L5 nerve root compression?","choices":{"A":"L5 radiculopathy produces weakness of plantar flexion with loss of the Achilles reflex and sensory loss over the lateral foot, reflecting involvement of the S1 myotome and triceps surae","B":"L5 radiculopathy produces weakness of knee extension and hip flexion with loss of the patellar reflex, reflecting femoral nerve territory and the L4 myotome rather than dorsiflexion weakness","C":"L5 radiculopathy causes proximal lower extremity weakness of hip abduction and knee flexion with preserved distal function and no foot drop on neurological examination","D":"L5 radiculopathy produces isolated ankle eversion weakness with sensory loss limited to the lateral calf, representing common peroneal nerve palsy at the fibular head rather than root pathology","E":"L5 radiculopathy produces weakness of dorsiflexion and great toe extension causing foot drop, with sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot; no reliable deep tendon reflex is associated with the L5 root"},"correct":"E","explanation":"L5 is compressed at L4-L5. Myotome: tibialis anterior (dorsiflexion) and extensor hallucis longus (great toe extension) — foot drop. Dermatome: dorsum of foot and lateral calf. Reflex: none reliably attributed to L5, making motor and sensory testing critical for localization. Peroneal nerve palsy mimics L5 but is distinguished by normal hip abduction and no low back pain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: L5 = foot drop (dorsiflexion weak) + dorsal foot numbness + NO reflex. Key distinguisher: peroneal nerve palsy vs. L5 radiculopathy — check hip abduction (preserved in peroneal palsy). COMLEX: \"Dragging toe\" = L5 root; assess with single-leg heel walking.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injury patterns and myotomal mapping are directly applied to lumbosacral radiculopathy localization","whyNot":{"D":"Peroneal nerve palsy mimics L5"}},{"id":"sdl19_q21","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the approximate sensitivity of the straight leg raise test for L4-L5 and L5-S1 disc herniation?","choices":{"A":"The straight leg raise test has 95% sensitivity and 95% specificity for lumbosacral radiculopathy, making it the single most reliable clinical test for identifying disc herniation at any level","B":"The straight leg raise test has approximately 40 to 45% sensitivity for lumbar disc herniation, limiting its diagnostic utility and making MRI mandatory for all patients with radicular symptoms","C":"The straight leg raise test has approximately 68 to 80% sensitivity for L4-L5 and L5-S1 disc herniation, making it useful for ruling out lower lumbar disc pathology when negative","D":"The straight leg raise test has 80 to 90% specificity for L4-L5 and L5-S1 disc herniation, meaning a positive result at less than 60 degrees is nearly diagnostic without further imaging","E":"The straight leg raise test has equal sensitivity and specificity of approximately 50%, and the crossed straight leg raise is far more sensitive for identifying lumbar disc herniation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A positive straight leg raise test is 68% to 80% sensitive for an L4-L5 or L5-S1 herniated disk (Tintinalli Ch 279, the SDL 19 course reading), so a negative test makes lower lumbar disc herniation less likely. The reading gives no specificity figure for the ipsilateral test; Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine (Ch 42) quotes 91% sensitivity and 26% specificity for the supine test, which likewise makes it better for ruling out than for ruling in. A positive test is radicular pain radiating below the knee; back, gluteal, or hamstring pain alone is not a positive result (Tintinalli), and the osteopathic texts place a positive test typically between 30 and 70 degrees of hip flexion. The crossed straight leg raise is highly specific but insensitive for nerve root compression by a herniated disk. No course source gives 95% sensitivity and specificity, a 40 to 45% sensitivity, or equal 50% values, and the reading does not make MRI mandatory for every patient with radicular symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLR = 68-80% sensitive for L4-L5/L5-S1 herniation (Tintinalli); positive = radicular pain below the knee, typically between 30 and 70 degrees. Crossed SLR = highly specific but insensitive. COMLEX: Foundations Ch 42 quotes 91% sensitivity and 26% specificity for the supine SLR, so a negative test helps exclude lumbar radiculopathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injury patterns and myotomal mapping are directly applied to lumbosacral radiculopathy localization","whyNot":{"A":"No course source gives 95% sen","E":"No course source gives 95% sen"}},{"id":"sdl19_q22","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the hallmark clinical feature of neurogenic claudication from lumbar spinal stenosis?","choices":{"A":"Unilateral calf pain that occurs after a fixed walking distance and is relieved only by cessation of walking, indistinguishable from vascular claudication without Doppler imaging studies","B":"Bilateral leg pain and heaviness with walking that is relieved by sitting, resting, or leaning forward, reflecting increased spinal canal diameter with lumbar flexion","C":"Leg pain with standing but not walking, relieved by lumbar extension and worsened by forward flexion, opposite to the typical positional pattern of lumbar spinal stenosis","D":"Acute onset bilateral lower extremity weakness occurring at rest without any relationship to positional change or walking distance, indicating an acute cord compression emergency","E":"Burning bilateral foot pain at rest that worsens with leg elevation, distinguishing it from vascular claudication where elevation relieves ischemic symptoms in the lower limbs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Neurogenic claudication from lumbar stenosis: bilateral leg pain, heaviness, or numbness that builds with walking and is relieved by forward flexion (sitting, stooping, leaning on a cart). Mechanism: flexion widens the lumbar canal; extension narrows it. Distinguishing from vascular claudication: neurogenic is relieved by flexion even while standing; vascular is relieved simply by stopping walking regardless of posture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neurogenic claudication = bilateral leg pain with walking relieved by flexion. Spinal canal narrows in extension, widens in flexion. Shopping cart sign = leans forward for relief. COMLEX: Lumbar somatic dysfunction in extension restriction pattern is common; OMM targets the flexion-relief mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Hip fracture and vascular disease must be systematically excluded when evaluating bilateral leg pain; neurogenic claudication is distinguished by positional relief","whyNot":{"A":"Distinguishing from vascular c","C":"Mechanism: flexion widens the "}},{"id":"sdl19_q23","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A course slide on lumbosacral stenosis labels a defect at L5 as \"spondylolysis\" and a forward slip of L5 on the sacrum as \"spondylolisthesis.\" Which statement correctly defines spondylolysis?","choices":{"A":"A fracture of the pars interarticularis, which can let the vertebral body slip forward and narrow the canal","B":"Forward slippage of one vertebral body on the one below, which then fractures the pars as a later complication","C":"Hypertrophy of the facet joints from osteoarthritis, which narrows the intervertebral foramen of the exiting root","D":"Thickening of the ligamentum flavum, which buckles into the posterior canal when the spine is held in extension","E":"Posterior herniation of the nucleus pulposus through a tear in the annulus fibrosus into the spinal canal"},"correct":"A","explanation":"On the course's lumbosacral stenosis figure, spondylolysis is labeled as a fracture in the pars interarticularis, and spondylolisthesis is the forward slip of the vertebra, drawn as L5 on the sacrum; the figure shows these with a thickened ligamentum flavum, hypertrophied facets, and a herniated disc as causes of canal narrowing. The Tintinalli chapter adds that patients under 18 have a higher incidence of spondylolysis and spondylolisthesis. Forward slippage is spondylolisthesis, not spondylolysis, and the pars defect comes first. Facet hypertrophy, ligamentum flavum thickening, and disc herniation are separate contributors to stenosis shown in the same figure, not definitions of spondylolysis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spondylolysis = pars interarticularis defect (stress fracture, adolescent athletes with repetitive extension); spondylolisthesis = anterior slip, most often L5 on S1. COMLEX: Lumbar stenosis sits on a spectrum with spondylosis and radiculopathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 6 — lumbosacral spondylosis pathology","whyNot":{"C":"Facet hypertrophy, ligamentum ","E":"Facet hypertrophy, ligamentum "}},{"id":"sdl19_q24","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which imaging modality is preferred for diagnosis and surgical planning in lumbar spinal stenosis?","choices":{"A":"CT myelography is the required first-line imaging for all patients with lumbar spinal stenosis, providing superior anatomic resolution of bony stenosis and dynamic nerve root compression versus MRI","B":"Plain radiographs with dynamic flexion-extension views are the gold standard for evaluating lumbar spinal stenosis, detecting instability and degenerative spondylolisthesis that static imaging misses","C":"MRI is preferred because it directly visualizes central and foraminal canal dimensions, disc pathology, ligamentum flavum hypertrophy, and facet joint changes without ionizing radiation","D":"Nuclear bone scan is the recommended first-line study for lumbar spinal stenosis because it identifies active metabolic bone turnover at stenotic levels, guiding targeted injection therapy","E":"Electromyography and nerve conduction studies are the preferred initial diagnostic modality, quantifying the degree of nerve root compromise before committing to cross-sectional imaging"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MRI is the gold standard for lumbar spinal stenosis: it shows the cross-sectional canal area, ligamentum flavum buckling, facet hypertrophy, and disc herniation. CT myelography is reserved for patients who cannot undergo MRI. Plain X-rays may show spondylolisthesis and disc height loss but cannot directly assess canal compromise. EMG is useful to confirm radiculopathy but does not image stenosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI = diagnostic standard for lumbar stenosis. CT myelography if MRI contraindicated. Look for \"trefoil\" canal shape on axial MRI — classic for severe stenosis. COMLEX: Neurogenic claudication with confirmed stenosis — conservative management first including OMM; surgery for refractory cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Hip fracture and vascular disease must be systematically excluded when evaluating bilateral leg pain; neurogenic claudication is distinguished by positional relief"},{"id":"sdl19_q25","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman presents with 3 years of worsening bilateral knee pain, morning stiffness lasting less than 30 minutes, and bony DIP joint enlargement. X-ray shows asymmetric joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and marginal osteophytes bilaterally. ESR and CRP are normal. Which of the following best explains the primary underlying mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Progressive proteoglycan depletion from articular cartilage reduces compressive stiffness, causing fibrillation and chondrocyte apoptosis with reactive subchondral sclerosis and marginal osteophytes","B":"Autoimmune CD4+ T-cell infiltration of the synovium with pannus formation progressively erodes articular cartilage and subchondral bone, producing symmetric joint destruction and systemic inflammation","C":"Calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition within articular cartilage triggers recurrent acute inflammatory episodes, producing joint space narrowing and osteophyte formation indistinguishable from primary OA","D":"Systemic elevation of TNF-alpha drives neutrophil influx into the joint space, producing enzymatic cartilage degradation and the reactive periarticular bone changes observed on plain radiography","E":"Uric acid crystal deposition in the periarticular tissues and synovium triggers a local inflammatory cascade, producing the chronic joint destruction and DIP joint enlargement in this patient"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This presentation — chronic knee pain, short morning stiffness, DIP Heberden's nodes, normal inflammatory markers, and asymmetric joint space narrowing with osteophytes — is classic OA. Mechanism: chondrocyte dysfunction → proteoglycan loss → decreased cartilage resilience → mechanical fibrillation → subchondral reactive changes. Pannus (RA), CPPD, TNF-alpha (systemic inflammatory arthritis), and uric acid (gout) each describe different pathophysiology with different clinical profiles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA = proteoglycan loss → cartilage fibrillation → joint space narrowing + osteophytes + subchondral sclerosis, WITHOUT periarticular osteopenia (distinguish from RA). DIP Heberden's nodes = OA. COMLEX: Short morning stiffness <30 min = OA; inflammatory arthritis lasts > 1 hour per FA 2025 (Bootcamp gives ~>30 min for RA).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — Exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact cartilage integrity and joint health through mechanisms directly relevant to OA progression","whyNot":{"B":"Pannus (RA), CPPD, TNF-alpha (","C":"Pannus (RA), CPPD, TNF-alpha (","D":"Pannus (RA), CPPD, TNF-alpha (","E":"Pannus (RA), CPPD, TNF-alpha ("}},{"id":"sdl19_q26","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old man presents with progressive right hip pain and difficulty with prolonged walking. Plain radiograph of the right hip shows medial joint space narrowing, subchondral cyst formation at the femoral head, and osteophytes at the acetabular rim. BMI is 31. Which combination of radiographic findings is most characteristic of osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Uniform joint space narrowing with periarticular osteopenia, marginal erosions at the joint edges, and bilateral symmetric involvement is the hallmark pattern that distinguishes OA from this patient's diagnosis","B":"Periarticular osteopenia with central marginal erosions and bilateral symmetric joint space narrowing define the underlying radiographic pattern causing this patient's progressive hip pain","C":"Chondrocalcinosis with calcification of the fibrocartilaginous labrum and periarticular soft tissues is the defining radiographic feature that confirms the diagnosis in this patient","D":"Rapid progressive joint space loss over weeks to months with normal bone density and no osteophyte formation is the radiographic pattern most consistent with this patient's diagnosis","E":"Non-uniform joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis and cyst formation, and marginal osteophyte formation without periarticular osteopenia is the classic radiographic tetrad of osteoarthritis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"OA radiographic findings: (1) non-uniform (asymmetric, weight-bearing surface) joint space narrowing; (2) subchondral sclerosis; (3) subchondral cyst formation (synovial fluid forced into bone under pressure); (4) marginal osteophytes; (5) NO periarticular osteopenia. Contrast: RA = symmetric, periarticular osteopenia, marginal erosions, no osteophytes early. CPPD = chondrocalcinosis. Rapid progression = septic arthritis or crystal arthropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA X-ray mnemonic LOSS — Loss of joint space (non-uniform), Osteophytes, Subchondral sclerosis, Subchondral cysts. No osteopenia. COMLEX: Hip OA commonly produces somatic dysfunction with restricted internal rotation and extension at the hip — OMM addresses this component.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — Aging and physical conditioning alter subchondral bone density and cartilage hydration, directly influencing OA radiographic progression","whyNot":{"A":"Contrast: RA = symmetric, peri","B":"Contrast: RA = symmetric, peri","C":"CPPD = chondrocalcinosis.","D":"Rapid progression = septic art"}},{"id":"sdl19_q27","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with confirmed bilateral knee osteoarthritis has failed 3 months of exercise therapy, a 10-pound weight loss, and scheduled acetaminophen. She rates her daily pain 7/10 with significant functional limitation. She has stage 3b CKD and a prior upper GI bleed, so oral NSAIDs are contraindicated, and 6 weeks of topical diclofenac gave no benefit. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Initiating long-term opioid therapy with extended-release oxycodone is the recommended next step for moderate OA pain that has not responded adequately to acetaminophen and weight management","B":"Referring for total knee arthroplasty is appropriate at this stage, as failure of one analgesic agent constitutes sufficient evidence that conservative management has been adequately exhausted","C":"Initiating hydroxychloroquine and methotrexate is the recommended escalation step for symptomatic knee OA after failure of first-line analgesic and nonpharmacologic therapy over 3 months","D":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection, which provides short-term pain relief and functional improvement when oral NSAIDs are contraindicated and topical NSAIDs have failed","E":"Prescribing scheduled maximum-dose oral naproxen is the most appropriate next step before considering any procedural intervention in this patient with bilateral knee OA"},"correct":"D","explanation":"With oral NSAIDs contraindicated (stage 3b CKD, prior GI bleed) and topical NSAIDs already failed, intra-articular corticosteroid injection is the appropriate next step, so scheduled oral naproxen is not an option. Intra-articular corticosteroids provide meaningful short-term (4-6 week) benefit and are a reasonable step before proceeding to surgery. Opioids are not recommended for OA. DMARDs have no role in OA. Arthroplasty is indicated after failure of prolonged conservative management, not after a 3-month trial.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA escalation — exercise/weight loss → NSAIDs/topical → intra-articular injection (corticosteroid or hyaluronic acid) → joint replacement. Opioids are not guideline-recommended for OA. COMLEX: OMM improves joint mechanics; document conservative trial before procedural escalation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — Exercise conditioning reduces joint loading forces and slows OA progression; weight loss has the strongest evidence for knee OA outcomes","whyNot":{"A":"Opioids are not recommended fo","B":"Arthroplasty is indicated afte","C":"DMARDs have no role in OA."}},{"id":"sdl19_q28","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 65-year-old man presents with 1 year of bilateral neck pain, morning cervical stiffness, and intermittent periscapular aching. MRI shows disc space narrowing at C5-C6 and C6-C7, vertebral endplate osteophytes, and facet joint hypertrophy. He has full upper extremity strength and intact reflexes. Which of the following best explains the underlying pathophysiology?","choices":{"A":"Inflammatory cytokine production by the synovial lining of the cervical facet joints drives progressive cartilage erosion and bone destruction, producing disc space collapse and endplate irregularity","B":"Hematogenous seeding of the cervical disc space by bacteria causes disc space collapse and adjacent vertebral endplate destruction, producing the MRI findings and bilateral neck pain","C":"Autoimmune attack targeting type II collagen within the nucleus pulposus triggers progressive disc destruction and bilateral facet involvement in this patient with cervical spondylosis","D":"Microcrystalline calcium pyrophosphate deposition within the posterior longitudinal ligament and annulus fibrosus drives disc space narrowing and the osteophyte formation seen on MRI","E":"Age-related nucleus pulposus dehydration lowers disc height and loads the endplates and facets, driving osteophytes, facet arthropathy, and PLL hypertrophy that narrow the canal and foramina"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cervical spondylosis pathophysiology: disc nucleus pulposus desiccates with age → reduced disc height → increased annular fissuring → increased load on facet joints → facet arthropathy → osteophyte formation at endplates and facet margins → PLL and ligamentum flavum thickening → canal and foraminal narrowing. This is a mechanical degenerative process, not inflammatory (as in RA or infection).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical spondylosis = disc degeneration + facet arthropathy + osteophyte formation. Disc height loss is seen on X-ray; cord/root compression seen on MRI. COMLEX: Restriction of cervical sidebending and rotation = somatic dysfunction — address with OMM as adjunct to spondylosis management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — RANKL/OPG balance and bone remodeling hormones govern osteophyte formation at vertebral endplates in cervical spondylosis","whyNot":{"A":"This is a mechanical degenerat","B":"This is a mechanical degenerat","C":"This is a mechanical degenerat"}},{"id":"sdl19_q29","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with cervical spondylosis has persistent cervical and periscapular pain despite 3 months of NSAIDs. She has increased bilateral upper trapezius tension and restricted C4-C5 sidebending on osteopathic examination, consistent with somatic dysfunction. Which of the following interventions most directly addresses the somatic dysfunction component of her presentation?","choices":{"A":"Prescribing a COX-2 selective NSAID from a different chemical class is the most direct intervention for her somatic dysfunction, as segmental hypertonicity is driven primarily by prostaglandin-mediated inflammation","B":"Referring for cervical epidural steroid injection is the most direct next step for the restricted C4-C5 sidebending and upper trapezius tension documented on osteopathic examination","C":"Ordering repeat cervical MRI is the most direct intervention for the documented somatic dysfunction, as imaging must confirm structural pathology before any manual medicine intervention","D":"Prescribing a benzodiazepine muscle relaxant for 4 weeks is the most direct treatment for somatic dysfunction with paraspinal hypertonicity in cervical spondylosis","E":"Osteopathic manipulative medicine targeting the cervical somatic dysfunction can restore segmental motion, reduce paraspinal hypertonicity, and decrease pain without relying on additional pharmacotherapy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Somatic dysfunction — defined as impaired or altered function of related components of the somatic system — is a distinct clinical entity from spondylosis. OMM (muscle energy, soft tissue, counterstrain, high-velocity low-amplitude with appropriate screening) directly addresses restricted joint motion and paraspinal muscle hypertonicity. NSAIDs address inflammation but not the mechanical dysfunction. Epidural injection targets nerve root, not somatic dysfunction. MRI does not treat dysfunction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical spondylosis + restricted motion = somatic dysfunction as comorbid finding. COMLEX: OMM is the primary intervention for somatic dysfunction in cervical spondylosis; document restricted sidebending, rotation, or flexion-extension as the dysfunction findings.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — Bone remodeling and osteophyte formation in spondylosis create structural constraints on segmental motion that contribute to somatic dysfunction","whyNot":{"A":"NSAIDs address inflammation bu","B":"Epidural injection targets ner","C":"MRI does not treat dysfunction"}},{"id":"sdl19_q30","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has cervical spondylosis with chronic neck and periscapular pain and stiffness, no myelopathic signs, and only partial relief from an NSAID. Her physician is following the course's adaptation of OARSI recommendations for the cervical spine. Which additional pharmacologic classes appear in that course plan?","choices":{"A":"Scheduled long-acting opioids and oral bisphosphonates, used together to treat pain and slow osteophyte growth","B":"Muscle relaxants and antidepressants, listed alongside NSAIDs in the pharmacologic arm of the management plan","C":"Colchicine and allopurinol, since crystal deposition in the facet joints drives most cervical spondylosis pain","D":"Methotrexate and hydroxychloroquine, since the degenerative disc changes reflect an autoimmune synovitis","E":"Oral antibiotics and antifungals, since low-grade disc space infection is the usual cause of chronic neck pain"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course's management slide for cervical spondylosis adapts OARSI recommendations to the cervical spine: brace or collar, avoidance of high-risk activities, pharmacologic treatment (NSAIDs, muscle relaxants, antidepressants), physical therapy, osteopathic manual medicine, acupuncture, injections, and surgical decompression. Muscle relaxants and antidepressants are the listed drug classes beyond NSAIDs. Long-acting opioids and bisphosphonates are not part of the plan, and bisphosphonates do not slow osteophytes. Colchicine and allopurinol treat gout, which is not the mechanism of spondylosis. DMARDs treat inflammatory arthritis, not degenerative disease. Infection is not the cause of cervical spondylosis, which is age-related wear of discs, facets, and ligaments.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical spondylosis = disc degeneration, facet arthropathy, osteophytes, PLL/ligamentum flavum hypertrophy; 95% by age 65, poor symptom correlation. COMLEX: OMM addresses associated somatic dysfunction; surgery is reserved for progressive neurologic deficit.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 4 — NSAID adverse effects that limit long-term use","whyNot":{"D":"DMARDs treat inflammatory arth","E":"Infection is not the cause of "}},{"id":"sdl19_q31","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has 3 weeks of neck pain radiating down the medial forearm to the ring and little fingers, with numbness of the medial forearm, medial hand, and fourth and fifth digits. He has weakness of finger abduction and of flexion at the thumb tip. Biceps, brachioradialis, and triceps reflexes are normal. Which nerve root is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"C8, which gives medial forearm and fourth and fifth digit numbness, intrinsic and distal thumb flexion weakness, and no reflex change","B":"C6, which gives lateral forearm, thumb, and index finger numbness, elbow flexion and supination weakness, and a reduced brachioradialis reflex","C":"C7, which gives index and middle finger numbness, elbow and wrist extension weakness, and a reduced triceps reflex on the affected side","D":"C5, which gives lateral arm numbness over the deltoid, shoulder abduction and external rotation weakness, and a reduced biceps reflex","E":"The ulnar nerve at the elbow, which gives fourth and fifth digit numbness with intrinsic weakness, including weakness of distal thumb flexion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course's cervical nerve root table lists C8 as pain in the neck, shoulder, medial forearm, fourth and fifth digits, and medial hand; numbness of the medial forearm, medial hand, and fourth and fifth digits; weakness of finger extension, ulnar wrist extension, distal finger flexion, finger abduction and adduction, and distal thumb flexion; and no reflex affected. Normal reflexes fit C8. C6 produces lateral forearm, thumb, and index numbness with a reduced brachioradialis reflex. C7 reduces the triceps reflex. C5 affects shoulder abduction and the biceps reflex. An ulnar nerve lesion does not explain medial forearm numbness or weakness of distal thumb flexion, which depends on flexor pollicis longus outside the ulnar nerve's territory.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical roots: C5 biceps/deltoid; C6 brachioradialis, thumb/index; C7 triceps, middle finger; C8 no reflex, ring/little finger and medial forearm, intrinsics; T1 thumb abduction, medial arm. COMLEX: Spurling test is specific (~90%) but insensitive for radiculopathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow as a mimic of C8 radiculopathy"},{"id":"sdl19_q32","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old carpenter with a right C6 radiculopathy is being managed as an outpatient with activity modification and oral analgesics. Which new finding at a follow-up visit would be the clearest indication for hospital admission?","choices":{"A":"A positive right Spurling sign that still reproduces his lateral forearm pain, while his arm strength, reflexes, and gait have not changed","B":"Persistent mild numbness of the right thumb and index finger two weeks after onset, with arm strength and reflexes the same as at the first visit","C":"Neck and trapezius pain that worsens with overhead work and eases with rest, with focal muscle tenderness and a normal neurological examination","D":"Right arm weakness that has progressed over successive visits, most notably in the C7 distribution, with worsening elbow extension strength","E":"An MRI showing a C5-C6 disc-osteophyte complex that contacts the right C6 root and matches his symptoms, while his examination stays stable"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Tintinalli (Ch 279, the SDL 19 course reading, p7) lists the indications for hospital admission in cervical radiculopathy: progressive upper extremity weakness, especially in the C7 distribution; acute or progressive symptoms or signs of myelopathy; and, in a small subset of patients, intractable radicular pain unresponsive to treatment. The course slides list conservative treatment first (activity modification, collar, analgesics, oral glucocorticoids, PT, OMM, traction) and note that severe cases show upper extremity weakness. A positive Spurling sign (90% specific, 45% sensitive, Tintinalli Table 279-2) supports the diagnosis but is not a reason to admit. Sensory loss in cervical radiculopathy is usually absent or mild (course slide), so stable mild numbness does not change disposition. Activity-related neck and trapezius pain with focal tenderness and a normal neurological examination favors uncomplicated musculoskeletal neck pain (Table 279-2). Concordant imaging becomes relevant when symptoms fail conservative treatment or recur, when surgery may be recommended, but with a stable examination it is not an admission criterion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Admit cervical radiculopathy for progressive arm weakness (especially C7), acute or progressive myelopathy, or intractable radicular pain (Tintinalli). COMLEX: Most cases are managed as outpatients with activity modification, analgesics, PT, and OMM; surgery is considered when symptoms fail conservative care or recur and imaging is concordant.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — myelopathy signs (hyperreflexia, gait change) also warrant admission or surgical referral","whyNot":{"E":"Concordant imaging becomes rel"}},{"id":"sdl19_q33","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman presents with right neck and posterior arm pain radiating to the dorsal forearm and middle finger. She notes weakness with pushing movements and a reduced triceps deep tendon reflex on the right. Which of the following best localizes this radiculopathy?","choices":{"A":"Biceps reflex loss with weakness of elbow flexion and sensory loss in the lateral forearm and thumb localizes the lesion to C5-C6 nerve root involvement rather than the presentation described","B":"Triceps reflex loss with weakness of elbow extension and sensory loss in the dorsal forearm and middle finger localizes the lesion to the C7 nerve root, most commonly compressed at C6-C7","C":"Brachioradialis reflex loss with weakness of wrist extension and sensory loss in the lateral forearm localizes the lesion to the C6 nerve root rather than the posterior arm and middle finger distribution","D":"Patellar reflex loss with weakness of knee extension and sensory loss over the medial leg localizes the lesion to the L4 nerve root at the lumbar level, not the cervical spine","E":"Achilles reflex loss with weakness of plantar flexion and sensory loss over the lateral foot localizes the lesion to the S1 nerve root at the lumbosacral level, not the cervical spine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"C7 root exits at C6-C7. Sensory: dorsal forearm and middle finger. Motor: triceps (elbow extension) and wrist flexors. Reflex: triceps deep tendon reflex. C7 is the most commonly affected cervical root. Weakness \"with pushing\" corresponds to triceps/elbow extension. C6 = lateral forearm/thumb/BR reflex. C5 = deltoid/biceps reflex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: C7 = middle finger numbness + triceps weakness + triceps reflex loss. Most common cervical root affected. Associated with C6-C7 disc herniation. COMLEX: C7 somatic dysfunction with tenderness at C6-C7 interspinous space — palpatory diagnosis supports level localization.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Nerve root localization by dermatomal and myotomal mapping is the same principle applied in upper extremity entrapment neuropathies at the elbow and wrist","whyNot":{"A":"C6 = lateral forearm/thumb/BR ","C":"C6 = lateral forearm/thumb/BR "}},{"id":"sdl19_q34","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman presents with 4 months of progressive bilateral hand weakness and difficulty with fine motor tasks, along with gait unsteadiness. Neurological exam shows hyperreflexia at biceps and triceps bilaterally and bilateral positive Hoffman's signs. MRI reveals a central disc herniation at C4-C5 with T2 cord signal change. Which of the following best explains her deficits?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral central canal compression at C4-C5 damages the corticospinal tracts, producing hyperreflexia and spastic gait, while the T2 cord signal reflects compressive myelomalacia or ischemia","B":"Bilateral C5 foraminal compression produces simultaneous bilateral lower motor neuron deficits including hyperreflexia, spastic gait, and hand weakness through axonal degeneration at both foramina","C":"Vertebral artery compression at C4-C5 produces posterior circulation ischemia, causing bilateral upper extremity weakness, hyperreflexia, and gait unsteadiness through cerebellar and brainstem dysfunction","D":"Inflammatory demyelination of the cervical cord produces bilateral upper motor neuron signs; however, the absence of optic nerve involvement makes this the mechanism underlying this patient's deficits","E":"Epidural hematoma compressing the cervical cord at C4-C5 produces the bilateral upper motor neuron deficits; the gradual 4-month progression and absence of trauma are consistent with this mechanism"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Central disc herniation compresses the cervical cord → corticospinal tract damage → UMN signs: hyperreflexia, spasticity, Hoffman's, gait ataxia, hand clumsiness. T2 signal change in the cord = myelomalacia (irreversible ischemic/compressive change) — a poor prognostic indicator requiring urgent surgical decompression. Foraminal (LMN) compression causes hyporeflexia, not hyperreflexia. Vertebral artery compression would cause brainstem/posterior fossa symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T2 cord signal change on MRI = myelomalacia = surgical emergency. Cervical myelopathy = bilateral UMN signs + hand dysfunction + gait ataxia (central cord syndrome is a distinct, usually traumatic, hyperextension injury with arms weaker than legs). Hoffman's + hyperreflexia = UMN. COMLEX: HVLA absolutely contraindicated; refer for neurosurgical decompression urgently.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — The structural features of the cervical spinal canal — facet joints, PLL, ligamentum flavum — directly determine the space available for the cord when central stenosis develops","whyNot":{"B":"Foraminal (LMN) compression ca"}},{"id":"sdl19_q35","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with cervical spinal stenosis has MRI findings described in two reports. In one, degenerative change narrows the central canal at C5-C6; in the other, osteophytes narrow the right C6-C7 intervertebral foramen. According to the course, which structure is compressed in each type of stenosis?","choices":{"A":"Central stenosis compresses a single nerve root, and foraminal stenosis compresses the spinal cord at that level","B":"Both types compress the vertebral artery, which explains why cervical stenosis presents with dizziness","C":"Central stenosis compresses the spinal cord, and foraminal stenosis compresses the exiting cervical nerve root","D":"Both types compress the spinal cord, so foraminal narrowing produces the same signs as central narrowing","E":"Central stenosis compresses the dorsal root ganglion, and foraminal stenosis compresses the dura by itself"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course defines cervical spinal stenosis as degeneration that leads to central or foraminal stenosis: central stenosis compresses the spinal cord, and foraminal stenosis compresses the nerve root. Stenosis can be congenital or acquired, prevalence is 14 to 24.2% and rises after age 65, and it presents and is treated like cervical spondylosis. Central cord compression produces myelopathic (upper motor neuron) findings, while foraminal compression produces radiculopathy in the distribution of the exiting root. Reversing the two structures is incorrect. The vertebral artery is not the structure the course describes as compressed. Foraminal narrowing affects the root, not the cord. The dorsal root ganglion lies in the foramen, not the central canal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical central stenosis → myelopathy (hyperreflexia, Hoffmann, spastic gait); foraminal stenosis → radiculopathy (dermatomal pain, root weakness, reduced reflex). COMLEX: MRI is preferred when neurologic signs are present; surgery when myelopathy progresses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 3 — cervical radiculopathy localization by nerve root","whyNot":{"A":"Reversing the two structures i","D":"Foraminal narrowing affects th"}},{"id":"sdl19_q36","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with cervical spinal stenosis has been managed with NSAIDs and physical therapy for 8 weeks. He now has progressive bilateral hand weakness and new urinary urgency. Repeat MRI shows worsening T2 cord signal at C5-C6. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Continue conservative management for an additional 12 weeks with a rigid cervical orthosis, as natural history data show spontaneous resolution of cord signal change in most patients","B":"Prescribe high-dose oral corticosteroid taper, as cord edema is the primary driver of progressive neurological deterioration and can be reversed with systemic steroid therapy","C":"Cervical epidural steroid injection is the most appropriate next step, as it provides cord decompression equivalent to surgical outcomes in patients with T2 signal change and sphincter symptoms","D":"Progressive myelopathy with worsening cord signal and new sphincter dysfunction despite conservative care warrants urgent surgical decompression (laminoplasty or laminectomy with fusion)","E":"Repeat MRI in 3 months to monitor progression before considering surgery, as cervical myelopathy uniformly plateaus without intervention and surgical outcomes worsen with delay"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Indications for surgical decompression in cervical myelopathy: progressive neurological deficits despite adequate conservative management, or moderate-to-severe myelopathy at presentation. Worsening T2 cord signal = myelomalacia (irreversible change if persistent) + new sphincter involvement = urgent surgical referral. Laminoplasty (cord-expansive) or laminectomy with fusion are standard approaches. Epidural injections address radiculopathy, not cord compression. Corticosteroids are not a standard treatment for compressive myelopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myelopathy + progressive deficits = surgical emergency. T2 cord signal = myelomalacia = operate before permanent injury. Surgical options: anterior cervical discectomy and fusion (ACDF), laminoplasty, or laminectomy with fusion. COMLEX: OMM is supportive only — does not substitute for surgery in myelopathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — Understanding the structural anatomy of the cervical spinal canal is essential to understanding why canal compromise from posterior ligamentum flavum and anterior osteophytes produces bilateral cord dysfunction","whyNot":{"B":"Corticosteroids are not a stan","C":"Epidural injections address ra"}},{"id":"sdl19_q37","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old teacher presents with 3 weeks of acute, non-radiating central low back pain rated 6/10, without leg pain, neurological deficits, fever, weight loss, or bowel or bladder symptoms. Examination shows diffuse paraspinal tenderness with full lower extremity strength and reflexes. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Emergent lumbar MRI is indicated because 3 weeks of unresolved low back pain represents failure of natural history and requires imaging to exclude structural pathology before initiating treatment","B":"Lumbar radiographs should be obtained immediately in this patient because plain films are a low-risk screening tool that excludes compression fracture, spondylolysis, and serious structural disease","C":"EMG and nerve conduction studies should be performed to quantify subclinical nerve root involvement before physical therapy is initiated, as radiculopathy may precede overt clinical symptoms","D":"CT of the lumbar spine is indicated in this patient to evaluate the posterior elements and intervertebral discs at higher resolution than radiography, guiding early management decisions","E":"Without red flag symptoms, routine imaging is not indicated in the first 4 to 6 weeks of acute mechanical low back pain; NSAIDs, relative rest, heat, and early physical therapy come first"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acute mechanical LBP without red flags does not require imaging in the first 4-6 weeks. Red flags that DO warrant imaging: age >50, unexplained weight loss, fever, history of cancer, IV drug use, prolonged steroid use, major trauma, neurological deficits, bowel/bladder dysfunction. This patient has none. Early imaging does not improve outcomes and may identify incidental findings that lead to unnecessary intervention. EMG and nerve conduction studies are listed for suspected lumbosacral radiculopathy (radiating pain, numbness, weakness), none of which this patient has.","boardPrep":"Step 1: No imaging for acute LBP without red flags in first 4-6 weeks. This is a high-yield clinical decision point on USMLE. Red flags = cancer, infection, CES, fracture. COMLEX: OMM for lumbar somatic dysfunction is appropriate and recommended; do not wait for imaging to begin manual therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — Low back pain without red flags is a primary indication for integrative and osteopathic medicine referral before escalating to pharmacologic or procedural interventions","whyNot":{"A":"Early imaging does not improve","B":"Early imaging does not improve","D":"Early imaging does not improve"}},{"id":"sdl19_q38","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old man with 2 days of severe low back pain now reports new perianal numbness and difficulty initiating urination. He has bilateral leg pain below the knee. Examination reveals decreased perianal sensation, 3/5 bilateral hip flexor strength, and reduced anal sphincter tone. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Administer IV methylprednisolone and arrange outpatient lumbar MRI within 48 hours, as corticosteroids can partially reverse cauda equina syndrome and avoid the risks of urgent surgery","B":"Perform lumbar puncture for CSF analysis to exclude infectious meningitis before obtaining lumbar MRI, as infectious etiologies must be ruled out before attributing symptoms to disc herniation","C":"This presentation is consistent with cauda equina syndrome and requires emergent lumbar MRI with immediate neurosurgical consultation for urgent decompression","D":"Order EMG of the bilateral lower extremities as the initial step to confirm nerve root involvement before committing to cross-sectional imaging or surgical consultation","E":"Prescribe oral NSAIDs and a muscle relaxant with close outpatient follow-up in one week, as bilateral radicular symptoms with urinary complaints often resolve with conservative management"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cauda equina syndrome (CES) = saddle anesthesia + bladder/bowel dysfunction + bilateral leg weakness. This is a surgical emergency. Next step: emergent MRI lumbar spine, then urgent neurosurgical decompression (within 24-48 hours to preserve bladder function). Delay = permanent incontinence. IV steroids are not standard treatment for CES. LP, EMG, and conservative management all inappropriately delay necessary surgery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CES = emergent MRI → urgent surgery. Saddle anesthesia is pathognomonic. Bladder/bowel dysfunction: the course slide lists usually incontinence; retention also occurs. COMLEX: CES is an absolute OMM contraindication — surgical emergency first.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — Integrative and conservative care is appropriate for mechanical LBP; recognizing when red flags mandate emergent workup is essential to safe practice","whyNot":{"A":"IV steroids are not standard t","B":"LP, EMG, and conservative mana","D":"LP, EMG, and conservative mana","E":"LP, EMG, and conservative mana"}},{"id":"sdl19_q39","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old construction worker on workers' compensation has had low back pain for 7 months. MRI shows mild L4-L5 disc narrowing without herniation or significant stenosis. He has fear-avoidance beliefs, has stopped all physical activity, scores high on the Pain Catastrophizing Scale, and reports job dissatisfaction. Which of the following is the strongest independent predictor of chronic disability in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Psychosocial yellow flags (fear-avoidance, catastrophizing, workers' compensation, low job satisfaction, depression) are the strongest predictors of chronic disability, outweighing imaging findings","B":"The presence of L4-L5 disc space narrowing on MRI is the strongest predictor of chronic disability in this patient, as structural degeneration at this level directly correlates with long-term functional impairment","C":"Duration of pain beyond 6 months alone is the strongest predictor of poor recovery, as chronification is driven purely by the peripheral pain generator and is unrelated to psychological factors","D":"Workers' compensation status alone, independent of all other psychosocial factors, completely accounts for the disability reported and is the strongest single predictor of poor recovery in LBP","E":"BMI and physical deconditioning are the strongest predictors of chronic disability in this patient, as adiposity drives systemic inflammation that amplifies the structural disc degeneration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Psychosocial yellow flags — fear-avoidance, catastrophizing, depression, anxiety, low job satisfaction, workers' comp — are stronger predictors of chronification and disability than structural imaging findings. The biopsychosocial model is the evidence-based framework for chronic LBP. MRI findings do not predict disability. Addressing yellow flags through cognitive behavioral therapy, graded exercise, and psychosocial support is as important as physical treatments.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Psychosocial factors (yellow flags) predict chronic LBP disability more than imaging. Biopsychosocial model is the framework for chronic LBP management. COMLEX: OMM combined with patient education and addressing yellow flags reflects the osteopathic whole-person approach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — LBP is the primary indication for integrative medicine; psychosocial yellow flags are a recognized indication for mind-body and behavioral approaches alongside manual therapy","whyNot":{"B":"MRI findings do not predict di"}},{"id":"sdl19_q40","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old retired teacher presents with 2 years of low back pain and stiffness, worst upon waking and improving after 1 hour of morning activity. She reports mild bilateral buttock pain without leg radiation. X-ray shows disc space narrowing at L4-L5 and L5-S1 with endplate sclerosis and marginal osteophytes. ESR and CRP are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Ankylosing spondylitis presents with morning low back stiffness that improves with activity, along with sacroiliitis on imaging, elevated inflammatory markers, and HLA-B27","B":"Lumbar disc herniation presents with pain radiating below the knee in a dermatomal pattern that worsens with sitting, coughing, and Valsalva maneuvers","C":"Vertebral compression fracture presents with acute-onset localized back pain in an osteoporotic patient and anterior vertebral height loss on radiograph","D":"Lumbar spinal stenosis presents with bilateral leg pain on walking that is relieved by forward flexion, sitting down, or leaning on a shopping cart","E":"Lumbosacral spondylosis presents with morning stiffness that eases with activity, nondermatomal buttock pain, and disc narrowing, osteophytes, and endplate sclerosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"LS spondylosis (lumbar DDD + OA): morning pain and stiffness that improve through the day (course slide SDL 19 p24), localized or referred buttock/posterior thigh pain (not dermatomal), normal inflammatory markers, X-ray with disc space narrowing + osteophytes + endplate sclerosis. Ankylosing spondylitis: similar morning pattern BUT sacroiliitis + elevated CRP/ESR + HLA-B27. Herniation = dermatomal radiation. Stenosis = neurogenic claudication. Compression fracture = acute localized pain with vertebral height loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LS spondylosis vs. ankylosing spondylitis — key differentiators: age, sacroiliitis, HLA-B27, elevated ESR/CRP. Both improve with activity. COMLEX: Lumbar spondylosis with restricted motion = somatic dysfunction at L4-L5 or L5-S1; document restriction pattern for OMM treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Osteocyte and chondrocyte dysfunction at vertebral endplates parallels the bone histology of degenerative disc disease in lumbosacral spondylosis","whyNot":{"B":"Herniation = dermatomal radiat","D":"Stenosis = neurogenic claudica"}},{"id":"sdl19_q41","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with chronic lumbosacral spondylosis undergoes lumbar MRI. The radiologist reports Modic type I changes at the L3-L4 vertebral endplates. Which of the following MRI signal characteristics corresponds to Modic type I changes?","choices":{"A":"Modic type I changes are hyperintense on T1 and hypointense on T2 sequences, representing fatty marrow replacement at the vertebral endplate indicating chronic, stable degeneration without active inflammation","B":"Modic type I changes are hypointense on both T1 and T2 sequences, representing vertebral endplate sclerosis indicating end-stage degeneration with dense bone mineralization at the disc-vertebra interface","C":"Modic type I changes represent disc material herniating through the endplate, producing a T2-hyperintense defect with cortical endplate disruption on sagittal MRI sequences at L3-L4","D":"Modic type I changes are hypointense on T1 and hyperintense on T2 sequences, representing bone marrow edema and hypervascularity at the vertebral endplate from an active inflammatory or degenerative process","E":"Modic type I changes represent the vacuum phenomenon of advanced disc degeneration, appearing as signal void on both T1 and T2 sequences at the intervertebral disc-endplate junction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Modic endplate changes: Type I = active inflammation/edema = T1 hypo, T2 hyper. Type II = fatty replacement (most common, stable) = T1 hyper, T2 iso/slightly hyper. Type III = sclerosis = T1 hypo, T2 hypo. Type I correlates with active pain. The mnemonic: Type I = Inflammatory (T1 dark, T2 bright). Type II = Fat (T1 bright). Type III = sclerosis (both dark).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Modic type I = active, painful — T1 low, T2 high. Type II = fatty, stable — T1 high. Type III = sclerotic — both low. COMLEX: Modic type I at a level with somatic dysfunction supports targeting that level with OMM, but inflammation may require medical co-management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Osteoblast and osteocyte activity at vertebral endplates generates the Modic change pattern; Type I reflects active bone marrow edema from disrupted endplate homeostasis","whyNot":{"B":"Type III = sclerosis = T1 hypo"}},{"id":"sdl19_q42","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with lumbosacral spondylosis has taken scheduled naproxen for 4 months without adequate pain relief. She has hypertension and mild CKD limiting continued NSAID use. Physical therapy provided partial benefit. She has no neurological deficits. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Lumbar fusion surgery is the most appropriate next step because failure of two conservative modalities meets the threshold for surgical intervention in lumbosacral spondylosis without neurological deficits","B":"Epidural steroid injection is an appropriate next-step procedural intervention for refractory lumbosacral spondylosis pain when NSAIDs are limited by comorbidities and physical therapy has provided only partial benefit","C":"Long-term opioid therapy with extended-release morphine is the most appropriate next step for this patient, as her renal and cardiovascular comorbidities make all further NSAID use contraindicated","D":"Repeating lumbar MRI and referring to rheumatology is the most appropriate next step, as age, chronic pain, and NSAID failure suggest an inflammatory arthropathy requiring DMARD therapy","E":"Prescribing long-term low-dose oral prednisone is the most appropriate next step for lumbosacral spondylosis refractory to NSAIDs, as chronic corticosteroids reduce the underlying degenerative process"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When NSAIDs are contraindicated and PT provides partial benefit, epidural steroid injections (ESI) offer a procedural option for spondylosis with radicular or axial pain. ESI is not curative but can provide months of relief and facilitate PT engagement. Lumbar fusion requires more exhaustive conservative failure AND structural instability or deformity. Opioids have minimal evidence for chronic LBP and significant risks. Long-term corticosteroids worsen osteoporosis and are not indicated for degenerative disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epidural steroid injection = next step when conservative measures fail in LBP with radiculopathy or refractory spondylosis. Surgery = last resort. Opioids = poor evidence for chronic LBP, high risk in elderly. COMLEX: OMM continues alongside procedural management and is not mutually exclusive.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Endplate and osteocyte dysfunction in lumbosacral spondylosis drives the structural changes that lead to clinical pain refractory to conservative management","whyNot":{"C":"Opioids have minimal evidence ","E":"Long-term corticosteroids wors"}},{"id":"sdl19_q43","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman has 5 weeks of right anterior thigh pain after lifting a heavy box. She says her right knee suddenly buckles when she walks down stairs. The patellar reflex is reduced on the right. Using the course's functional clues for lumbosacral radiculopathy, which interpretation of the knee buckling is best?","choices":{"A":"Toe dragging from tibialis anterior weakness, which points to the L5 root and would come with a reduced Achilles reflex","B":"Hip extensor weakness from gluteus maximus involvement, which points to the S1 root and causes the knee to collapse","C":"Saddle anesthesia with sphincter weakness, which points to the S2 to S4 roots and requires emergent decompression","D":"Quadriceps weakness, which the course ties to the L3 and L4 roots, matching her anterior thigh pain and patellar reflex","E":"Hamstring weakness from tibial nerve involvement, which points to the L5 root and destabilizes the knee in flexion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course lists bedside functional clues for lumbosacral radiculopathy: inability to get up from a chair suggests iliopsoas or quadriceps weakness (L2, L3, L4); buckling of the knee is consistent with quadriceps weakness (L3, L4); dragging of the toe points to tibialis anterior weakness (L5); and urinary or fecal incontinence may reflect S2 to S4 sphincter weakness. Knee buckling with anterior thigh pain and a reduced patellar reflex fits L3 or L4. Toe dragging is the L5 clue, and L5 has no reliable reflex. Gluteus maximus weakness does not produce knee buckling on stairs as described. She has no saddle anesthesia or sphincter symptoms. Hamstring weakness would not cause the knee to buckle into flexion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lumbar roots: L4 patellar reflex, quadriceps, medial leg; L5 dorsiflexion/EHL, dorsum of foot, no reliable reflex; S1 Achilles reflex, plantarflexion, lateral foot. COMLEX: Initial care = analgesics/NSAIDs or oral steroids + activity modification; later PT, OMM, epidural steroid injection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — closed kinetic chain quadriceps strengthening"},{"id":"sdl19_q44","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman presents with right leg pain radiating from the buttock to the posterior thigh, calf, and plantar surface of the foot. She has difficulty standing on her tiptoes. Examination reveals 4/5 plantar flexion on the right, absent right Achilles reflex, and reduced pinprick sensation over the right lateral foot and little toe. Which nerve root is most likely compressed?","choices":{"A":"Dorsiflexion weakness, foot drop, and sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot with intact Achilles reflex are the classic triad of L5 nerve root compression rather than the S1 presentation described","B":"Knee extension weakness, diminished patellar reflex, and sensory loss over the medial calf and ankle define L4 nerve root involvement rather than the posterior leg and plantar foot distribution","C":"Plantar flexion weakness, absent Achilles reflex, and sensory loss over the lateral foot and little toe are the classic triad of S1 nerve root compression at the L5-S1 disc level","D":"Hip abductor weakness, preserved lower extremity reflexes, and lateral thigh numbness define L2 to L3 nerve root involvement rather than the posterior chain sensory and reflex pattern described","E":"Bilateral leg weakness with bowel or bladder dysfunction and saddle anesthesia defines cauda equina syndrome rather than the unilateral radicular pattern with isolated S1 reflex loss described"},"correct":"C","explanation":"S1 myotome: gastrocnemius/soleus (plantar flexion — heel raises). S1 dermatome: lateral foot, little toe, posterior calf. S1 reflex: Achilles. Compressed at L5-S1. Clinical test: inability to stand on tiptoes = S1 weakness. Distinguish from S1 from L5: L5 = dorsiflexion + dorsal foot; S1 = plantar flexion + lateral/plantar foot + Achilles loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: S1 = can't do heel raises (plantar flexion weak) + Achilles reflex loss + lateral foot/little toe numbness. Compressed by L5-S1 disc. COMLEX: \"Can't walk on heels\" = L4/L5 (dorsiflexion); \"Can't walk on toes\" = S1 (plantar flexion).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injury patterns and the Achilles reflex arc are the same mechanisms evaluated in lumbosacral radiculopathy and peripheral nerve injury","whyNot":{"A":"Distinguish from S1 from L5: L"}},{"id":"sdl19_q45","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old man has a 4-week history of left leg pain. Straight leg raise on the left reproduces radicular pain below the knee at 35 degrees of elevation. A crossed straight leg raise on the right also reproduces left leg pain. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of the straight leg raise test?","choices":{"A":"The straight leg raise compresses the sacroiliac joint by externally rotating the hip in the supine position, reproducing sacroiliac joint pain that radiates to the buttock and posterior thigh","B":"The straight leg raise measures sciatic nerve mobility at the popliteal fossa, and a positive test at any angle confirms a tibial neuropathy","C":"The straight leg raise stretches the hamstring muscles, and pain at less than 60 degrees indicates hamstring tightness or proximal hamstring tendinopathy","D":"The straight leg raise loads the L4-L5 facet joints by extending the lumbar spine in the supine position, reproducing facet-mediated pain that radiates to the lateral thigh in facet arthropathy","E":"The straight leg raise tensions the L4, L5, and S1 roots; radicular pain radiating below the knee indicates an inflamed or compressed lower lumbar root"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SLR mechanism: hip flexion with knee extension stretches the sciatic nerve and its contributing roots (L4, L5, S1). When a root is inflamed or compressed by a disc herniation, this tension reproduces radicular pain below the knee, typically between 30 and 70 degrees of hip flexion (Foundations Ch 93). A positive crossed SLR (lifting the asymptomatic leg reproduces pain down the affected leg) is highly specific but insensitive for nerve root compression by a herniated disk (Tintinalli Ch 279, the SDL 19 course reading). SLR does not test the sacroiliac joint, hamstrings, or facets.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLR positive = radicular pain below the knee, typically between 30 and 70 degrees = inflamed or compressed nerve root. Sensitivity 68-80% for L4-L5/L5-S1 herniation (Tintinalli). Crossed SLR = highly specific but insensitive. COMLEX: SLR is part of standard lumbar OMM neurological screening before any high-velocity technique.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — The straight leg raise test tensioning L4-S1 nerve roots is the same principle used in lower extremity peripheral nerve tension tests for sciatic nerve mobility","whyNot":{"C":"SLR does not test the sacroili","D":"SLR does not test the sacroili"}},{"id":"sdl19_q46","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 69-year-old man has 1 year of low back pain and bilateral buttock and leg pain that comes on with walking and prolonged standing and eases with sitting. His strength, sensation, reflexes, and pulses are normal. His daughter asks how stenosis can be the diagnosis when the examination is normal. Which response best reflects the course reading?","choices":{"A":"Examination findings are often absent in lumbar stenosis; the diagnosis rests mainly on history and is confirmed by MRI or CT","B":"A normal examination excludes lumbar stenosis, because canal narrowing always reduces the Achilles reflexes on both sides","C":"A normal examination means the pain is psychosomatic, so imaging should be avoided and counseling arranged instead","D":"A normal examination points to vascular claudication, so ankle-brachial indices must be abnormal for stenosis to be considered","E":"A normal examination requires electrodiagnostic testing, because stenosis is confirmed exclusively by nerve conduction studies"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Tintinalli chapter assigned for this SDL describes lumbar spinal stenosis as narrowing of the spinal canal, nerve root canal, or foramina from degenerative disease, with symptoms usually beginning in the sixth decade: back and leg pain aggravated by standing and extension and relieved by rest and forward flexion. It states that physical examination findings are often absent and that the diagnosis is made principally by history and confirmed by CT or MRI. A normal exam therefore does not exclude stenosis. Reflex loss is not required. Calling the pain psychosomatic ignores a classic history. Normal pulses do not point to vascular disease. Nerve conduction studies are not the confirmatory test.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neurogenic claudication: worse with standing/extension, better with sitting/flexion; pulses normal. Vascular claudication: relieved by standing still, abnormal pulses/ABI. COMLEX: Symptomatic treatment matches chronic back pain; surgery after failed conservative care.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — red flags and imaging rules in mechanical low back pain","whyNot":{"C":"Calling the pain psychosomatic"}},{"id":"sdl19_q47","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with achondroplasia has 6 months of bilateral leg pain and heaviness when walking that improves when he sits. MRI shows a narrow lumbar canal along several levels with a narrow interpedicular distance and little degenerative change. How is his spinal stenosis best classified?","choices":{"A":"Acquired degenerative stenosis, since lumbar stenosis in any patient arises from disc and facet wear after age 50","B":"Foraminal stenosis at one level, since a narrow canal compresses just the single exiting root at the tightest foramen","C":"Spondylolisthesis, since a narrow interpedicular distance in achondroplasia is by definition a forward vertebral slip","D":"Vascular claudication, since achondroplasia narrows the iliac arteries and causes leg pain relieved by sitting","E":"Congenital stenosis, since FGFR3-related growth plate failure leaves a small canal that can compress neural tissue"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that lumbosacral spinal stenosis can be congenital or acquired. The SDL 1 reading explains that achondroplasia, a gain-of-function FGFR3 mutation that impairs chondrocyte proliferation in growth plates, is commonly associated with spinal stenosis that may cause signs of neural compression. A young adult with achondroplasia, a multilevel narrow canal with a narrow interpedicular distance, and little degeneration has congenital stenosis. Acquired degenerative stenosis typically begins in the sixth decade with ligamentum flavum, facet, and disc changes. A multilevel narrow canal is central, not a single-level foraminal lesion. A narrow interpedicular distance is not spondylolisthesis. Vascular claudication is relieved by stopping, regardless of posture, and is not a feature of achondroplasia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Achondroplasia = AD gain-of-function FGFR3 → impaired endochondral growth; short limbs, normal trunk, large head, narrow interpedicular distance, spinal stenosis risk. COMLEX: Congenitally narrow canals become symptomatic earlier and with less degeneration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — achondroplasia and the small spinal canal"},{"id":"sdl19_q48","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with MRI-confirmed severe central lumbar spinal stenosis at L3-L4 and L4-L5 presents after 6 months of physical therapy, NSAIDs, and two epidural steroid injections. She continues to have neurogenic claudication limiting walking to less than half a block. She has no significant surgical comorbidities. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Continuing epidural steroid injections indefinitely at 3-month intervals is the appropriate management for lumbar stenosis that has failed conservative therapy, as surgical outcomes are equivalent to injection therapy at 5 years","B":"Surgical decompression via lumbar laminectomy with or without fusion is indicated for lumbar spinal stenosis with persistent neurogenic claudication after failure of adequate conservative management including epidural injections","C":"Intrathecal morphine via implanted pump is the next appropriate step for lumbar stenosis refractory to two epidural steroid injections, providing analgesia without the risks of open surgical decompression","D":"Repeating lumbar MRI in 6 months to reassess stenosis severity before considering any procedural intervention is the most appropriate next step, as neurogenic claudication uniformly stabilizes without surgery","E":"Prescribing a walker and permanent activity restriction is the appropriate management for elderly patients with lumbar stenosis who fail conservative therapy, as surgical decompression is contraindicated over age 65"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Surgical indication for lumbar stenosis: persistent, functionally limiting neurogenic claudication after failure of adequate conservative management including PT and ESI. Standard procedure: lumbar laminectomy (decompression), with fusion if instability or spondylolisthesis coexists. In the SPORT trial, surgically treated patients with spinal stenosis and degenerative spondylolisthesis improved more than nonsurgical patients over 2 and 4 years (Foundations Ch 93). Age alone is not a contraindication. Intrathecal pumps are not standard for stenosis. Indefinite ESI is not appropriate after two failures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lumbar stenosis surgical indication = persistent neurogenic claudication after failed conservative management (PT + ESI). SPORT trial demonstrated surgical benefit at 2-4 years. Laminectomy = standard decompression. Fusion added if spondylolisthesis present. COMLEX: OMM is adjunctive; does not substitute for surgery when indicated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — Hip and vascular causes of leg pain with ambulation are excluded by workup before surgical planning for lumbar stenosis, ensuring the correct etiology is targeted","whyNot":{"A":"Indefinite ESI is not appropri","C":"Intrathecal pumps are not stan","E":"Age alone is not a contraindic"}}],"22":[{"id":"sdl22_q01","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the common properties of the kinetics of NSAIDs.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis asks why her naproxen must be taken twice daily while her friend's piroxicam is dosed only once daily. Which pharmacokinetic property most directly determines NSAID dosing frequency?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic first-pass extraction rate, which determines the fraction of an oral dose that reaches systemic circulation before entering the portal circulation and undergoing presystemic metabolism","B":"Renal clearance rate, which governs the daily volume of plasma cleared of drug and determines the risk of toxicity accumulation in patients with impaired kidney function","C":"Elimination half-life, which ranges from approximately 2 hours for ibuprofen to approximately 50 hours for piroxicam, governs how often a dose must be administered to maintain therapeutic concentrations","D":"Volume of distribution, which determines how extensively a drug distributes into peripheral tissues and fat depots relative to the plasma compartment","E":"Oral bioavailability, which determines the maximum achievable plasma concentration after a single dose and sets the upper bound for therapeutic exposure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NSAIDs vary widely in half-life: ibuprofen ~2 h, naproxen ~12-17 h, piroxicam ~50 h. Half-life determines the dosing interval needed to maintain therapeutic plasma and synovial fluid concentrations. Bioavailability, volume of distribution, and clearance all influence pharmacokinetics but it is half-life specifically that dictates how frequently a drug must be redosed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Piroxicam (t1/2 ~50 h) = once daily; naproxen (t1/2 ~12-17 h) = twice daily; ibuprofen (t1/2 ~2 h) = three to four times daily. COMLEX: NSAID dosing frequency reflects half-life; synovial fluid concentrations can outlast serum concentrations for short-t1/2 agents.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis management includes NSAIDs; kinetic properties determine dosing interval and joint-level drug accumulation","whyNot":{"B":"Bioavailability, volume of dis","E":"Bioavailability, volume of dis"}},{"id":"sdl22_q02","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the common source of mechanism of action for NSAID anti-inflammatory activity.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pharmacology student is asked to describe the primary mechanism by which NSAIDs reduce inflammation, pain, and fever. Which of the following best describes this mechanism?","choices":{"A":"NSAIDs activate lipocortin, which inhibits phospholipase A2 and prevents the release of arachidonic acid from membrane phospholipids, thereby blocking all downstream eicosanoid synthesis upstream of cyclooxygenase","B":"NSAIDs block nuclear factor kappa-B nuclear translocation, reducing transcription of pro-inflammatory cytokines including TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6 in activated macrophages and synoviocytes","C":"NSAIDs directly antagonize bradykinin B2 and histamine H1 receptors on sensory nerve fibers, preventing peripheral nociceptor activation without affecting prostaglandin biosynthesis","D":"NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase enzymes, blocking conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and prostacyclin and thereby reducing pain sensitization, vasodilation, and fever","E":"NSAIDs upregulate heme oxygenase-1 expression in endothelial cells, increasing production of anti-inflammatory carbon monoxide and biliverdin that suppress leukocyte adhesion and activation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The primary mechanism of all NSAIDs is COX (cyclooxygenase) inhibition. COX-1 and COX-2 convert arachidonic acid to PGH2, the precursor to prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and prostacyclin. Prostaglandins sensitize nociceptors, promote vasodilation, and act on the hypothalamus to raise body temperature. Blocking COX reduces all these effects. The other choices describe mechanisms of corticosteroids, biologics, or secondary NSAID actions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: COX-1 = constitutive (gastric protection, platelet aggregation); COX-2 = inducible (inflammation, fever). NSAIDs block both (nonselective) or COX-2 only (celecoxib). COMLEX: Prostaglandins mediate inflammation and sensitize nociceptors; COX inhibition reduces PGE2-dependent fever set point in the hypothalamus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Bone remodeling is partially regulated by prostaglandins; COX-derived PGs modulate osteoclast and osteoblast activity","whyNot":{"A":"The other choices describe mec","B":"The other choices describe mec","C":"The other choices describe mec","E":"The other choices describe mec"}},{"id":"sdl22_q03","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe other secondary mechanisms of action common for NSAIDs.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"An attending explains that indomethacin's anti-inflammatory potency exceeds what would be expected from COX inhibition alone. Which of the following best describes indomethacin's secondary mechanisms of action?","choices":{"A":"Indomethacin inhibits phospholipase A2 and C, reduces neutrophil chemotaxis and adhesion, suppresses free radical production, inhibits lymphocyte proliferation, and lowers IL-1 synthesis independently of COX","B":"Indomethacin activates peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma in macrophages, shifting polarization toward an anti-inflammatory phenotype and suppressing NF-kB target gene transcription in synovial tissue","C":"Indomethacin competitively blocks bradykinin B2 receptors and histamine H1 receptors at peripheral nociceptors, reducing afferent pain signal transduction independently of prostaglandin synthesis inhibition","D":"Indomethacin inhibits 5-lipoxygenase in mast cells and eosinophils, blocking leukotriene C4 and D4 synthesis and preventing bronchoconstriction and vascular permeability changes at sites of inflammation","E":"Indomethacin stabilizes lysosomal membranes in neutrophils and macrophages, preventing release of proteolytic enzymes and reactive oxygen species that would otherwise amplify tissue inflammation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Indomethacin and several other NSAIDs have secondary anti-inflammatory mechanisms including inhibition of phospholipase A2/C (reducing arachidonic acid availability upstream of COX), inhibition of neutrophil migration and adhesion, suppression of free radical production, and reduction of T- and B-cell proliferation. These account for its clinical potency beyond COX inhibition alone and also contribute to its toxicity profile.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secondary NSAID mechanisms include phospholipase inhibition, anti-chemotaxis, and lymphocyte suppression — these are lower-yield but distinguish indomethacin as a particularly potent anti-inflammatory. COMLEX: Indomethacin is used for ankylosing spondylitis and gout; its broad mechanisms underlie both efficacy and toxicity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — NSAIDs exert secondary effects on connective tissue and joint physiology beyond COX inhibition"},{"id":"sdl22_q04","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Discuss adverse effects of NSAIDs by body system.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with knee osteoarthritis takes ibuprofen 600 mg three times daily. After six months he develops epigastric pain and is found to have a gastric ulcer on endoscopy. Which mechanism best explains NSAID-induced gastric mucosal injury?","choices":{"A":"NSAIDs undergo extensive enterohepatic recirculation, directly damaging the colonic and gastric mucosa through repeated high-concentration biliary re-exposure to the chemically irritant parent drug compound","B":"COX-1 inhibition reduces prostaglandin E2 and prostacyclin in gastric mucosa, impairing mucus and bicarbonate secretion, decreasing mucosal blood flow, and leaving the epithelium vulnerable to acid-peptic injury","C":"NSAIDs block parietal cell histamine H2 receptors, paradoxically increasing gastrin-stimulated basal acid output and reducing the efficacy of endogenous mucosal protection mechanisms","D":"COX-2 inhibition in gastric epithelial stem cells impairs mucosal regeneration and prevents healing of micro-erosions caused by normal mechanical and chemical stress from gastric contents","E":"NSAIDs directly activate mast cells in the gastric submucosa, triggering histamine release that increases acid secretion through H2 receptors while simultaneously causing local vascular permeability changes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"COX-1 is constitutively expressed in gastric mucosa and produces PGE2 and prostacyclin that stimulate mucus and bicarbonate secretion, promote mucosal blood flow, and inhibit acid secretion. NSAID-mediated COX-1 inhibition removes this protection, exposing the epithelium to acid-peptic damage. Selective COX-2 inhibitors (celecoxib) spare gastric COX-1 and have significantly lower GI ulcer rates.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gastric protection by COX-1 prostaglandins is the key concept — inhibiting COX-1 removes the mucus/bicarbonate/blood flow triad. Add a PPI or use celecoxib to reduce risk. COMLEX: GI adverse effects are the most common reason NSAIDs are discontinued; H. pylori co-infection multiplies the risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 9 — Long-term NSAID adverse effects (GI, renal, CV) are central considerations in the knee arthritis diagnostic and management workflow","whyNot":{"D":"Selective COX-2 inhibitors (ce"}},{"id":"sdl22_q05","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss adverse effects of NSAIDs by category (salicylates, COX-2 selective inhibitors, nonselective COX inhibitors).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A hematologist is counseling an orthopedic patient about NSAID selection. She explains that one class carries a critical antiplatelet effect that can persist for over a week. Which statement best distinguishes aspirin from nonacetylated salicylates such as salsalate regarding platelet effects?","choices":{"A":"Aspirin irreversibly acetylates platelet COX-1, blocking thromboxane A2 synthesis for the 8 to 10 day platelet lifespan; nonacetylated salicylates cannot acetylate COX and do not inhibit aggregation","B":"Nonacetylated salicylates selectively inhibit COX-2 in platelets, reducing prostacyclin production while leaving thromboxane A2 intact, thereby paradoxically promoting platelet aggregation compared to aspirin","C":"Aspirin causes immune-mediated thrombocytopenia through hapten formation, reducing platelet counts and prolonging bleeding time; nonacetylated salicylates prolong aggregation time through reversible COX-1 binding only","D":"Nonacetylated salicylates cause greater platelet inhibition than aspirin because their longer plasma half-lives allow more sustained exposure of platelet COX-1 to the active salicylate moiety","E":"Aspirin activates fibrinolysis through tissue plasminogen activator upregulation in endothelial cells; nonacetylated salicylates inhibit fibrinolysis through plasminogen activator inhibitor-1 induction in hepatocytes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aspirin is unique among salicylates in permanently acetylating serine-530 on COX-1. Because platelets are anucleate, they cannot synthesize new COX-1; therefore, the antiplatelet effect lasts the entire ~8-10 day platelet lifespan. Nonacetylated salicylates (salsalate, choline magnesium trisalicylate) do not acetylate COX and thus do not inhibit platelet aggregation, making them suitable for patients with bleeding risk or aspirin-exacerbated asthma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aspirin = only NSAID that covalently (irreversibly) modifies COX. All others = reversible competitive inhibitors. COMLEX: Nonacetylated salicylates tolerated in aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (no COX-1 platelet effect, no leukotriene shunting).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — Hip osteoarthritis management requires selecting NSAIDs by category based on individual patient risk profile (GI, CV, renal)","whyNot":{"B":"Nonacetylated salicylates (sal","C":"Nonacetylated salicylates (sal","D":"Nonacetylated salicylates (sal"}},{"id":"sdl22_q06","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the mechanism of action for other analgesics including acetaminophen, ketorolac, and tramadol.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with moderate musculoskeletal low back pain is prescribed tramadol. His provider explains that tramadol works differently from both traditional NSAIDs and pure opioids. Which statement best describes tramadol's mechanism of action?","choices":{"A":"Tramadol is a selective COX-2 inhibitor with no opioid receptor activity, providing anti-inflammatory analgesia through prostaglandin inhibition without the respiratory depression or abuse potential of opioids","B":"Tramadol is a full mu-opioid agonist with additional anticholinergic properties that reduce smooth muscle spasm contributing to musculoskeletal pain without affecting reuptake transporters","C":"Tramadol inhibits both COX-1 and COX-2 while activating alpha-2 adrenoceptors in the dorsal horn, reducing substance P release through a presynaptic mechanism independent of opioid receptor engagement","D":"Tramadol is a selective delta-opioid receptor agonist that modulates limbic system pain processing through hippocampal circuits without affecting spinal cord neurotransmission or reuptake transporters","E":"Tramadol acts as a weak mu-opioid agonist while also inhibiting serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake transporters, modulating descending pain inhibitory pathways in the spinal cord and brainstem"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tramadol combines two distinct mechanisms: weak mu-opioid receptor agonism (via the active O-desmethyltramadol metabolite) and inhibition of both serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake, similar to SNRIs. This dual action modulates descending inhibitory pain pathways. The SNRI component creates significant risk for serotonin syndrome when combined with SSRIs, MAOIs, or other serotonergic drugs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tramadol = weak mu agonist + SNRI. Risk: serotonin syndrome with SSRIs/MAOIs; lower seizure threshold. Not a first-line analgesic for patients on serotonergic drugs. COMLEX: Tramadol serotonin syndrome is a tested clinical presentation; also note it requires CYP2D6 for active metabolite formation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain management employs acetaminophen, ketorolac, and tramadol as non-opioid analgesic options"},{"id":"sdl22_q07","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the mechanism of action of opioid medications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A medical student is studying opioid receptor pharmacology for Step 1. Which of the following best describes the receptor type, signal transduction mechanism, and net physiological effect shared by mu, delta, and kappa opioid receptors?","choices":{"A":"Opioid receptors are ligand-gated chloride channels that directly hyperpolarize neurons upon agonist binding, bypassing second-messenger cascades and producing instantaneous inhibition of action potential generation","B":"Mu receptors couple exclusively to Gs proteins, increasing cAMP and activating protein kinase A to open calcium channels and facilitate ascending pain signal transmission in the dorsal horn","C":"Delta and kappa receptors couple to Gq proteins, activating phospholipase C to increase IP3 and diacylglycerol, triggering protein kinase C-mediated excitatory neuronal effects that paradoxically reduce pain perception","D":"Opioid receptors activate receptor tyrosine kinase cascades similar to growth factor receptors, producing long-term synaptic potentiation in pain processing circuits through ERK and Akt phosphorylation","E":"Mu, delta, and kappa receptors are Gi/o-coupled GPCRs that lower cAMP, close presynaptic calcium channels, and open postsynaptic potassium channels, reducing neuronal excitability and pain transmission"},"correct":"E","explanation":"All three classical opioid receptors (mu, delta, kappa) are Gi/o-coupled GPCRs. Gi/o activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase (decreasing cAMP), closes N-type presynaptic Ca2+ channels (reducing neurotransmitter release), and opens GIRK (inwardly rectifying K+) channels postsynaptically (causing hyperpolarization). The net result is reduced neuronal excitability and pain signal propagation at all levels of the pain pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mu/delta/kappa = Gi/o GPCRs. Endogenous ligands: beta-endorphin (mu preferred), enkephalin (delta preferred), dynorphin (kappa preferred). COMLEX: Opioid receptor classification is core pharmacology; Gi/o coupling is the unifying mechanism across all three subtypes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — Opioid receptor pharmacology is foundational to understanding acute pain management in femoroacetabular dislocations"},{"id":"sdl22_q08","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Outline acceptable uses for opioid medications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A primary care physician is reviewing the current opioid prescribing framework with a third-year student. Which of the following represents an acceptable and guideline-supported use of opioid medications?","choices":{"A":"First-line therapy for mild-to-moderate chronic low back pain in patients who have not yet tried non-opioid analgesics such as NSAIDs, acetaminophen, physical therapy, or cognitive behavioral therapy","B":"Routine long-term maintenance therapy for osteoarthritis of the knee or hip when patients report inadequate response to acetaminophen after only two weeks of therapy","C":"Management of acute severe pain (postoperative, trauma-related), cancer-related pain at any stage, and palliative or end-of-life care where comfort is the primary treatment goal","D":"Preferred initial treatment for all neuropathic pain syndromes over gabapentin, pregabalin, tricyclic antidepressants, or SNRIs in adult outpatient settings with documented chronic pain","E":"Long-term monotherapy for fibromyalgia syndrome when physical therapy and antidepressant medications have been trialed for fewer than four weeks without achieving complete symptom resolution"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Opioids are appropriate for acute severe pain (surgery, trauma), cancer pain at any stage, and palliative/end-of-life care. They are not first-line for chronic non-cancer musculoskeletal pain, where non-opioid agents and physical therapy should be exhausted first. Long-term opioid use for OA or fibromyalgia is generally not guideline-supported as first- or second-line therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the three main acceptable opioid indications. For chronic pain, CDC guidelines emphasize non-opioid alternatives first. COMLEX: Acceptable opioid use is a tested clinical presentation; addiction medicine and responsible prescribing are COMLEX-emphasized topics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Opioid prescribing guidelines apply to Achilles tendinopathy and musculoskeletal pain management","whyNot":{"A":"They are not first-line for ch","B":"Long-term opioid use for OA or","E":"Long-term opioid use for OA or"}},{"id":"sdl22_q09","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Discuss adverse effects of opioid medication.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with chronic cancer pain has taken oral morphine at stable doses for two years. He requires the same dose for adequate analgesia as when he started. He continues to have severe constipation despite daily laxative use. Which explanation is most accurate for this finding?","choices":{"A":"Respiratory depression does not develop tolerance because brainstem pre-Botzinger complex mu receptors remain constitutively sensitized throughout chronic opioid therapy regardless of dose or duration","B":"Constipation and miosis persist throughout chronic opioid therapy because enteric mu receptors and pupillary sphincter neurons do not undergo the receptor desensitization seen in CNS pain-processing circuits","C":"Euphoria does not develop tolerance because mesolimbic dopamine release in the nucleus accumbens is mediated by delta receptors, which are not subject to the Gi-mediated desensitization that affects mu receptors","D":"Sedation is uniquely resistant to tolerance because cortical arousal pathways in the ascending reticular activating system are constitutively protected from receptor internalization by scaffold proteins","E":"Nausea is the opioid adverse effect most resistant to tolerance because vestibular system chemoreceptor trigger zone neurons have constitutively high baseline mu receptor expression that resists downregulation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tolerance develops to most CNS-mediated opioid effects (analgesia, euphoria, sedation, respiratory depression) through receptor desensitization, downregulation, and uncoupling from Gi/o proteins. However, constipation and miosis do not develop tolerance because peripheral myenteric plexus mu receptors (governing GI motility) and iris sphincter neurons do not undergo the same adaptive changes. This is why laxatives are always co-prescribed with chronic opioids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tolerance does NOT develop to constipation or miosis — classic board point. COMLEX: Always prescribe prophylactic laxative with chronic opioids; stimulant laxatives (senna) preferred over osmotic agents for opioid-induced constipation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Opioid adverse effects inform chronic shoulder pathology pain management decisions"},{"id":"sdl22_q10","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the common properties of the kinetics of NSAIDs.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 59-year-old man with knee osteoarthritis takes ibuprofen three times daily. He reads that ibuprofen has a serum half-life of about 2 hours and worries that his knee is unprotected for most of each dosing interval. According to the pharmacology reading for this SDL, which statement about NSAID distribution into joints best answers his concern?","choices":{"A":"NSAIDs do not enter synovial fluid, so joint benefit comes from central COX inhibition and lasts as long as serum levels","B":"Short half-life NSAIDs remain in synovial fluid longer than their serum half-lives predict after repeated dosing","C":"Long half-life NSAIDs leave synovial fluid much faster than they leave serum, so short-acting drugs are always preferred","D":"NSAIDs reach the joint exclusively after hepatic conversion to active metabolites, which have half-lives of several days","E":"Synovial NSAID levels depend on albumin in joint fluid, so levels fall to zero as soon as serum albumin binding drops"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Katzung's NSAID chapter assigned for this SDL states that all NSAIDs can be found in synovial fluid after repeated dosing, and that drugs with short half-lives remain in the joints longer than their serum half-lives would predict, while drugs with longer half-lives leave synovial fluid at a rate proportional to their serum half-lives. NSAIDs do reach synovial fluid. Long half-life drugs track their serum half-lives rather than clearing faster. Most NSAIDs are active parent compounds; prodrugs such as nabumetone and sulindac are exceptions, not the rule. The chapter notes most NSAIDs are about 98% bound to albumin in plasma, but it does not describe synovial levels vanishing when binding changes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAID kinetics: well absorbed, ~98% albumin bound, hepatic CYP2C/CYP3A metabolism and glucuronidation, renal elimination, enterohepatic circulation (more circulation → more lower GI irritation); half-life ranges ~2 h (ibuprofen) to ~50 h (piroxicam). COMLEX: Dosing interval follows serum half-life, but joint levels persist longer for short-acting agents.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 4 — NSAID adverse effects by body system","whyNot":{"A":"NSAIDs do reach synovial fluid","D":"Most NSAIDs are active parent "}},{"id":"sdl22_q11","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the common source of mechanism of action for NSAID anti-inflammatory activity.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with a history of myocardial infarction takes low-dose aspirin 81 mg daily for secondary prevention. His cardiologist explains that aspirin's antiplatelet effect is irreversible, unlike other NSAIDs. Which mechanism best explains why aspirin's COX inhibition permanently eliminates thromboxane A2 synthesis in platelets for 8 to 10 days?","choices":{"A":"Aspirin is the only NSAID small enough to cross the platelet plasma membrane and reach intracellular COX-1 stores; other NSAIDs are excluded by the platelet membrane lipid bilayer and can only inhibit surface-associated COX","B":"Aspirin selectively inhibits COX-2 at low doses while simultaneously acetylating a different serine residue on COX-1 that produces 15-epi-lipoxin A4, an endogenous anti-aggregatory mediator that suppresses platelet function for days","C":"Aspirin covalently acetylates serine-530 on the COX-1 active site, permanently inactivating the enzyme; since platelets are anucleate and cannot transcribe or translate new COX-1 protein, the inhibition lasts the entire platelet lifespan","D":"Aspirin is metabolized to salicylate in the liver, and it is the salicylate metabolite that permanently binds the ADP receptor P2Y12 on platelet surfaces, preventing aggregation signal transduction independently of the COX pathway","E":"Aspirin irreversibly inhibits both COX-1 and COX-2 with equal potency at any dose; other NSAIDs selectively target COX-2 at clinical doses and therefore preserve thromboxane A2 synthesis from platelet COX-1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aspirin uniquely acetylates serine-530 on COX-1 (and serine-516 on COX-2), forming a covalent bond that permanently inactivates the enzyme. In nucleated cells, new COX protein can be synthesized within hours, restoring function. Platelets, being anucleate, cannot replace inactivated COX-1 for the rest of their 8-10 day lifespan. All other NSAIDs are reversible competitive inhibitors, so their antiplatelet effect resolves as drug is cleared.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aspirin = irreversible (covalent) COX acetylation; all other NSAIDs = reversible competitive inhibition. Platelets anucleate = no new COX synthesis = lasting antiplatelet effect. COMLEX: Preoperative aspirin cessation timing is based on platelet lifespan (~10 days for full recovery).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — Knee ligament injuries: perioperative antiplatelet management requires understanding aspirin irreversible COX-1 acetylation and the timeline for platelet recovery"},{"id":"sdl22_q12","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe other secondary mechanisms of action common for NSAIDs.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with osteoarthritis of both knees asks his rheumatologist whether long-term NSAID use might offer any cancer prevention benefits. The rheumatologist mentions epidemiological data suggesting reduced colorectal cancer incidence with regular NSAID use. Which secondary mechanism best explains this potential protective effect?","choices":{"A":"NSAIDs upregulate p53 tumor suppressor expression in colonic epithelial cells by reducing prostaglandin E2-mediated ubiquitination of p53 protein, enhancing apoptotic elimination of genetically damaged pre-malignant cells","B":"NSAIDs reduce vascular endothelial growth factor expression in colonic stroma by blocking COX-2-derived 12-hydroxyeicosatetraenoic acid signaling, impairing angiogenesis in early adenoma microenvironments","C":"NSAIDs stimulate Toll-like receptor 4 signaling in colonic macrophages, increasing local interferon-gamma production that activates cytotoxic T-lymphocytes against dysplastic epithelial cells expressing altered surface antigens","D":"COX-2 inhibition in colonic epithelium reduces PGE2-driven proliferation and apoptosis resistance in pre-malignant cells; epidemiological studies show up to 50 percent lower colorectal cancer risk with 5 or more years of use","E":"NSAIDs inhibit the Wnt signaling pathway in intestinal stem cells by reducing beta-catenin phosphorylation downstream of prostaglandin receptor activation, decreasing stem cell renewal in the colon crypt epithelium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A well-documented secondary benefit of NSAIDs — particularly aspirin and indomethacin — is colorectal cancer risk reduction. COX-2 is overexpressed in colonic adenomas and carcinomas; its product PGE2 promotes cell proliferation and suppresses apoptosis. Regular NSAID use inhibits this pathway, with epidemiological data showing ~50% reduction in colorectal cancer incidence with long-term use. This is distinct from COX primary anti-inflammatory mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: COX-2 overexpressed in colorectal adenomas/carcinomas — NSAIDs reduce cancer risk via PGE2 reduction. COMLEX: Secondary NSAID mechanisms include chemopreventive properties; indomethacin is particularly well-studied in this regard.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — Exercise and joint aging: secondary NSAID mechanisms reduce oxidative and proliferative tissue injury in chronically inflamed tissues beyond primary COX inhibition"},{"id":"sdl22_q13","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Discuss adverse effects of NSAIDs by body system.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with NYHA Class II heart failure and knee osteoarthritis is prescribed ibuprofen by his orthopedic surgeon. One week later, his serum creatinine rises from 1.1 to 2.4 mg/dL. His urine output has decreased. Which mechanism best explains this NSAID-induced acute kidney injury?","choices":{"A":"Renal prostaglandins normally maintain afferent arteriolar vasodilation to preserve GFR when renal perfusion is reduced; NSAID COX inhibition removes this protection, precipitating acute prerenal azotemia in heart failure","B":"Ibuprofen directly inhibits aquaporin-2 water channels in collecting duct principal cells, reducing free water reabsorption, causing medullary washout, and impairing the concentrating ability needed to maintain adequate GFR","C":"NSAID metabolites compete with creatinine for proximal tubular secretion transporters, pharmacologically blocking creatinine excretion and artificially elevating serum creatinine without truly reducing the glomerular filtration rate","D":"Ibuprofen activates renal thromboxane A2 receptors on afferent arterioles, directly causing vasoconstriction and reducing single-nephron GFR through a prostaglandin-independent mechanism that is not reversed by stopping the drug","E":"Chronic ibuprofen use triggers autoimmune tubulointerstitial nephritis through T-cell sensitization to NSAID-protein adducts formed in renal proximal tubular epithelial cells during phase I hepatic biotransformation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In states of reduced effective circulating volume (heart failure, cirrhosis, hypovolemia), the kidney becomes prostaglandin-dependent to maintain afferent arteriolar tone and GFR. COX inhibition removes this compensatory prostaglandin vasodilation, leading to afferent arteriolar constriction and acute reduction in GFR. This is a hemodynamically mediated, reversible form of acute kidney injury that resolves with NSAID discontinuation. NSAIDs are contraindicated in patients with decompensated heart failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prostaglandins maintain RBF/GFR when perfusion is compromised (heart failure, cirrhosis, dehydration) — NSAIDs most dangerous in these settings. COMLEX: NSAIDs contraindicated in decompensated heart failure; renal PG-dependence underlies the mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — Hip OA: NSAID pharmacokinetics and renal hemodynamic adverse effects are critical in elderly patients with underlying cardiovascular and renal comorbidities","whyNot":{"D":"This is a hemodynamically medi"}},{"id":"sdl22_q14","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss adverse effects of NSAIDs by category (salicylates, COX-2 selective inhibitors, nonselective COX inhibitors).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis requires long-term NSAID therapy. She has a documented history of peptic ulcer disease but no cardiovascular disease history and a normal echocardiogram. She needs adequate anti-inflammatory coverage. Which NSAID class best minimizes her GI complication risk?","choices":{"A":"High-dose nonselective COX inhibitors such as ibuprofen are preferred because their potent COX-2 inhibition reduces joint inflammation more rapidly than selective agents and their GI risk can be neutralized with routine proton pump inhibitor co-prescription","B":"High-dose aspirin is preferred because irreversible COX-1 inhibition paradoxically protects the gastric mucosa by suppressing thromboxane A2-mediated mast cell degranulation and reducing local histamine-driven acid secretion","C":"Selective COX-2 inhibitors such as celecoxib are preferred because sparing gastric COX-1 preserves prostaglandin E2-mediated mucus and bicarbonate secretion, significantly reducing peptic ulcer risk compared to nonselective NSAIDs","D":"Nonacetylated salicylates such as salsalate are the best choice because they provide full anti-inflammatory efficacy equivalent to celecoxib without any COX inhibition and therefore carry no GI ulcer risk","E":"Nonselective COX inhibitors combined with misoprostol fully eliminate peptic ulcer risk by replacing the gastroprotective prostaglandins lost to COX-1 inhibition and should be preferred over selective agents for their superior anti-inflammatory potency"},"correct":"C","explanation":"COX-2 selective inhibitors (celecoxib) spare gastric COX-1, preserving PGE2-mediated mucosal protection (mucus, bicarbonate, blood flow). This results in significantly fewer gastric ulcers compared to nonselective NSAIDs. In this patient with no CV history, the increased CV risk of celecoxib is less concerning than her peptic ulcer history. Nonacetylated salicylates have reduced but not absent GI risk and may have less anti-inflammatory potency for RA. Misoprostol co-prescription reduces but does not eliminate risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: COX-2 selective = fewer GI ulcers (spares COX-1 gastric protection) but greater CV risk (reduces endothelial prostacyclin). Weigh GI vs. CV risk per patient profile. COMLEX: Patient-specific risk stratification determines NSAID category selection in the clinical management framework.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — Hip OA: NSAID category selection balances GI and cardiovascular risk in elderly patients with competing comorbidities requiring chronic anti-inflammatory therapy","whyNot":{"E":"Misoprostol co-prescription re"}},{"id":"sdl22_q15","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the mechanism of action for other analgesics including acetaminophen, ketorolac, and tramadol.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with chronic low back pain and major depressive disorder is taking sertraline 100 mg daily. Her orthopedic specialist prescribes tramadol 50 mg every 6 hours. Three days later she presents with agitation, clonus, diaphoresis, and hyperthermia. Which mechanism best explains her presentation?","choices":{"A":"Tramadol is a potent inhibitor of CYP2D6, blocking sertraline metabolism and increasing sertraline plasma concentrations to toxic levels that directly stimulate 5-HT2A receptors in the brainstem independent of reuptake inhibition","B":"Tramadol activates mu-opioid receptors in the dorsal raphe nucleus, paradoxically increasing serotonin synthesis and release into synaptic clefts where sertraline prevents reuptake, creating a purely pharmacodynamic interaction","C":"Tramadol is metabolized to O-desmethyltramadol, which acts as a potent direct 5-HT2C receptor agonist and produces serotonergic toxicity independently of reuptake inhibition or pharmacokinetic interactions with sertraline","D":"Tramadol inhibits both serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake; combined with sertraline's serotonin reuptake inhibition, synaptic serotonin accumulates and overstimulates 5-HT1A and 5-HT2A receptors, causing serotonin syndrome","E":"Tramadol and sertraline both inhibit dopamine reuptake in striatal circuits, producing dopaminergic overstimulation that mimics serotonin syndrome clinically but is mechanistically mediated through D2 receptor hyperstimulation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Tramadol has dual mechanisms: weak mu-opioid agonism and inhibition of both serotonin (SERT) and norepinephrine (NET) reuptake transporters. Sertraline independently blocks SERT. The combination creates marked excess synaptic serotonin, activating 5-HT1A and 5-HT2A receptors and producing serotonin syndrome (agitation, clonus, diaphoresis, hyperthermia, tachycardia). Tramadol is contraindicated with SSRIs, SNRIs, MAOIs, and other serotonergic medications.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Serotonin syndrome triad = altered mental status + autonomic instability + neuromuscular abnormalities (clonus). Tramadol + SSRI is the classic precipitant. COMLEX: Serotonin syndrome is distinguished from NMS by clonus and rapid onset; treatment = cyproheptadine, stop offending agents.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain: tramadol serotonin syndrome risk mandates careful screening for concurrent serotonergic medications before prescribing"},{"id":"sdl22_q16","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the mechanism of action of opioid medications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A first-year medical student asks their neuroscience tutor to explain how opioids reduce pain transmission at the level of the spinal dorsal horn. The tutor describes both presynaptic and postsynaptic mechanisms. Which of the following correctly describes both components of opioid-mediated pain inhibition?","choices":{"A":"Presynaptically, opioids activate adenylyl cyclase to increase cAMP, which phosphorylates potassium channels and hyperpolarizes the presynaptic terminal; postsynaptically, opioids stimulate IP3 production to sequester intracellular calcium and reduce AMPA receptor sensitivity","B":"Presynaptically, opioids block voltage-gated sodium channels to prevent action potential propagation into the synaptic terminal; postsynaptically, opioids activate chloride channels to hyperpolarize the secondary neuron and suppress ascending pain signal transmission","C":"Presynaptically, opioids activate phospholipase C to produce diacylglycerol that directly closes N-type calcium channels; postsynaptically, opioids increase cyclic GMP through nitric oxide synthase activation, reducing glutamate receptor phosphorylation and sensitivity","D":"Presynaptically, opioids stimulate large-conductance potassium channels to prolong repolarization and reduce firing frequency; postsynaptically, opioids block NMDA receptors through an open-channel mechanism to prevent calcium influx and central sensitization","E":"Presynaptically, opioids close voltage-gated calcium channels on primary afferent terminals, reducing Ca2+ influx and release of glutamate and substance P; postsynaptically, they open inwardly rectifying potassium channels and hyperpolarize dorsal horn neurons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mu (and other opioid) receptor activation via Gi/o coupling produces two key synaptic effects: (1) presynaptically — closure of N-type (and P/Q-type) voltage-gated Ca2+ channels reduces intracellular Ca2+ and decreases vesicular release of glutamate and substance P from primary afferent C-fiber and A-delta terminals; (2) postsynaptically — opening of GIRK (G-protein-coupled inwardly rectifying K+) channels hyperpolarizes the secondary neuron, raising its threshold for firing. Together these reduce ascending pain transmission in the dorsal horn.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pre = close Ca2+ channels (less neurotransmitter release); Post = open K+ channels (hyperpolarization). Both mediated by Gi/o. COMLEX: Same mechanisms operate in supraspinal sites (PAG, RVM) to activate descending inhibitory pathways.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — Hip dislocations: opioid presynaptic and postsynaptic mechanisms explain the rapid analgesic onset needed for procedural pain control in acute joint reductions"},{"id":"sdl22_q17","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Outline acceptable uses for opioid medications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with opioid use disorder and a history of intravenous heroin use is started on buprenorphine plus naloxone (Suboxone) for maintenance therapy. His provider explains the specific pharmacological rationale for this combination. Which statement best explains the therapeutic design of this regimen?","choices":{"A":"Buprenorphine is a full mu-opioid agonist with a very long plasma half-life exceeding 72 hours; naloxone is added as a competitive antagonist that partially occupies mu receptors and creates a pharmacological ceiling preventing dose escalation-related euphoria","B":"Buprenorphine is a partial mu-opioid agonist with very high receptor affinity and slow dissociation, producing a ceiling on respiratory depression; naloxone deters IV misuse, since it is poorly absorbed sublingually but precipitates withdrawal if injected","C":"Buprenorphine is a kappa-opioid receptor antagonist that blocks the dysphoria and paranoia produced by endogenous dynorphin signaling; naloxone functions as a partial mu agonist to suppress opioid withdrawal symptoms without producing significant euphoria","D":"Buprenorphine undergoes extensive hepatic first-pass metabolism that converts it to norbuprenorphine, a full mu-opioid agonist; naloxone competitively blocks this active metabolite from binding mu receptors in mesolimbic reward circuits to eliminate the addiction potential","E":"Buprenorphine and naloxone target different opioid receptor populations in the same brain region; buprenorphine selectively activates spinal cord mu receptors for analgesia while naloxone selectively blocks limbic mu receptors to eliminate the rewarding properties of opioids"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Buprenorphine is a partial mu-opioid agonist with exceptionally high receptor affinity (displaces full agonists) and slow receptor dissociation. Its partial agonism produces analgesia and suppresses withdrawal while creating a ceiling on euphoria and respiratory depression. Sublingual naloxone is poorly absorbed (first-pass destroyed), contributing minimally to the clinical effect when taken properly. However, if the tablet is dissolved and injected, naloxone reaches systemic circulation and precipitates withdrawal, deterring intravenous misuse.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Buprenorphine = partial mu agonist (ceiling effect on respiratory depression); high affinity displaces other opioids. Naloxone in Suboxone = abuse deterrent (poor sublingual bioavailability but active IV). COMLEX: Opioid use disorder treatment with buprenorphine is a COMLEX-emphasized clinical presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — Hip dislocations: buprenorphine partial agonism and naloxone deterrence inform management decisions when opioid-maintained patients require acute pain control"},{"id":"sdl22_q18","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Discuss adverse effects of opioid medication.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 79-year-old man with stage 3 chronic kidney disease (GFR 35 mL/min) undergoes elective total hip arthroplasty. The anesthesiologist orders meperidine 50 mg IV every 4 hours for postoperative pain. On postoperative day 3, the patient develops myoclonus and then a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. Naloxone administration fails to terminate the seizure. Which mechanism is responsible?","choices":{"A":"Meperidine is metabolized to normeperidine, a renally excreted active metabolite; in renal insufficiency it accumulates and lowers the seizure threshold, which naloxone cannot reverse because normeperidine excites the CNS through non-opioid receptor mechanisms","B":"Meperidine itself directly activates NMDA glutamate receptors in the hippocampal CA3 region after accumulating to toxic plasma concentrations in renal failure, triggering excitotoxic calcium influx and seizure activity independent of opioid receptor binding","C":"Chronic meperidine administration upregulates mu-opioid receptor expression in motor cortex interneurons, paradoxically increasing cortical excitability and producing myoclonus through receptor sensitization and increased Gi-mediated disinhibition of glutamatergic circuits","D":"Meperidine undergoes O-demethylation to meperidinic acid in hepatic microsomes, and this metabolite competes with GABA at ionotropic GABA-A receptors, reducing inhibitory chloride conductance in cortical motor networks and triggering focal seizure activity","E":"Renal failure impairs meperidine excretion and forces accumulation of the parent compound in central nervous system tissue where it directly blocks glycine receptors in spinal cord and brainstem interneurons, causing disinhibitory myoclonus through loss of glycinergic inhibition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Meperidine is demethylated in the liver to normeperidine, an active metabolite that is proconvulsant. Normeperidine accumulates in patients with decreased renal function or high doses and may cause seizures (Katzung Ch 31, SDL 22 reading). Normeperidine lowers the seizure threshold through non-opioid excitatory CNS mechanisms and therefore does not respond to naloxone. Meperidine is absolutely contraindicated in renal insufficiency and in elderly patients. Preferred alternatives in renal insufficiency include hydromorphone or fentanyl; morphine is less suitable because its glucuronide metabolites (M3G, M6G) also accumulate in renal failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meperidine contraindicated in renal failure (normeperidine accumulation) and MAOIs (serotonin syndrome/hyperpyrexic crisis). Seizures from normeperidine are naloxone-unresponsive — classic board trap. COMLEX: Meperidine is also contraindicated with MAOIs; alternative opioids must be selected in elderly patients with CKD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — Hip fracture: normeperidine accumulation in renal-impaired elderly patients produces non-naloxone-reversible seizures, mandating alternative opioid analgesic selection in the perioperative setting"}],"23":[{"id":"sdl23_q01","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which therapeutic exercise type fixes the distal segment so the proximal segment moves, and is specifically recommended for shoulder and knee rehabilitation and dynamic stability?","choices":{"A":"Open kinetic chain exercise fixes the proximal segment while the distal segment moves freely, as in long arc quadriceps extension and most arm weight-lifting, making it ideal for functional improvement in activities of daily living","B":"Concentric exercise involves the distal segment being fixed during muscle shortening, generating compression forces that make it specifically suited for early post-operative knee and shoulder rehabilitation programs","C":"Eccentric exercise braces the distal segment against a fixed surface while lengthening contraction produces controlled deceleration forces, which is why it is prescribed for shoulder and knee sport-specific strengthening protocols","D":"Isometric exercise involves the distal segment being fixed against an immovable surface while the muscle contracts without joint movement, and is specifically prescribed for shoulder and knee rehabilitation after joint replacement surgery","E":"Closed kinetic chain exercise fixes the distal segment while the proximal segment moves, as in leg presses, squats, and elliptical walking, making it ideal for shoulder and knee rehabilitation and dynamic stability training"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In closed kinetic chain (CKC) exercise, the distal segment is fixed and the proximal segment moves — e.g., leg press, squat, elliptical walker. This creates multi-joint co-contraction and is ideal for shoulder and knee rehab and dynamic stability. Open kinetic chain (OKC) is the opposite: distal segment moves freely (e.g., long arc quad extension), used for ADL functional improvement. Concentric = shortening contraction; eccentric = lengthening contraction; isometric = no joint movement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know CKC vs OKC — CKC has distal segment fixed (leg press, squat), OKC has distal segment free (quad extension). COMLEX: CKC is used in ACL/post-op knee rehab due to joint stability from co-contraction; OMM complements CKC by addressing somatic dysfunction that limits kinetic chain mechanics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL injuries: therapeutic exercise types are central to conservative management and structured rehabilitation protocols","whyNot":{"B":"Concentric = shortening contra","C":"Concentric = shortening contra"}},{"id":"sdl23_q02","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A sports medicine physician prescribes eccentric heel drops for a patient with Achilles tendinopathy. Which statement correctly defines eccentric exercise and its primary therapeutic application?","choices":{"A":"Eccentric exercise occurs when a muscle contracts while simultaneously shortening, generating maximum force at the shortest fiber length, which is why it is the primary mechanism for increasing muscle mass and strength","B":"Eccentric exercise occurs when a muscle contracts while simultaneously lengthening, such as during the extension phase of a biceps or hamstring curl, and is prescribed for sport-specific strengthening and injury prevention","C":"Eccentric exercise maintains constant muscle length during contraction against an immovable surface, making it ideal for joint-protective strengthening when joint mobility is restricted after injury or surgery","D":"Eccentric exercise applies constant resistance through the complete joint range of motion in both directions using free weights or cable machines, and is primarily prescribed for general muscle conditioning","E":"Eccentric exercise specifically targets deep trunk and abdominal stabilizers through whole-body lengthening movements such as Pilates and back extension, making it the preferred choice for lumbar spine rehabilitation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eccentric contraction = muscle contracts while lengthening (e.g., extension phase of a biceps curl, lowering phase of a squat). Key therapeutic use: sport-specific strengthening and injury prevention — especially Achilles and patellar tendinopathy. Concentric (shortening) = increases mass/strength. Isometric = no length change. Isotonic = constant resistance through ROM. Core stability targets low back/trunk/abdomen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Eccentric exercises are first-line for tendinopathy rehabilitation — Alfredson protocol (eccentric heel drops) is the classic example for Achilles tendinopathy. COMLEX: Eccentric loading promotes collagen remodeling in tendons; failure to prescribe appropriately leads to chronic tendinopathy that OMM alone cannot address.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL injuries: therapeutic exercise types are central to conservative management and structured rehabilitation protocols","whyNot":{"C":"Isometric = no length change.","D":"Isotonic = constant resistance","E":"Core stability targets low bac"}},{"id":"sdl23_q03","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which therapeutic exercise type is most specifically indicated for a patient with patellofemoral pain syndrome when joint range of motion is limited or contraindicated?","choices":{"A":"Isotonic exercise using constant resistance through full range of motion is most appropriate when joint mobility must be preserved and the goal is general muscle conditioning for the entire lower extremity","B":"Open kinetic chain exercise is most appropriate when joint mobility is limited because the free distal segment reduces compressive forces at the patellofemoral interface and allows strengthening without joint stress","C":"Isometric exercise contracts the muscle at constant length without joint movement, making it ideal when joint mobility is restricted; quadriceps isometrics are specifically recommended for patellofemoral pain syndrome","D":"Core stability exercise is most appropriate for patients with patellofemoral joint mobility limitations because abdominal bracing reduces compressive forces on peripheral joints and enables safe supervised exercise","E":"Closed kinetic chain exercise is specifically recommended for patellofemoral syndrome because weight-bearing joint co-contraction reduces shear forces and maintains mobility through progressive loading of the articular surface"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Isometric exercise = muscle contracts without changing length or moving the joint (e.g., holding a weight in a stationary position, quad sets). Specifically indicated when joint mobility is contraindicated. Quadriceps isometrics are first-line for patellofemoral pain syndrome. Isotonic = constant resistance through ROM (general conditioning). OKC = distal segment free (ADL function). CKC = dynamic stability for shoulder/knee rehab.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Isometric quad exercises are specifically recommended for patellofemoral pain syndrome — the lack of joint movement avoids provocative arc loading. COMLEX: When prescribing PT for patellofemoral syndrome, isometrics bridge the gap between immobility and full loading; OMM addressing patellar tracking and hip somatic dysfunction complements isometric strengthening.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL injuries: therapeutic exercise types are central to conservative management and structured rehabilitation protocols","whyNot":{"E":"CKC = dynamic stability for sh"}},{"id":"sdl23_q04","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the primary mechanism by which pulsed (nonthermal) therapeutic ultrasound promotes tissue healing, as distinguished from the thermal effects of continuous ultrasound?","choices":{"A":"Pulsed ultrasound heats superficial soft tissues more efficiently than continuous ultrasound because intermittent energy delivery allows deeper penetration before thermal energy dissipates into surrounding structures","B":"Pulsed ultrasound stimulates type I collagen synthesis by directly activating fibroblast gene expression through nuclear membrane deformation, a mechanism unique to intermittent acoustic radiation not seen with continuous delivery","C":"Pulsed ultrasound generates stable cavitation bubbles that mechanically disrupt collagen cross-links in scar tissue, enabling remodeling that continuous ultrasound cannot achieve due to its lower average power output","D":"Pulsed ultrasound activates the gate control mechanism in peripheral nociceptors by creating rhythmic pressure waves that override pain signal transmission to the dorsal horn, providing analgesia without tissue healing effects","E":"Pulsed ultrasound produces nonthermal effects by altering membrane permeability, ionic concentration gradients, and cellular biochemical activity at the cellular level, accelerating tissue healing without generating significant heat"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Continuous ultrasound = thermal effect (heats tissue). Pulsed ultrasound = nonthermal effect (membrane permeability, ionic gradients, cellular biochemical activity) → accelerates healing at the cellular level. High-frequency US treats superficial tissue (patellar tendons); lower frequencies penetrate deeper (up to 5 cm) for deep muscle bruising/spasms/strains. Can cause burns or endothelial damage if applied incorrectly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know thermal vs nonthermal US — continuous = heat, pulsed = cellular healing. Ultrasound CI include pacemakers, malignant tumors, epiphyseal plates, thrombophlebitis, acute inflammation, and reproductive organs. COMLEX: Ultrasound is an adjunctive modality; it does not replace OMM or therapeutic exercise but complements soft tissue treatment in PT protocols.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain: PT modalities including ultrasound, TENS, and iontophoresis are widely applied in LBP management"},{"id":"sdl23_q05","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Phonophoresis uses ultrasound to deliver topical medications through the skin to deeper tissues. Which preparation is typically used with this modality, and for which conditions?","choices":{"A":"A corticosteroid or nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug, usually mixed in an aqueous base at 10 percent concentration and applied with ultrasound at 1 to 2 watts per cm2, for inflammatory conditions such as tendonitis, arthritis, and bursitis","B":"Methotrexate 2.5% solution is the most commonly used medication in phonophoresis, delivered into inflamed synovium to suppress local lymphocyte proliferation and provide disease-modifying effects in inflammatory arthritis","C":"Botulinum toxin type A solution is the most commonly used medication in phonophoresis, driven into skeletal muscle to block acetylcholine release and relieve spasm and myofascial trigger point pain in chronic conditions","D":"Hyaluronic acid 0.5% solution is the most commonly used medication in phonophoresis, delivered into periarticular tissues to augment synovial fluid viscosity and provide viscoelastic cushioning comparable to intra-articular hyaluronate injection","E":"Capsaicin 0.025% cream is the most commonly used medication in phonophoresis, delivered through the dermis to deplete substance P in peripheral nociceptors and provide long-lasting analgesia for chronic musculoskeletal pain syndromes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Phonophoresis uses ultrasound to drive topical medication through the skin to deeper tissues. Per the course reading (Rand et al., Am Fam Physician 2007), a corticosteroid or NSAID is usually mixed in an aqueous base at a 10 percent concentration and applied with ultrasound at 1 to 2 W/cm2, for inflammatory conditions such as tendonitis, arthritis, and bursitis; ketoprofen tissue levels rise with phonophoresis compared with topical application alone. Dexamethasone 0.4% solution is the most commonly prescribed iontophoresis drug, not the phonophoresis preparation. Methotrexate, botulinum toxin, hyaluronic acid, and capsaicin are not the described phonophoresis preparations. The risk of thermal injury increases with the energy applied, and the contraindications are the same as for ultrasound.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Phonophoresis = ultrasound + corticosteroid/NSAID (10% in an aqueous base, 1-2 W/cm2) for tendonitis, arthritis, bursitis. Iontophoresis = electric current + ionic drug (dexamethasone 0.4% most common). Contraindications for phonophoresis are the same as for ultrasound. COMLEX: Phonophoresis is a common adjunct in PT for inflammatory tendinopathies that may also benefit from OMM soft-tissue techniques targeting the same regions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain: PT modalities including ultrasound, TENS, and iontophoresis are widely applied in LBP management","whyNot":{"B":"Methotrexate, botulinum toxin,","C":"Methotrexate, botulinum toxin,","D":"Methotrexate, botulinum toxin,","E":"Methotrexate, botulinum toxin,"}},{"id":"sdl23_q06","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In which patient group is electrical stimulation absolutely contraindicated, and what is the physiological rationale for this restriction?","choices":{"A":"Electrical stimulation is contraindicated in patients with any metal implant anywhere in the body because electrical current concentrates at all metallic surfaces and causes resistive heating that leads to thermal injury of surrounding bone and soft tissue","B":"Electrical stimulation is contraindicated in patients with cardiac pacemakers and known cardiac arrhythmias because the external current can interfere with pacemaker sensing circuits, trigger arrhythmias, or compete with paced impulses","C":"Electrical stimulation is contraindicated in patients with peripheral neuropathy because absent protective sensation prevents patients from reporting excessive current intensity, leading to inadvertent burns and electrode-related skin breakdown","D":"Electrical stimulation is contraindicated in patients with osteoporosis because the muscle contractions generated by neuromuscular electrical stimulation create excessive compressive loads on fragile trabecular bone and may cause pathologic fractures","E":"Electrical stimulation is contraindicated in patients with inflammatory arthritis because the electrical current activates synovial mast cells and fibroblasts, increasing prostaglandin and cytokine production and worsening joint inflammation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Electrical stimulation absolute CI: cardiac pacemakers, known cardiac arrhythmias, thrombophlebitis, and abdomen/pelvis of pregnant patients. Near metallic implants is a precaution (not absolute CI for all metals — CI specifically near metallic implants/wires/staples per iontophoresis; electrical stimulation is used with caution in patients with cardiac disease). Mechanism of ES analgesia: gate control + endogenous opioid/cortisol release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Electrical stimulation CI = cardiac pacemakers, arrhythmias, thrombophlebitis, pregnant abdomen. TENS mechanism: gate control theory + endogenous opioid release. COMLEX: TENS for LBP and neuropathic pain is commonly tested; OMM is often combined with TENS in multimodal pain management protocols for chronic low back pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain: PT modalities including ultrasound, TENS, and iontophoresis are widely applied in LBP management","whyNot":{"A":"Near metallic implants is a pr"}},{"id":"sdl23_q07","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following is a required component of a valid physical therapy prescription according to standard prescribing guidelines?","choices":{"A":"A completed insurance pre-authorization form signed by both the ordering physician and the physical therapist is required before initiating any PT intervention because payers mandate prospective coverage review for all outpatient rehabilitation services","B":"An objective functional assessment score such as the Oswestry Disability Index or DASH questionnaire is required on the PT prescription to establish a documented baseline for measuring therapeutic progress and justifying continuation of care","C":"A list of all current medications including dosage, frequency, and prescribing physician is required on the PT prescription so the therapist can screen for pharmacological agents that may alter pain perception, muscle tone, or cardiovascular response","D":"A radiology report confirming musculoskeletal pathology via X-ray or MRI is required before a PT prescription is valid, ensuring the therapist treats a documented structural abnormality rather than subjective symptoms without imaging confirmation","E":"A physician signature and date are required for a physical therapist to perform requested services; the prescription must also include the diagnosis, frequency and duration, specific protocols or treatments, and any safety precautions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Required components of a PT prescription (Table 3 from SDL): 1. Diagnosis (with proper coding for insurance billing), 2. Frequency and duration of therapy, 3. Specific protocols or treatments, 4. Safety precautions (ROM limitations, weight-bearing limits, comorbidities), 5. Physician signature and date (required for therapist to perform services). Goals are also a standard part. Functional scores, pre-auth forms, imaging, and medication lists are not required prescription components.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PT prescription components = Diagnosis, Goals, Frequency/Duration, Specific treatments, Safety precautions, Physician signature/date. Missing any required component invalidates the prescription. COMLEX: Prescribing PT is a core competency — COMLEX tests whether students can write complete PT orders that include all mandated elements for musculoskeletal conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Shoulder diagnostic workflow: PT prescription is a core management step integrating diagnosis, frequency, and safety precautions","whyNot":{"A":"Functional scores, pre-auth fo","B":"Functional scores, pre-auth fo","C":"Functional scores, pre-auth fo","D":"Functional scores, pre-auth fo"}},{"id":"sdl23_q08","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician writes a PT prescription for knee osteoarthritis that includes the diagnosis, frequency, specific exercises, and physician signature. The physical therapist returns it as incomplete. Which required element is most likely missing?","choices":{"A":"Safety precautions are a required prescription component, including weight-bearing limitations, range-of-motion restrictions, and comorbidities affecting therapy; without them the therapist cannot safely set exercise intensity","B":"The patient's insurance policy number and prior authorization code are the missing required elements, without which the physical therapy clinic cannot bill for services or begin treatment under federal outpatient therapy billing regulations","C":"Documentation of prior conservative treatment attempts is the missing required element because payers require evidence that home-based exercise was tried and failed before approving supervised physical therapy for knee osteoarthritis management","D":"The specific brand name of exercise equipment to be used is the missing required element because physical therapists are legally required to use physician-specified equipment for all therapeutic exercise prescriptions for musculoskeletal conditions","E":"The expected discharge functional status stated in measurable terms such as anticipated walking distance or stair-climbing ability is the missing required element required by federal guidelines for establishing an end-point for care"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A complete PT prescription includes: diagnosis, frequency/duration, specific protocols/treatments, safety precautions, and physician signature/date. In this case the prescription includes diagnosis, frequency, exercises, and signature — so the most likely missing element is safety precautions (weight-bearing limits, ROM restrictions, comorbidities affecting therapy decisions). These are critical for post-surgical, elderly, or high-risk patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Safety precautions are a required PT prescription element — especially critical for post-operative and high-risk patients. They inform the therapist about weight-bearing status, ROM restrictions, and clinical conditions affecting exercise prescription. COMLEX: Incomplete prescriptions are a legal liability; learn all required components to write a valid, actionable PT order.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Shoulder diagnostic workflow: PT prescription is a core management step integrating diagnosis, frequency, and safety precautions"},{"id":"sdl23_q09","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Based on the standard algorithm for selecting physical therapy interventions by phase of injury, which combination of modality, exercise, and frequency is most appropriate for a subacute musculoskeletal injury at 10 days post-injury?","choices":{"A":"In the subacute phase, cold application, pulsed ultrasound, and electrical stimulation are the preferred modalities combined with isometric and gentle active range-of-motion exercises with gentle massage, prescribed daily for five days","B":"In the subacute phase, transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation and continuous ultrasound are the preferred modalities combined with progressive strengthening and stabilization exercises with myofascial release, prescribed twice per week for four weeks","C":"In the subacute phase, heat application, low-level laser therapy, and iontophoresis are preferred modalities combined with isotonic and active range-of-motion exercises and joint mobilization, prescribed three times per week for two weeks","D":"In the subacute phase, cryotherapy and compression with neuromuscular electrical stimulation are the preferred modalities combined with eccentric loading and concentric strengthening with soft tissue mobilization, prescribed four times per week for one week","E":"In the subacute phase, phonophoresis and interferential electrical stimulation are the preferred modalities combined with core stability and closed kinetic chain exercises with passive stretching only, prescribed five times per week for two weeks"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Injury phase algorithm: Acute (<72h) = cold, electrical stimulation, pulsed ultrasound; isometric, gentle ROM; gentle massage; daily x5 days. Subacute (3-14 days) = heat, electrical stimulation, low-level laser, iontophoresis; isotonic, active ROM, stretching; massage, joint mobilization; 3x/week x2 weeks. Chronic (>2 weeks) = TENS, continuous ultrasound; strengthening, stabilization; myofascial release; 2x/week x4 weeks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the three injury phases and their corresponding modalities, exercises, and dosing: acute = cold/isometric/daily; subacute = heat/isotonic/3x/week; chronic = TENS/strengthening/2x/week. COMLEX: Prescribing PT by injury phase is a core clinical skill — COMLEX tests appropriate modality and exercise selection based on the timeline of musculoskeletal injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Shoulder diagnostic workflow: PT prescription is a core management step integrating diagnosis, frequency, and safety precautions"},{"id":"sdl23_q10","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old female competitive runner presents with 4 months of posterior heel pain. Imaging confirms Achilles tendinopathy without tear. She has failed rest and passive stretching. Which type of therapeutic exercise has the strongest evidence for promoting tendon collagen remodeling and should anchor her rehabilitation program?","choices":{"A":"Isometric exercise, in which muscle contraction occurs without any change in length, has the strongest evidence for tendinopathy because static loading at therapeutic angles reduces cortical pain inhibition without creating the shear forces associated with dynamic loading","B":"Open kinetic chain exercise, in which the distal segment is free, has the strongest evidence for tendinopathy because the absence of weight-bearing compressive forces on the inflamed tendon allows strengthening while maintaining cardiovascular fitness","C":"Core stability exercise targeting the lumbopelvic region has the strongest evidence for Achilles tendinopathy because improving proximal control reduces excessive ground reaction force transmission and indirectly offloads the distal tendon","D":"Eccentric exercise, in which the muscle contracts while lengthening, has the strongest evidence for treating tendinopathy; eccentric heel drops load the Achilles tendon during the lengthening phase, stimulating collagen synthesis and tendon remodeling as first-line exercise therapy","E":"Isotonic exercise applying constant resistance through full range of motion in both concentric and eccentric phases has the strongest evidence for tendinopathy because it stimulates the highest absolute rate of tendon collagen cross-linking per session"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Eccentric exercise is the exercise type with the strongest evidence for tendinopathy rehabilitation. The controlled lengthening contraction (e.g., Alfredson protocol: eccentric heel drops for Achilles) stimulates collagen synthesis and remodeling. Isometric exercise may reduce acute pain but does not drive tendon structural change as effectively. OKC reduces load but lacks the mechanotransduction signal needed for tendon healing. Core stability is complementary, not primary.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Eccentric exercise = first-line for tendinopathy; Alfredson protocol (eccentric heel drops) is classic for Achilles. COMLEX: Tendinopathy rehabilitation relies on controlled progressive loading — eccentric protocols are complemented by OMM soft-tissue techniques to address fascial restrictions contributing to mechanical load on the tendon.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: eccentric heel drops are first-line exercise therapy and the cornerstone of conservative management","whyNot":{"A":"Isometric exercise may reduce ","B":"OKC reduces load but lacks the","C":"Core stability is complementar"}},{"id":"sdl23_q11","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old male undergoes ACL reconstruction with a patellar tendon autograft. The physical therapist plans to use closed kinetic chain (CKC) exercises preferentially over open kinetic chain (OKC) exercises in the early post-operative period. Which biomechanical feature of CKC exercise justifies this preference?","choices":{"A":"In closed kinetic chain exercise, the fixed distal segment promotes co-contraction of agonist and antagonist muscles, enhancing joint stability and reducing anterior tibial shear on the healing graft compared with quadriceps-isolating open chain exercise","B":"Closed kinetic chain exercise is preferred post-ACL because it isolates the quadriceps without engaging the hamstrings, which reduces compressive load from antagonist co-contraction and prevents anterior drawer forces at the tibiofemoral joint","C":"Closed kinetic chain exercise is preferred post-ACL because it allows high-velocity ballistic movements that accelerate neuromuscular re-education and restore proprioception faster than the slower controlled movements characteristic of open kinetic chain protocols","D":"Closed kinetic chain exercise is preferred post-ACL because it loads the graft exclusively through concentric contraction, avoiding eccentric lengthening forces that cause repetitive microtrauma and graft elongation during early healing","E":"Closed kinetic chain exercise is preferred post-ACL because the distal segment moves freely about the proximal segment, which reduces the bending moment at the knee and distributes load evenly across the healing graft and native structures"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CKC = distal segment fixed, proximal moves (leg press, squat, lunges). Co-contraction of quads + hamstrings in CKC reduces anterior tibial shear (and therefore graft stress) compared to OKC quad-isolated exercises (e.g., leg extension). This is why CKC is the foundation of early ACL rehab. OKC exercises are reintroduced later in rehab when the graft is more mature. The option describing CKC with a free distal segment is incorrect — that defines OKC.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CKC = distal fixed, proximal moves; co-contraction protects ACL graft. OKC = distal free (leg extension) — delayed in ACL rehab due to anterior tibial shear. COMLEX: Post-surgical PT prescriptions should specify CKC vs OKC progression — COMLEX tests appropriate exercise selection in post-operative rehabilitation scenarios.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL injuries: CKC exercises are the foundation of post-operative ACL rehabilitation due to co-contraction-mediated joint stability","whyNot":{"E":"The option describing CKC with"}},{"id":"sdl23_q12","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old sedentary office worker presents with chronic non-specific low back pain without neurological deficits or red flags. Her physician wants to prescribe a therapeutic exercise category specifically shown to reduce low back pain symptoms. Which exercise classification is most directly targeted at her primary needs?","choices":{"A":"Closed kinetic chain exercises in weight-bearing positions such as squats and leg presses strengthen the paraspinal muscles that support the lumbar vertebral column and are most specifically indicated for non-specific low back pain in sedentary adults","B":"Open kinetic chain exercises activating free trunk movement in space are most specifically indicated because they engage the deep multifidus stabilizers more effectively than weight-bearing exercises that primarily recruit superficial global movers of the spine","C":"Eccentric exercises involving controlled lengthening contractions of the erector spinae and multifidus during forward bending are the most efficient mechanism for achieving lumbar stabilization in deconditioned sedentary patients with chronic low back pain","D":"Isometric paraspinal contractions without joint movement are the safest approach for deconditioned sedentary adults and produce the highest absolute strength gains in the lumbar extensors per session without risk of exacerbation","E":"Core stability exercises targeting the low back, trunk, and abdominal muscles through sit-ups, back extension, abdominal crunches, and Pilates are specifically indicated for the relief of low back pain and pregnancy-related pelvic pain"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Core stability exercises (sit-ups, back extension, abdominal crunch, Pilates) specifically target the low back, trunk, and abdominal muscles and are the exercise category indicated for low back pain and pregnancy-related pelvic pain per Table 2. CKC is for shoulder/knee rehab and dynamic stability, not specifically LBP. Eccentric is for tendinopathy/sport strengthening. Isometric is for when joint mobility is restricted. OKC is for ADL functional improvement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Core stability = specific indication for low back pain and pregnancy-related pelvic pain (sit-up, back extension, Pilates). CKC = shoulder/knee rehab. Isometric = patellofemoral or restricted mobility. COMLEX: Core stability exercises are prescribed alongside OMM for low back pain — both address the deep stabilizing musculature that is often inhibited in chronic LBP.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain: core stability exercises are the primary therapeutic exercise intervention for non-specific low back pain management","whyNot":{"A":"CKC is for shoulder/knee rehab","B":"OKC is for ADL functional impr","C":"Eccentric is for tendinopathy/","D":"Isometric is for when joint mo"}},{"id":"sdl23_q13","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physical therapist plans to treat a patient's calcific shoulder tendinopathy with iontophoresis using acetic acid. A medical student asks how iontophoresis differs from phonophoresis as a drug delivery mechanism. Which comparison is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Phonophoresis uses a direct electric current to deliver charged anti-inflammatory molecules into the joint, while iontophoresis uses high-frequency sound waves to heat the medication and increase its thermal diffusion rate into periarticular tissues","B":"Phonophoresis and iontophoresis use identical delivery mechanisms based on sonic vibration, differing only in the frequency range: phonophoresis operates at audible frequencies while iontophoresis operates at ultrasonic frequencies above 20 kHz","C":"Phonophoresis uses ultrasound waves to push topical medications through the skin into deeper tissue, while iontophoresis uses direct current to drive ionically charged substances, with cathodes repelling negative drugs and anodes repelling positive drugs","D":"Phonophoresis delivers medications exclusively into the superficial epidermis, while iontophoresis penetrates all the way to bone and joint capsule because the electrical gradient overcomes resistance of all tissue layers simultaneously and without limitation","E":"Phonophoresis and iontophoresis are equally effective for any musculoskeletal condition, and the clinical distinction is only whether the medication is formulated as a topical gel or as an aqueous ionic solution compatible with the delivery device"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Phonophoresis = ultrasound used to drive topical corticosteroid/NSAID through skin. Iontophoresis = direct electric current used to drive ionically charged substances through skin to deeper tissue. Cathode delivers negatively charged substances; anode delivers positively charged substances. Iontophoresis uses: calcific tendinopathy, inflammatory conditions, hyperhidrosis. CI for iontophoresis: allergy to applied substance, open wounds, decreased sensation, near metallic implants/wires/staples.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Phonophoresis = ultrasound + corticosteroid/NSAID (10%). Iontophoresis = current + ionic drug (dexamethasone 0.4% most common; acetic acid for calcific deposits). Cathode = negatively charged; anode = positively charged. COMLEX: Both modalities deliver anti-inflammatory agents; iontophoresis is specifically indicated for calcific tendinopathy and hyperhidrosis in addition to general inflammatory conditions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — De Quervain's tenosynovitis: phonophoresis and iontophoresis are commonly applied PT modalities for tenosynovitis and inflammatory tendon conditions"},{"id":"sdl23_q14","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old male with chronic low back pain reports significant pain relief from transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS) during his PT sessions. His physician asks the therapist to explain the mechanism of TENS analgesia. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"TENS reduces pain by generating microcurrents that directly reverse depolarization of peripheral nociceptor membranes, restoring resting membrane potential and preventing action potential generation in C-fibers and A-delta fibers","B":"TENS reduces pain by delivering an electrical field that disrupts substance P binding to NK-1 receptors in the dorsal horn, preventing central sensitization without engaging the descending inhibitory system or any endogenous analgesic pathway","C":"TENS reduces pain by activating sympathetic efferents that produce local vasoconstriction, reducing edema and lowering the concentration of algogenic chemicals such as bradykinin and prostaglandins in the interstitial fluid around nociceptors","D":"TENS reduces pain by activating the gate control mechanism, in which large-diameter sensory fiber stimulation inhibits pain transmission from small C and A-delta fibers at the dorsal horn, and by releasing endogenous opioids and cortisol","E":"TENS reduces pain by generating a therapeutic electrical field that directly inactivates COX-2 in injured tissue, reducing local prostaglandin E2 concentration through the same pathway as NSAIDs but without systemic adverse effects"},"correct":"D","explanation":"TENS analgesia mechanisms: 1) Gate control theory — large A-beta fiber stimulation inhibits C-fiber and A-delta nociceptive transmission at the dorsal horn interneurons; 2) Stimulates endogenous opioid and cortisol release. Electrical stimulation also reduces inflammation and edema and facilitates chronic wound healing. This is distinct from phonophoresis (drug delivery) or ultrasound (tissue heating/healing).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TENS mechanism = gate control (large fiber inhibits small fiber at dorsal horn) + endogenous opioid/cortisol release. TENS is used for chronic LBP, neuropathic pain, and TENS is applied in the chronic phase of injury. COMLEX: TENS is used alongside OMM for chronic musculoskeletal pain; understanding the gate control mechanism is testable for both Step 1 and COMLEX-1.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 8 — Lumbosacral spinal stenosis: electrical stimulation including TENS is a standard PT modality for managing chronic lumbar pain and neurogenic claudication"},{"id":"sdl23_q15","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with bilateral hand numbness and weakness, consistent with carpal tunnel syndrome, asks her physician whether low-level laser therapy is an option for her condition. Which statement most accurately characterizes the evidence base and regulatory status of LLLT?","choices":{"A":"LLLT is FDA approved for all musculoskeletal conditions including tendinopathy, osteoarthritis, and rheumatoid arthritis based on consistent level-1 randomized controlled trial evidence demonstrating clinically significant improvements in pain and joint function","B":"LLLT is FDA approved for carpal tunnel syndrome and minor musculoskeletal pain; Cochrane reviews show limited but inconsistent benefit for osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis, and LLLT has not been shown to cause adverse effects in standard use","C":"LLLT has no FDA approval for any musculoskeletal condition because randomized controlled trials have consistently demonstrated no benefit beyond sham therapy, and its use in clinical practice is considered experimental and generally discouraged","D":"LLLT is FDA approved specifically for rheumatoid arthritis because its mechanism of decreasing prostaglandin E2 production mimics the anti-inflammatory effect of NSAIDs without the associated gastrointestinal and cardiovascular adverse effects","E":"LLLT is contraindicated in all patients with musculoskeletal pain because photon radiation causes cumulative DNA damage in connective tissue cells, accelerating cartilage degeneration and ultimately worsening the conditions it is intended to treat"},"correct":"B","explanation":"LLLT mechanism: absorption of photon radiation → alters cellular oxidative metabolism (adenosine triphosphate production via cytochrome oxidase) → decreases prostaglandin E2 concentration in peritendinous fluid. FDA approved for carpal tunnel syndrome and minor MSK pain. Evidence for OA and RA is limited and conflicting per Cochrane reviews. Safety goggles are required; caution in patients with malignant tumors, anticoagulants, corticosteroids, immunosuppressives, and pregnant patients (not on uterus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: LLLT FDA approved for CTS and minor MSK pain. Mechanism: cytochrome oxidase activation → increased ATP, decreased PGE2. Evidence for OA/RA is limited and inconsistent. COMLEX: LLLT is a low-risk modality used in chronic MSK pain management — cautions include malignant tumors and anticoagulant therapy; safety goggles protect therapists and patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: low-level laser therapy is FDA approved for CTS management and is one of the PT modalities applied for this condition"},{"id":"sdl23_q16","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician writes a physical therapy referral for a 52-year-old woman: \"PT 3 times per week for 4 weeks; strengthening and stabilization exercises; no lifting over 10 pounds; goals: improve function and reduce pain. Signed and dated.\" The clinic's billing office returns it before the first visit. Which required component is missing, and why does it matter?","choices":{"A":"The therapist's own treatment plan, because a prescription is invalid until the therapist cosigns the physician's orders","B":"A list of every modality to be used, because the therapist may not choose interventions unless each one is named","C":"The diagnosis to be treated, with proper coding, which is needed for accurate insurance billing and reimbursement","D":"The patient's imaging results, because therapy cannot begin until radiographs or MRI are attached to the referral","E":"A follow-up date with the physician, because the prescription expires unless the next office visit is scheduled"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AAFP prescription article assigned for this SDL lists the components of a PT prescription: the diagnosis to be treated with proper coding for accurate insurance billing and reimbursement; frequency and duration; specific protocols or treatments; safety precautions; and the physician's signature and date. This referral includes frequency and duration, specific exercises, a safety restriction, goals, and a signature and date, but no diagnosis, so the billing office cannot code it. A therapist cosignature is not a listed component. Naming every modality is not required; forms often include an evaluate-and-treat option that lets the therapist use discretion. Imaging results and a scheduled follow-up date are not listed components.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PT prescription = diagnosis (coded), frequency and duration, specific protocols, safety precautions, signature and date; \"evaluate and treat\" lets the therapist choose interventions. COMLEX: Acute strains often get daily therapy for a short period; chronic problems get less frequent therapy over longer periods.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — physical therapy modalities chosen by phase of injury","whyNot":{"A":"A therapist cosignature is not"}},{"id":"sdl23_q17","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A family physician writes the following PT referral: \"Dx: right shoulder rotator cuff tendinopathy. Tx: therapeutic exercises and ultrasound. Signed, Dr. Smith, 4/28/26.\" The physical therapist calls requesting a critical addition before scheduling can begin. What required element is most likely missing from this prescription?","choices":{"A":"Frequency and duration of therapy are missing; without specifying how many sessions per week and the total treatment length the therapist cannot create a schedule, and the clinic cannot obtain payer authorization for a defined episode of care","B":"The patient's insurance co-payment information and referral number are missing; without prior verification of coverage details the physical therapy clinic cannot register the patient or confirm that the payer will reimburse for the prescribed services","C":"An MRI report confirming rotator cuff tendinopathy is missing; physical therapists are legally required to verify structural pathology via imaging before initiating therapeutic exercise for suspected rotator cuff conditions in any patient over 40","D":"Specific exercise names and repetition counts for each planned therapeutic intervention are missing; physical therapists are required to obtain physician approval for exact exercises rather than using clinical judgment to select appropriate interventions","E":"The patient's numeric pain rating at rest and with overhead activity is missing; federal billing guidelines require an objective pain score to be documented on every PT prescription before payers will authorize outpatient musculoskeletal rehabilitation services"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This prescription has: diagnosis (rotator cuff tendinopathy), specific treatments (exercises, ultrasound), and physician signature/date. The missing required component is frequency and duration of therapy (e.g., \"3x/week for 6 weeks\"). Without this, the therapist cannot schedule sessions appropriately or submit for insurance authorization. The other prescription elements present are adequate; insurance details, imaging, exact exercise names, and pain scores are not required prescription components.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PT prescription must include frequency and duration — this is distinct from just specifying the type of treatment. A complete prescription enables scheduling, insurance authorization, and a defined treatment episode. COMLEX: A common COMLEX stem presents an incomplete PT order and asks what is missing — frequency/duration and safety precautions are the most commonly omitted elements.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Shoulder rotator cuff injuries: PT prescription with complete frequency, duration, and safety precautions is the standard management step following rotator cuff tendinopathy diagnosis","whyNot":{"B":"The other prescription element","C":"The other prescription element","D":"The other prescription element","E":"The other prescription element"}},{"id":"sdl23_q18","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A family physician refers a 47-year-old man with chronic low back pain to physical therapy on the clinic's standard referral form. Instead of listing individual modalities, he checks the box marked \"evaluate and treat.\" Which statement best describes this option and the follow-up the physician should expect?","choices":{"A":"It hands prescribing to the therapist, so the referral no longer needs a diagnosis or the physician's signature and date before therapy begins","B":"It authorizes passive modalities such as heat and ultrasound, while any exercise program still has to be listed item by item on the form","C":"It covers one evaluation visit, after which the physician must write a second prescription that states the frequency and duration of therapy","D":"It lets the therapist choose the interventions, and the therapist reports progress to the physician, typically every 30 days or before follow-up","E":"It lets the therapist set the treatment plan, and progress is reported to the physician once, at discharge, unless the pain worsens during therapy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Rand et al. (Am Fam Physician 2007, the SDL 23 course reading) note that physical therapy clinics often provide standard forms, which usually include an \"evaluate and treat\" option that allows the therapist to use discretion in applying therapeutic interventions. The therapist documents the patient's progress so that the physician can modify the care plan if needed, and this documentation is typically given to the physician every 30 days or before the patient's follow-up visit. Checking the option does not remove the other components in Table 3 of the reading: the diagnosis (with proper coding) is still needed, and a physician signature and date are required for a therapist to perform the requested services. The reading does not limit the option to passive modalities or to a single evaluation visit, and progress reporting is ongoing rather than deferred to discharge.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PT prescription (Rand 2007, Table 3) = diagnosis with coding, frequency and duration, specific protocols or treatments, safety precautions, physician signature and date; \"evaluate and treat\" lets the therapist choose the interventions. COMLEX: Expect therapist progress documentation about every 30 days or before follow-up, and use it to adjust the care plan.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — components of a PT prescription (Table 3)","whyNot":{"B":"The reading does not limit the","E":"The reading does not limit the"}}],"25":[{"id":"sdl25_q01","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which structure is primarily disrupted in a dorsal proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joint dislocation, producing posterior displacement of the middle phalanx on the proximal phalanx?","choices":{"A":"Collateral ligament rupture alone without disruption of the palmar restraint structures, producing lateral instability rather than dorsal displacement of the middle phalanx","B":"Central extensor slip avulsion from the base of the middle phalanx, producing volar rather than dorsal displacement of the middle phalanx at the PIP joint","C":"Volar plate rupture from its distal attachment, allowing dorsal translation of the middle phalanx through the resulting gap in the palmar restraint system","D":"Flexor digitorum profundus tendon rupture from its distal phalanx insertion, producing DIP extension deformity rather than PIP joint displacement","E":"Dorsal capsule rupture and posterior ligamentous disruption, which would produce anterior rather than posterior displacement of the middle phalanx"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dorsal PIP dislocation is the most common finger dislocation. The mechanism is axial load combined with hyperextension, which ruptures the volar plate from its distal attachment to the middle phalanx, allowing dorsal translation. Collateral ligament rupture alone causes lateral instability. Central slip injury causes volar PIP dislocation (rare) and boutonnière risk. FDP rupture creates a jersey finger deformity at the DIP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Volar plate rupture = dorsal PIP dislocation (hyperextension); central slip injury = volar PIP dislocation → boutonnière if untreated. COMLEX: Post-reduction splint at 30° flexion for 3 weeks; somatic dysfunction of the hand may accompany high-energy digit injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — Joint stabilizers: volar plate and collateral ligament integrity at interphalangeal joints underlies dislocation pattern and deformity risk","whyNot":{"D":"FDP rupture creates a jersey f"}},{"id":"sdl25_q02","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"After closed reduction of a dorsal PIP joint dislocation with a confirmed volar plate avulsion on post-reduction X-ray, what is the appropriate immobilization position and duration?","choices":{"A":"Splint the PIP joint in full extension for 6 weeks, which places maximum tension on the healing volar plate and is appropriate only for central slip injuries requiring boutonnière prevention","B":"Splint the PIP joint at 30 degrees of flexion for 3 weeks, which maintains reduction while allowing the volar plate to heal in a shortened position and prevents swan neck deformity","C":"Buddy tape only to the adjacent finger with immediate active range of motion exercises, which does not adequately immobilize the ruptured volar plate during the critical healing period","D":"Immobilize in 90 degrees of PIP flexion for 4 weeks, which over-shortens the volar plate and produces a flexion contracture of the PIP joint as the primary complication","E":"Apply a dynamic extension splint immediately to restore full range of motion within 48 hours, bypassing the initial immobilization phase required for volar plate healing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Post-reduction management of dorsal PIP dislocation requires splinting at 30° flexion for 3 weeks to allow volar plate healing. Full extension splinting is reserved for central slip injuries (boutonnière prevention). Immediate motion without immobilization risks chronic volar plate laxity. 90° flexion causes a flexion contracture. The goal is healing without deformity — inadequate treatment leads to swan neck deformity from chronic volar plate laxity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dorsal PIP → volar plate → splint at 30° flexion 3 weeks; volar PIP → central slip → splint in full extension. COMLEX: Untreated volar plate laxity → swan neck deformity (PIP hyperextension, DIP flexion); somatic dysfunction of MCP/PIP may accompany ligamentous injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — Joint stabilizers: volar plate and collateral ligament integrity at interphalangeal joints underlies dislocation pattern and deformity risk","whyNot":{"C":"Immediate motion without immob","D":"90° flexion causes a flexion c"}},{"id":"sdl25_q03","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with a previously untreated dorsal PIP dislocation presents 6 weeks later with PIP joint hyperextension and DIP joint flexion. Which deformity has developed from the untreated volar plate tear?","choices":{"A":"Swan neck deformity — chronic volar plate laxity at the PIP allows hyperextension, which increases tension on the oblique retinacular ligament and produces reciprocal DIP flexion","B":"Boutonnière deformity — central slip rupture at the PIP joint allows lateral bands to migrate volarly, producing PIP flexion and DIP hyperextension as the characteristic pattern","C":"Mallet finger deformity — terminal extensor tendon rupture at the DIP joint produces DIP flexion and cannot be extended actively, with no associated PIP deformity","D":"Trigger finger deformity — stenosing tenosynovitis at the A1 pulley produces a catching or locking finger in flexion that snaps into extension with passive manipulation","E":"Dupuytren contracture — palmar fascia fibrosis producing progressive flexion deformity at the MCP and PIP joints bilaterally, unrelated to acute ligamentous injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Swan neck deformity (PIP hyperextension + DIP flexion) develops from chronic volar plate laxity after untreated dorsal PIP dislocation. Boutonnière is the opposite pattern (PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension) from central slip rupture. Mallet finger is isolated DIP flexion from terminal extensor tendon avulsion. Trigger finger is A1 pulley stenosis. Dupuytren is palmar fascia fibrosis — not post-traumatic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Swan neck = PIP hyperextension + DIP flexion (volar plate laxity or FDS rupture); boutonnière = PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension (central slip rupture). COMLEX: Both deformities benefit from orthotic management and hand rehabilitation; OMM of the wrist/finger may address secondary somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — Joint stabilizers: volar plate and collateral ligament integrity at interphalangeal joints underlies dislocation pattern and deformity risk"},{"id":"sdl25_q04","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Elbow dislocations are the second most common major joint dislocation in adults. Which direction of dislocation is most common, and how is the elbow typically positioned by the patient?","choices":{"A":"Anterior dislocation is most common; the patient holds the elbow in full extension with the arm appearing elongated and the antecubital fossa showing a palpable humeral mass anteriorly","B":"Medial dislocation is most common; the patient holds the elbow at 90 degrees with the forearm deviated medially and the radial head palpable laterally through the skin","C":"Posterior or posterolateral dislocation is most common; the patient holds the elbow in partial flexion with the forearm appearing shortened and the olecranon prominent posteriorly","D":"Lateral dislocation is most common; the patient holds the elbow in supination with the forearm deviated laterally and the medial epicondyle palpable on the medial arm","E":"Rotatory dislocation is most common; the patient holds the elbow in a neutral rotation with clicking and locking that prevents full flexion or extension of the forearm"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Posterior and posterolateral dislocations account for the majority of elbow dislocations. The mechanism is a FOOSH (fall on outstretched hand) with the elbow in extension and a valgus stress. The olecranon is prominent posteriorly and the elbow is held in partial flexion. Anterior dislocations are rare and result from a direct posterior blow. Medial and lateral dislocations are uncommon isolated injuries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Elbow dislocation = second most common major joint dislocation (shoulder is first); posterior/posterolateral most common; terrible triad = dislocation + radial head fracture + coronoid fracture. COMLEX: Neurovascular check before and after reduction; somatic dysfunction of the elbow and forearm common after dislocation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve at elbow: posterior medial epicondyle course makes this nerve vulnerable in elbow dislocations and the terrible triad","whyNot":{"A":"Anterior dislocations are rare","B":"Medial and lateral dislocation","D":"Medial and lateral dislocation"}},{"id":"sdl25_q05","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The \"terrible triad\" of the elbow describes a combination of injuries that produces severe instability. Which combination of findings defines this injury pattern?","choices":{"A":"Elbow dislocation combined with olecranon fracture and avulsion of the medial epicondyle, creating a pattern that is best managed with immediate cast immobilization in 90 degrees of flexion","B":"Elbow dislocation combined with radial head fracture and coronoid process fracture, creating a triplanar instability that typically requires surgical fixation to restore elbow stability","C":"Elbow dislocation combined with distal humerus fracture and median nerve laceration, representing the classic high-energy pattern seen in pediatric supracondylar fractures with dislocation","D":"Elbow dislocation combined with radial shaft fracture and complete ulnar nerve transection, creating the typical low-energy injury seen in elderly patients with osteoporosis","E":"Elbow dislocation combined with lateral epicondyle avulsion and biceps tendon rupture, producing an anterolateral rotatory instability pattern requiring ligament reconstruction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The terrible triad consists of elbow dislocation + radial head fracture + coronoid process fracture. The coronoid provides the anterior buttress against posterior ulnar translation, and the radial head provides lateral column support. Loss of both creates triplanar instability that typically requires surgical fixation of both the coronoid and radial head, plus ligamentous repair. This combination is notoriously unstable.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Terrible triad = elbow dislocation + radial head fracture + coronoid fracture; coronoid is the key stabilizer against posterior translation — even small fragments matter. COMLEX: Ulnar nerve monitoring essential post-reduction; elbow somatic dysfunction and restricted ROM are common sequelae requiring OMM and PT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve at elbow: posterior medial epicondyle course makes this nerve vulnerable in elbow dislocations and the terrible triad"},{"id":"sdl25_q06","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which mechanism most commonly produces a posterior elbow dislocation in an adult?","choices":{"A":"Direct medial blow to the flexed elbow with the forearm in pronation, which applies a varus moment that drives the radial head medially and the olecranon anteriorly under the trochlea","B":"Forced hyperflexion of the elbow against an axial compressive load, which fails the posterior capsule and drives the humeral trochlea anteriorly through the coronoid fossa into the brachialis","C":"Repetitive valgus stress from overhead throwing mechanics, which gradually attenuates the ulnar collateral ligament and eventually allows posterior subluxation of the elbow with throwing","D":"Posterior blow to the olecranon with the elbow in 90 degrees of flexion, which is the typical mechanism of anterior elbow dislocation rather than the posterior pattern","E":"Fall on an outstretched hand with the elbow in extension and a valgus stress, which drives the olecranon posteriorly over the distal humerus as the anterior capsule fails under traction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Posterior elbow dislocation results from a FOOSH with the elbow in extension plus valgus stress. This combination axially loads the elbow while forcing the olecranon posteriorly, failing the anterior capsule and lateral collateral ligament complex. Hyperflexion with axial load would more likely fracture the olecranon. Repetitive valgus stress causes UCL attenuation (thrower's elbow) without dislocation. A direct posterior blow to the olecranon produces anterior dislocation (rare).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FOOSH + extension + valgus → posterior elbow dislocation; reduction by longitudinal traction on forearm with supination, then flexion under conscious sedation. COMLEX: Post-reduction early mobilization to prevent elbow stiffness (most common complication); assess for heterotopic ossification in high-energy injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve at elbow: posterior medial epicondyle course makes this nerve vulnerable in elbow dislocations and the terrible triad","whyNot":{"B":"Hyperflexion with axial load w","C":"Repetitive valgus stress cause","D":"A direct posterior blow to the"}},{"id":"sdl25_q07","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old athlete sustains recurrent anterior glenohumeral dislocations. MRI shows an impaction fracture on the posterolateral humeral head. What is this lesion, and what causes it?","choices":{"A":"Hill-Sachs lesion — a compression fracture of the posterolateral humeral head created by the sharp anteroinferior glenoid rim as the humeral head dislocates anteriorly over it during dislocation","B":"Bankart lesion — an anteroinferior capsular tear with avulsion of the glenoid labrum from the anteroinferior glenoid rim, representing the soft-tissue restraint failure rather than a bony humeral lesion","C":"Reverse Hill-Sachs lesion — a compression fracture of the anterior humeral head created in posterior dislocations when the posterior glenoid rim impacts the humeral head from behind","D":"Greater tuberosity avulsion — traction failure of the supraspinatus and infraspinatus insertions from the greater tuberosity during reduction maneuvers in elderly patients over 40 years of age","E":"SLAP lesion — a tear of the superior labrum extending from anterior to posterior at the biceps anchor, resulting from repetitive overhead traction rather than acute traumatic dislocation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Hill-Sachs lesion is a posterolateral humeral head impaction fracture caused by the anterior glenoid rim during anterior dislocation. The Bankart lesion is the glenoid labrum tear — the soft-tissue equivalent. Reverse Hill-Sachs (McLaughlin lesion) occurs in posterior dislocations — anterior humeral head impaction on the posterior glenoid. Greater tuberosity avulsion occurs in patients >40 years from rotator cuff traction. SLAP lesions are overhead athlete injuries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hill-Sachs = posterolateral humeral head (anterior dislocation); reverse Hill-Sachs = anterior humeral head (posterior dislocation); both Hill-Sachs and Bankart predispose to recurrent dislocation when large. COMLEX: Apprehension test detects anterior instability; relocation test (posterior pressure) relieves symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff integrity: disrupted in anterior shoulder dislocation in patients over 40, contributing to glenohumeral instability","whyNot":{"E":"SLAP lesions are overhead athl"}},{"id":"sdl25_q08","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which nerve is most commonly injured in anterior glenohumeral dislocation, and what is the classic sensory finding used to assess its function?","choices":{"A":"Radial nerve — sensory loss over the dorsal forearm and hand with wrist drop confirms injury; the radial nerve is the most vulnerable structure during reduction maneuvers at the elbow","B":"Musculocutaneous nerve — sensory loss over the lateral forearm (lateral antebrachial cutaneous) with loss of elbow flexion and forearm supination confirms injury after dislocation","C":"Median nerve — sensory loss over the thenar eminence and radial 3.5 digits with hand of benediction deformity on attempted fist closure confirms median nerve injury at the shoulder","D":"Axillary nerve — sensory loss over the proximal lateral aspect of the arm (regimental badge area) confirms injury; the deltoid extension lag sign assesses motor function","E":"Suprascapular nerve — motor loss of supraspinatus and infraspinatus with empty can test weakness and external rotation deficit confirms suprascapular nerve injury after dislocation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The axillary nerve courses around the surgical neck of the humerus and through the quadrilateral space, making it the nerve most vulnerable in anterior glenohumeral dislocation. Sensory loss over the lateral deltoid (regimental badge area) is the classic finding. The deltoid extension lag test assesses motor function — the shoulder is passively extended and the patient cannot hold the position if the axillary nerve is injured. Radial nerve injury occurs with midshaft humerus fractures, not shoulder dislocations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Axillary nerve = most common nerve injured in anterior shoulder dislocation; sensory = lateral deltoid (regimental badge); motor = deltoid (abduction) and teres minor (external rotation). COMLEX: Assess axillary nerve BEFORE and AFTER reduction; dead arm syndrome is a transient variant from brief axillary nerve compression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff integrity: disrupted in anterior shoulder dislocation in patients over 40, contributing to glenohumeral instability","whyNot":{"A":"Radial nerve injury occurs wit"}},{"id":"sdl25_q09","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient sustains a posterior glenohumeral dislocation and undergoes successful closed reduction. In what position should the shoulder be immobilized, and why?","choices":{"A":"Internal rotation in a standard sling for 2 to 6 weeks, which is appropriate for anterior dislocations where external rotation is the apprehension position but would recreate the vulnerable position for posterior dislocations","B":"Full abduction at 90 degrees in a shoulder spica cast for 4 weeks, which is an outdated approach previously used for all shoulder dislocations but not specific to directional instability patterns","C":"External rotation with a small amount of abduction for 2 to 6 weeks, because this keeps the humeral head seated against the posterior glenoid and the arm out of the internally rotated position that caused the dislocation","D":"Neutral rotation with the elbow at 90 degrees in a sling for 3 weeks, which provides no directional preference and fails to tighten the posterior capsule against re-dislocation in the posterior direction","E":"Flexion at 90 degrees with internal rotation in a body harness for 6 weeks, which is the position used for acromioclavicular separations and is inappropriate for posterior glenohumeral instability"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Posterior dislocation is immobilized in external rotation (+ slight abduction) for 2-6 weeks. This is opposite to anterior dislocation management (sling in internal rotation). External rotation keeps the humeral head seated against the posterior glenoid while the torn posterior capsule heals, and keeps the arm out of the vulnerable position of internal rotation and forward flexion (it is internal rotation that tensions the posterior capsule). Immobilizing in internal rotation recreates the injury position. Posterior dislocation is classically associated with seizures, lightning strike, and electrocution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior dislocation → arm in internal rotation, cannot externally rotate; AP x-ray may look normal; axillary lateral view confirms; immobilize in EXTERNAL rotation. COMLEX: Reverse Hill-Sachs (anterior humeral head) is the bony lesion; suspect posterior dislocation in any patient after seizure with shoulder pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff integrity: disrupted in anterior shoulder dislocation in patients over 40, contributing to glenohumeral instability","whyNot":{"A":"Immobilizing in internal rotat"}},{"id":"sdl25_q10","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Traumatic femoroacetabular dislocations most commonly occur in which direction, and in what characteristic position is the limb held?","choices":{"A":"Posterior dislocation accounts for approximately 85% of cases; the hip is held in flexion, adduction, and internal rotation with apparent limb shortening","B":"Anterior dislocation is most common at 65% of cases; the hip is held in extension, abduction, and external rotation with the femoral head palpable in the inguinal region or over the obturator foramen","C":"Central dislocation is the most common pattern; the femoral head is driven medially through the acetabular floor by axial loading, with no characteristic limb position as the femur remains in neutral","D":"Superior dislocation occurs most commonly in high-energy trauma; the limb is held in abduction with the greater trochanter riding superiorly above the acetabular rim on plain radiograph","E":"Inferior dislocation is most common in lower extremity compression injuries; the limb is held in abduction with the femoral head palpable in the perineum and the sciatic nerve stretched superiorly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Posterior hip dislocation accounts for approximately 85% of cases. The classic mechanism is a posteriorly directed force on the knee with the hip flexed — the \"dashboard injury\" in MVCs. The limb is held in flexion, adduction, and internal rotation. Anterior dislocation (15%) produces the opposite — extension, abduction, external rotation — with the femoral head near the obturator foramen or femoral triangle. Central dislocation involves acetabular fracture with medial femoral head displacement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior hip dislocation = flexion/adduction/internal rotation; MVC dashboard mechanism; reduce as soon as possible, preferably within the first few hours, to minimize AVN risk. COMLEX: Posterior hip dislocation = CP8 named condition; sciatic nerve injury (10-20%); somatic dysfunction of the pelvis and sacrum common after high-energy hip trauma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Hip dislocation: femoroacetabular instability, AVN risk, and sciatic nerve proximity established in hip trauma framework","whyNot":{"C":"Central dislocation involves a"}},{"id":"sdl25_q11","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which factor most significantly increases the risk of avascular necrosis (AVN) of the femoral head following posterior hip dislocation?","choices":{"A":"Anterior direction of hip dislocation increases AVN risk more than posterior direction, because the medial circumflex femoral artery is more directly compressed during anterior displacement of the femoral head","B":"Patient age under 30 years is the strongest predictor of AVN because the femoral head blood supply is more dependent on the ligamentum teres in skeletally immature patients with open physes","C":"Delay in reduction — hips reduced more than 12 hours after injury have about 5.6 times the odds of AVN compared with those reduced within 12 hours, from prolonged ischemia of the retinacular vessels","D":"Use of closed rather than open reduction increases AVN risk significantly, because open reduction with direct visualization prevents additional vascular traction injury during the reduction maneuver","E":"Absence of an associated acetabular fracture paradoxically increases AVN risk because pure ligamentous dislocations require greater force to dislocate and cause more vascular stretch injury"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Time to reduction is the most critical modifiable factor for AVN prevention after hip dislocation. Hips reduced more than 12 hours after injury have about 5.6 times the odds of AVN compared with those reduced within 12 hours (course text), reflecting prolonged ischemia of the retinacular vessels (branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery) supplying the femoral head. Posterior dislocation has higher AVN risk than anterior. Closed reduction is preferred and does not increase AVN risk. Age and fracture pattern affect prognosis but are not the primary AVN determinant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AVN after hip dislocation = medial circumflex femoral artery injury; reduce as soon as possible (odds of AVN 5.6× higher after 12 hours); AVN may not manifest clinically for 2 years post-injury — follow with serial MRI. COMLEX: Hip dislocation = CP8 named condition; post-reduction PT and protected weight-bearing protocols are essential rehabilitation components.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Hip dislocation: femoroacetabular instability, AVN risk, and sciatic nerve proximity established in hip trauma framework","whyNot":{"A":"Posterior dislocation has high","B":"Age and fracture pattern affec","D":"Closed reduction is preferred ","E":"Age and fracture pattern affec"}},{"id":"sdl25_q12","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman is injured when her extended, externally rotated hip is forced further outward in a high-speed fall while waterskiing. Which presentation is characteristic of the less common anterior type of hip dislocation?","choices":{"A":"The hip held flexed, adducted, and internally rotated with the limb shortened and the femoral head palpable in the buttock","B":"The hip held flexed and externally rotated with the leg lengthened and the greater trochanter displaced posteriorly","C":"The leg shortened and externally rotated with groin pain and an intact capsule, the pattern of a femoral neck fracture","D":"A normal resting position with pain on internal rotation, since anterior dislocations reduce spontaneously","E":"The hip held extended and externally rotated, with the femoral head palpable anteriorly below the inguinal crease"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The Current Diagnosis and Treatment in Orthopedics chapter for this SDL states that anterior hip dislocations account for 10 to 15% of hip dislocations and occur when the hip is extended and externally rotated at impact. The hip is classically held extended and externally rotated, and the femoral head is palpable anteriorly below the inguinal flexion crease. The head may lie beneath the obturator externus (obturator dislocation) or under the iliopsoas against the superior pubic ramus (pubic dislocation). Flexion, adduction, internal rotation, and shortening describe the 85% posterior type. A flexed, externally rotated, lengthened limb is not the described pattern. Shortening with external rotation and groin pain after a fall describes a femoral neck fracture. Anterior dislocations do not reduce spontaneously; closed reduction, usually under general anesthesia, is required, followed by radiographs to confirm a concentric reduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip dislocation: posterior (85%; flexion, adduction, internal rotation; sciatic nerve; dashboard injury) vs anterior (10–15%; extension, external rotation; head palpable anteriorly). COMLEX: Reduce promptly; AVN odds rise about 5.6-fold when reduction is delayed beyond 12 hours.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — hip fracture and dislocation presentation"},{"id":"sdl25_q13","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"After reduction of a traumatic knee dislocation, which ankle-brachial index (ABI) threshold mandates CT angiography to evaluate for popliteal artery injury?","choices":{"A":"An ABI below 0.5 is the only threshold requiring CT angiography, because ABIs between 0.5 and 0.9 represent mild flow reduction that can be managed with serial pulse checks every 4 hours","B":"An ABI below 1.2 compared to the contralateral limb requires CT angiography, because any reduction from the expected normal value of 1.0 to 1.2 indicates arterial injury requiring investigation","C":"An ABI below 0.9 is considered abnormal and mandates CT angiography, as even hard signs of vascular injury may be absent despite significant popliteal artery damage requiring surgical repair","D":"An ABI below 0.7 requires CT angiography, while ABIs between 0.7 and 0.9 can be managed conservatively with Doppler monitoring and repeat examination at 24 and 48 hours post-reduction","E":"Any ABI below 1.0 in the absolute sense requires CT angiography regardless of the degree of reduction, even though ABIs of 0.9 to 1.0 are commonly seen in normal limbs without vascular injury"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After knee dislocation, vascular assessment is critical because popliteal artery injury occurs in a significant proportion of cases. An ABI below 0.9 is abnormal and mandates CT angiography. Vascular status should be monitored up to 72 hours post-reduction because intimal injuries can cause delayed thrombosis even with initially normal pulses. Ischemia lasting more than 4 hours is associated with poor outcomes and amputation risk requiring urgent fasciotomy plus vascular repair.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee dislocation = popliteal artery injury (most important vascular complication); ABI <0.9 → CTA; monitor vascular status for 72 hours. COMLEX: Knee dislocation = CP8 named condition; fasciotomy + vascular repair for ischemia; multiligamentous injury requires surgical reconstruction in young active patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — Knee ligamentous injury: ACL/PCL/collateral disruption all occur in traumatic knee dislocations requiring extensive soft-tissue tearing"},{"id":"sdl25_q14","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"How are traumatic tibiofemoral dislocations classified, and why is this classification clinically important?","choices":{"A":"Classified by the direction of femoral displacement relative to the tibia, which determines whether the knee extensors or flexors are primarily involved and whether weight-bearing can begin immediately","B":"Classified by the degree of ligamentous tear on MRI (grade I through III), which directly determines surgical versus conservative management independent of the direction of dislocation","C":"Classified by the mechanism of injury (contact versus non-contact), which predicts the pattern of associated neurovascular injury and determines timing of surgical ligamentous reconstruction","D":"Classified by the direction of tibial displacement relative to the femur (anterior, posterior, lateral, medial, or rotatory), which guides ligamentous injury pattern and management strategy","E":"Classified by the associated meniscal injury pattern on arthroscopic examination, which is the primary determinant of long-term functional outcome and return to sport timeline after knee dislocation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Knee dislocations are classified by the direction of tibial displacement relative to the femur. This is clinically important because the direction helps anticipate which ligament complexes and neurovascular structures are injured and guides reduction and management. Complete dislocation can occur only after extensive tearing of the supporting ligaments and soft tissues (both cruciates are usually torn, with collateral involvement in lateral, medial, and rotatory patterns). Rotatory dislocations may be irreducible by closed means due to soft tissue interposition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee dislocation = classified by tibial displacement direction; all knee dislocations injure multiple ligaments; spontaneous reduction can occur before ED arrival — maintain high suspicion in multiligamentous instability. COMLEX: Knee dislocation = CP8 named condition; popliteal artery and common peroneal nerve at greatest risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — Knee ligamentous injury: ACL/PCL/collateral disruption all occur in traumatic knee dislocations requiring extensive soft-tissue tearing"},{"id":"sdl25_q15","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Following reduction of a traumatic knee dislocation, at what threshold of ischemia time is prognosis considered poor and amputation risk high?","choices":{"A":"Ischemia lasting more than 12 hours is the threshold for poor prognosis; the first 12 hours represent a safe window during which vascular repair consistently restores limb viability without fasciotomy","B":"Ischemia lasting more than 4 hours is associated with poor prognosis; irreversible muscle and nerve damage accelerates after this threshold, requiring urgent vascular repair with fasciotomy","C":"Ischemia lasting more than 8 hours is the definitive threshold; outcomes remain acceptable within 8 hours of injury regardless of the degree of vascular occlusion in the popliteal artery","D":"Any period of ischemia beyond 30 minutes universally results in amputation regardless of subsequent vascular repair, making fasciotomy and immediate OR the only acceptable management","E":"Ischemia lasting more than 24 hours is the clinical threshold used in current guidelines, as modern vascular and microsurgical techniques reliably restore perfusion within this window after knee dislocation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The 4-hour ischemia threshold is critical for knee dislocations. After 4 hours of ischemia, irreversible muscle and nerve damage accelerates sharply, dramatically worsening amputation risk. Management requires immediate surgical vascular repair plus fasciotomy to address compartment syndrome from reperfusion. The 4-hour threshold emphasizes the urgency of vascular evaluation after knee dislocation — even spontaneously reduced dislocations require emergent vascular assessment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Popliteal artery injury after knee dislocation + ischemia >4 hours = poor prognosis; vascular repair + fasciotomy are emergent; monitor vascular status for 72 hours post-reduction for delayed intimal tear thrombosis. COMLEX: Physical exam findings of progressive pallor, pulselessness, paresthesias, and pain with passive stretch signal compartment syndrome post-repair.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — Knee ligamentous injury: ACL/PCL/collateral disruption all occur in traumatic knee dislocations requiring extensive soft-tissue tearing"},{"id":"sdl25_q16","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Patellar dislocations almost always occur in which direction, and which anatomical feature explains this directional predilection?","choices":{"A":"Medial dislocation is most common because the lateral retinaculum is thicker and stronger than the medial retinaculum, making medial displacement the path of least resistance under forceful contraction","B":"Superior dislocation is most common from quadriceps tendon avulsion injuries, because the superior pole of the patella loses its restraining structure during forced extension against resistance","C":"Inferior dislocation is most common from patellar tendon rupture, because inferior pole displacement follows loss of the patellar tendon anchor during sudden deceleration or landing injuries","D":"Lateral dislocation is nearly universal because the Q-angle creates a net lateral force on the patella, and the medial patellofemoral ligament is the primary soft-tissue restraint resisting that force","E":"Posterior dislocation into the intercondylar notch is most common in young athletes, because the thin posterior patellar cartilage fails under compressive loading from direct impact during pivoting"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lateral patellar dislocation is almost universal due to the lateral force vector of the quadriceps pull angle (Q-angle). The medial patellofemoral ligament (MPFL) is the primary restraint against lateral displacement, and it tears in >50% of acute dislocations, most often at its femoral attachment. Predisposing factors include trochlear dysplasia (reduced bony constraint), patella alta, and excessive femoral anteversion. Spontaneous reduction occurs as the knee is extended, tightening the quadriceps.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Patellar dislocation = almost always lateral; MPFL is primary restraint; predisposing factors = trochlear dysplasia, patella alta, increased Q-angle. COMLEX: CP8 named condition; first-time dislocation → brace in extension 2-3 weeks + VMO strengthening; recurrent → MPFL reconstruction ± tibial tubercle transfer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral mechanics: trochlear dysplasia and quadriceps vector abnormalities predispose to lateral patellar instability and dislocation"},{"id":"sdl25_q17","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which ligament is torn in more than 50% of acute patellar dislocations, and at which attachment site does failure most commonly occur?","choices":{"A":"The lateral retinaculum is torn in more than 50% of cases; failure occurs at the patellar attachment and is the primary structure addressed surgically during lateral retinacular release procedures","B":"The medial patellofemoral ligament (MPFL) is torn in more than 50% of cases; failure most commonly occurs at the femoral attachment on the medial femoral condyle near the adductor tubercle","C":"The medial collateral ligament of the knee is torn in more than 50% of patellar dislocations; failure occurs at the tibial attachment and produces combined valgus and patellar instability","D":"The anterior cruciate ligament is torn in more than 50% of patellar dislocations because the same valgus mechanism that dislocates the patella also applies an anterior tibial shear force to the ACL","E":"The patellar tendon is disrupted in more than 50% of cases; failure occurs at the tibial tubercle insertion and produces superior patellar migration rather than lateral displacement at the trochlea"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The MPFL is the primary restraint against lateral patellar displacement and is torn in >50% of acute lateral patellar dislocations. The femoral attachment near the adductor tubercle is the most common failure site. MPFL reconstruction targeting this anatomical footprint is the basis of surgical treatment for recurrent instability. Lateral retinaculum release alone is no longer recommended as an isolated procedure. MCL and ACL injuries are associated with different mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MPFL = primary medial restraint of the patella; femoral attachment most commonly torn; MRI confirms injury location prior to reconstruction planning. COMLEX: Hemarthrosis after patellar dislocation suggests osteochondral injury; apprehension test (lateral patellar force) + medial patellar tenderness = classic exam findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral mechanics: trochlear dysplasia and quadriceps vector abnormalities predispose to lateral patellar instability and dislocation","whyNot":{"C":"MCL and ACL injuries are assoc","D":"MCL and ACL injuries are assoc"}},{"id":"sdl25_q18","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old athlete sustains a first-time lateral patellar dislocation that reduces spontaneously. X-ray shows no fracture. What is the initial management?","choices":{"A":"Brace or cylinder cast with the knee in extension for 2 to 3 weeks, followed by quadriceps strengthening focusing on the VMO, with operative management reserved for chronic or recurrent instability","B":"Immediate surgical MPFL repair is indicated for all first-time patellar dislocations in athletes to prevent recurrence, as conservative management has unacceptably high re-dislocation rates in young patients","C":"Closed reduction with splinting in 90 degrees of knee flexion for 6 weeks, which relaxes the quadriceps and allows the MPFL to heal in a shortened position suitable for return to sport","D":"Arthroscopic washout alone without immobilization, because hemarthrosis drainage is the primary treatment goal and early range of motion prevents arthrofibrosis after patellar dislocation","E":"Open patellar realignment surgery with tibial tubercle osteotomy for all first-time dislocations in patients with patella alta, regardless of TT-TG distance or degree of trochlear dysplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"First-time lateral patellar dislocation without fracture is managed conservatively: brace/cylinder cast in extension for 2-3 weeks, then quadriceps and VMO strengthening, and gradual return to activity. Operative management (MPFL reconstruction, tibial tubercle transfer) is reserved for recurrent dislocations or when structural risk factors (elevated TT-TG distance, severe trochlear dysplasia) warrant early surgical correction. Immobilization in flexion is incorrect — extension maintains patellar alignment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First-time patellar dislocation → conservative management (brace in extension, PT); recurrent → MPFL reconstruction; elevated TT-TG → consider tibial tubercle anteromedialization. COMLEX: VMO strengthening is the cornerstone of patellar rehabilitation; assess for somatic dysfunction at the knee and hip affecting quadriceps mechanics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral mechanics: trochlear dysplasia and quadriceps vector abnormalities predispose to lateral patellar instability and dislocation","whyNot":{"B":"Operative management (MPFL rec","C":"Immobilization in flexion is i"}},{"id":"sdl25_q19","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The distal tibiofibular syndesmosis is made up of four ligaments that hold the fibula against the tibia above the ankle joint. According to the course reading, which of these is the thickest and strongest?","choices":{"A":"The anterior talofibular ligament, which runs from the fibula to the talar neck","B":"The deltoid ligament, which fans from the medial malleolus to the talus","C":"The calcaneofibular ligament, which crosses the subtalar joint laterally","D":"The anterior inferior tibiofibular ligament, at the front of the ankle","E":"The posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament, at the level of the plafond"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The Current Diagnosis and Treatment in Orthopedics ankle section states that the syndesmotic ligament is composed of four ligaments and that the posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament is the thickest and strongest, connecting the tibia to the fibula at the level of the tibial plafond. The anterior inferior tibiofibular ligament is also syndesmotic but is not the strongest. The anterior talofibular and calcaneofibular ligaments are lateral collateral ligaments of the ankle, not syndesmotic ligaments. The deltoid ligament is the medial collateral complex. Syndesmotic injury follows external rotation and hyperdorsiflexion, a tibiofibular clear space over 6 mm is the most reliable radiographic sign, and anatomic reduction is the most important determinant of outcome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Syndesmosis = four ligaments, with the posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament the strongest; injury from external rotation + dorsiflexion (high ankle sprain); tibiofibular clear space >6 mm. COMLEX: Squeeze and external rotation tests screen for syndesmotic injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — syndesmotic (high ankle) sprain mechanism and examination","whyNot":{"D":"The anterior inferior tibiofib"}},{"id":"sdl25_q20","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"On a post-reduction mortise view following ankle dislocation, which radiographic finding is the most reliable indicator of syndesmotic injury requiring fixation?","choices":{"A":"Medial clear space greater than 4 mm indicates isolated deltoid ligament rupture and is the most reliable indicator of syndesmotic injury requiring operative repair of the medial ankle structures","B":"Tibiofibular clear space greater than 6 mm on the mortise view is the most reliable indicator of syndesmotic disruption and indicates instability requiring syndesmotic fixation","C":"Fibular shortening greater than 2 mm on the AP view is the primary indicator of syndesmotic injury because fibular length is always maintained when the syndesmotic ligaments are intact","D":"Loss of talar dome cartilage on MRI is the most reliable indicator of syndesmotic disruption, as osteochondral injury consistently accompanies complete syndesmotic ligament failure in ankle dislocations","E":"Tibiofibular overlap less than 10 mm on the AP view is the standard threshold for syndesmotic injury, while tibiofibular overlap measurement on the mortise view is unreliable due to projection variation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tibiofibular clear space >6 mm on the mortise view is the most reliable radiographic indicator of syndesmotic injury. Medial clear space widening indicates deltoid ligament disruption, which is a separate finding. Fibular shortening suggests a Maisonneuve or Weber C pattern. When the syndesmosis is disrupted, the mortise widens and the talus laterally shifts, causing the characteristic contact area reduction. Syndesmotic fixation (screws or flexible suture-button device) restores mortise anatomy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Syndesmotic injury = tibiofibular clear space >6 mm (mortise view); medial clear space widening = deltoid rupture; assess both views after ankle dislocation reduction. COMLEX: Ankle dislocation never left unreduced — skin necrosis risk; subtalar dislocation (talocalcaneal + talonavicular) is a separate entity from tibiotalar dislocation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: lateral ligamentous laxity and mortise stability principles bridge ankle sprain to talar dislocation pathomechanics"},{"id":"sdl25_q21","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the highest-priority management step immediately upon recognizing an ankle dislocation in the emergency setting, before advanced imaging is obtained?","choices":{"A":"Obtain a full MRI of the ankle before any reduction attempt to characterize all associated soft-tissue injuries and plan the definitive surgical approach for ligamentous reconstruction","B":"Apply a posterior splint in the dislocated position and admit for observation, because premature reduction attempts without orthopedic consultation risk conversion to an open dislocation","C":"Administer IV antibiotics prophylactically before any reduction attempt, because all ankle dislocations are considered contaminated injuries requiring antibiotic coverage prior to manipulation","D":"Prompt closed reduction must be performed immediately, because the ankle should never be left dislocated — skin tenting risks pressure necrosis, and neurovascular compromise worsens with delay","E":"Obtain CT angiography of the foot and ankle before reduction to document vascular anatomy, as reduction without vascular imaging risks embolism from disrupted ankle vessels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ankle dislocations must be reduced immediately — the ankle should never be left in a dislocated position. Skin tenting risks pressure necrosis and conversion to an open injury. Neurovascular compromise worsens with prolonged dislocation. Reduction is performed with adequate analgesia/sedation, followed by splinting, elevation, and post-reduction imaging. MRI is not a pre-reduction study. Antibiotics are indicated for open fractures/dislocations but not routinely before closed reduction. The reduction priority parallels hip and knee dislocation urgency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ankle dislocation = immediate reduction; never leave dislocated; post-reduction: splint, elevate, neurovascular check, AP/mortise/lateral x-rays; persistent instability → CRIF or ORIF. COMLEX: Subtalar dislocation (medial 85%, lateral 15%) is distinct — inversion produces medial subtalar dislocation; reduction under sedation, NWB cast 6 weeks.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: lateral ligamentous laxity and mortise stability principles bridge ankle sprain to talar dislocation pathomechanics","whyNot":{"A":"MRI is not a pre-reduction stu","C":"Antibiotics are indicated for "}},{"id":"sdl25_q22","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old male basketball player catches a fast-pass awkwardly, jamming the ring finger. He presents with swelling, point tenderness at the PIP joint, and dorsal displacement of the middle phalanx. Post-reduction X-ray shows a small volar plate avulsion fragment at the base of the middle phalanx with concentric joint reduction. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Digital nerve block followed by splinting the PIP joint at 30 degrees of flexion for 3 weeks, protecting the volar plate during healing while allowing early DIP and MCP range of motion","B":"Closed reduction under general anesthesia in the operating room, because all avulsion fractures at the base of the middle phalanx require intraoperative fluoroscopy to confirm fragment position before immobilization","C":"Immediate open reduction and internal fixation of the volar plate avulsion fragment regardless of fragment size, because any bony avulsion at the PIP joint destabilizes the flexor tendon sheath mechanism","D":"Hyperextend the PIP joint and apply a dorsal blocking splint in 90 degrees of flexion for 6 weeks, which is the correct technique for dorsal DIP dislocations but not for PIP volar plate avulsions","E":"Buddy tape the ring finger to the middle finger and allow immediate full active range of motion, because concentric reduction confirmed on X-ray means the joint is inherently stable without immobilization"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A concentric dorsal PIP dislocation with a small volar plate avulsion is managed with digital block, reduction, and splinting at 30° flexion for 3 weeks. This protects the healing volar plate while allowing adjacent joint motion. Surgical fixation is reserved for unstable fracture-dislocations with more than 40% of the middle phalanx base involved (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11) or irreducible dislocations. Buddy taping alone is insufficient for acute volar plate injuries. 90° flexion immobilization would cause flexion contracture. The avulsion fragment does not destabilize the flexor mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Small volar plate avulsion + concentric PIP reduction = splint at 30° flexion 3 weeks; fragment >40% of the middle phalanx base (CDT Orthopedics) or irreducible = operative. COMLEX: Irreducible PIP dislocation = interposed volar plate or FDP tendon blocking reduction; assess for associated osteochondral injury with X-ray.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — Connective tissue physiology: volar plate composition and attachment determine IP joint dislocation pattern and healing capacity after avulsion injury","whyNot":{"D":"90° flexion immobilization wou","E":"Buddy taping alone is insuffic"}},{"id":"sdl25_q23","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 27-year-old volleyball player jams her right ring finger blocking a spike. The PIP joint is dislocated sideways, and the finger points toward the ulnar side. After reduction under a digital block, the joint is tender along one side. According to the course reading, which ligament most likely ruptured, and why is the finger deviated ulnarly?","choices":{"A":"The ulnar collateral ligament, which is the more commonly torn PIP collateral, letting the finger drift toward the torn side","B":"The central slip of the extensor tendon, which lets the lateral bands slide ulnarly and pull the middle phalanx with them","C":"The flexor digitorum profundus tendon, which avulses from the distal phalanx and pulls the finger toward the ulnar side","D":"The radial collateral ligament, about six times more likely than the ulnar collateral ligament to rupture at the PIP joint","E":"The A1 pulley, which tears with the volar plate and lets the flexor tendons bowstring toward the ulnar side of the digit"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Tintinalli hand chapter assigned with this SDL states that lateral PIP dislocations occur when one collateral ligament ruptures, with at least a partial avulsion of the volar plate from the middle phalanx, and that the digit is usually ulnarly deviated because the radial collateral ligament is six times more likely than the ulnar collateral ligament to rupture. The ulnar collateral is the less commonly torn ligament. Central slip rupture is associated with volar PIP dislocations and causes a boutonniere pattern, not a lateral dislocation. FDP avulsion is a jersey finger at the DIP. The A1 pulley sits at the MCP level and is not the restraint for lateral PIP stability. After reduction, active motion and strength are tested; normal findings allow splinting at 30 degrees of flexion for 3 weeks, while an irreducible joint or complete ligament disruption needs operative repair.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PIP dislocations: dorsal (volar plate rupture, most common), lateral (collateral + partial volar plate, RCL > UCL), volar (central slip risk; splint PIP in extension). COMLEX: Test active motion after reduction; untreated volar plate injury → swan neck.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 4 — ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb MCP","whyNot":{"C":"FDP avulsion is a jersey finge"}},{"id":"sdl25_q24","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old male construction worker sustains a volar PIP dislocation — the middle phalanx is displaced volarly on the proximal phalanx. This injury is rare and requires careful evaluation. Which additional structure injury must be specifically assessed, and what deformity results if it is missed?","choices":{"A":"Flexor digitorum profundus avulsion must be assessed — volar PIP dislocation is caused by FDP rupture at the distal phalanx, and if missed leads to jersey finger deformity with DIP flexion deficit and a palpable mass in the palm","B":"Volar plate rupture must be assessed as the primary associated injury — in volar PIP dislocations the volar plate avulses volarly, and if untreated leads to swan neck deformity from the resulting PIP hyperextension","C":"Central extensor slip rupture must be assessed — volar PIP dislocation is associated with central slip injury, and if missed and left unsplinted in extension, progressive boutonnière deformity develops as the lateral bands migrate volarly","D":"Radial collateral ligament rupture must be assessed — radial collateral ligament is six times more likely to rupture than the ulnar collateral, and if missed leads to permanent lateral instability at the PIP joint","E":"Terminal extensor tendon avulsion must be assessed — volar displacement of the middle phalanx stretches the terminal tendon at the DIP, and if missed leads to mallet finger deformity with isolated DIP flexion deficit"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Volar PIP dislocation (middle phalanx displaced volarly) is the rare counterpart to the common dorsal dislocation. It is associated with central slip rupture — the extensor mechanism's middle slip that inserts on the dorsal base of the middle phalanx. If the central slip is disrupted and not splinted in full extension (6 weeks for acute closed central slip injuries, SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11), the lateral bands migrate volarly past the PIP axis, producing boutonnière deformity (PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension). This distinction from dorsal PIP dislocation management is critical.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Volar PIP dislocation → central slip rupture → boutonnière if untreated; splint PIP in full EXTENSION for 6 weeks (CDT Orthopedics) (opposite of dorsal PIP); allow active DIP flexion during immobilization to prevent DIP stiffness. COMLEX: Elson test (DIP extension with PIP held at 90° flexion) detects central slip integrity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — Connective tissue physiology: central slip extensor mechanism integrity at PIP joint determines whether volar dislocation produces boutonnière deformity on healing"},{"id":"sdl25_q25","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old male falls from a ladder, landing on an outstretched right hand. He presents with elbow pain, deformity, and a shortened forearm with the olecranon prominent posteriorly. X-ray confirms a posterior elbow dislocation without fracture. After successful closed reduction under conscious sedation, which of the following is the most appropriate immediate post-reduction management?","choices":{"A":"Apply a long arm cast in full extension for 6 weeks to allow complete capsuloligamentous healing before initiating any range of motion, which is the standard protocol for all elbow dislocations without fracture","B":"Perform immediate ORIF of the collateral ligaments and anterior capsule under general anesthesia, because all elbow dislocations result in complete ligamentous disruption requiring operative repair","C":"Obtain MRI arthrogram before allowing any range of motion to document the completeness of capsuloligamentous healing and determine if any occult osteochondral injuries require arthroscopic treatment","D":"Apply a circular cast in 30 degrees of elbow flexion for 8 weeks, because immobilization in flexion prevents posterior subluxation but is maintained at less than 90 degrees to avoid neurovascular compromise","E":"Assess neurovascular status and range of motion after reduction, then splint in 45 to 90 degrees of flexion and begin motion within 5 to 10 days to prevent stiffness, the most common complication"},"correct":"E","explanation":"After reduction of a simple posterior elbow dislocation (no fracture), the priority is neurovascular reassessment and range of motion testing to confirm stability. The elbow is splinted in 45 to 90 degrees of flexion, and motion is started as soon as possible, generally within 5 to 10 days (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 3), because elbow stiffness is the most common complication of elbow dislocation, and prolonged immobilization dramatically worsens outcomes. Long arm casting in extension is contraindicated. ORIF is reserved for unstable reductions or terrible triad injuries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Simple elbow dislocation → closed reduction → splint in 45-90° → motion within 5-10 days (CDT Orthopedics); stiffness = most common complication. COMLEX: Assess brachial artery and median/ulnar/radial nerves pre- and post-reduction; heterotopic ossification is a delayed complication of high-energy elbow dislocations treated with prolonged immobilization.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at elbow: posterior medial epicondyle anatomy makes the ulnar nerve vulnerable during elbow dislocation and a priority for post-reduction neurologic assessment","whyNot":{"B":"ORIF is reserved for unstable "}},{"id":"sdl25_q26","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 27-year-old female rugby player sustains a posterior elbow dislocation during a match. After closed reduction, she reports paresthesias along the medial aspect of the forearm and the medial one and a half digits, along with weakness of her grip and finger abduction. Which nerve is most likely injured, and what is the anatomic explanation?","choices":{"A":"Radial nerve — it spirals around the radial groove of the humerus and is injured during dislocation, producing wrist drop and loss of dorsal forearm and hand sensation from the posterior interosseous nerve branch","B":"Ulnar nerve — it courses in a superficial groove posterior to the medial epicondyle and is stretched or contused during dislocation, producing medial forearm and ring/small finger paresthesias with intrinsic hand weakness","C":"Median nerve — it runs anteromedially through the cubital fossa and is the most commonly injured nerve in elbow dislocation, producing thenar atrophy and radial 3.5 digit sensory loss as the primary deficit","D":"Medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve — it provides sensory innervation to the medial forearm and is the most commonly injured structure during elbow reduction maneuvers rather than the dislocation itself","E":"Anterior interosseous nerve — it branches from the median nerve just below the elbow and produces an isolated motor deficit of the FPL and index FDP without any sensory loss or intrinsic hand weakness"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ulnar nerve lies in the cubital tunnel posterior to the medial epicondyle and is the nerve most vulnerable during elbow dislocation due to this superficial medial position. Injury produces medial forearm paresthesias (dorsal and palmar medial forearm) and medial 1.5 digit numbness (ring and small finger), plus intrinsic hand weakness (interossei — finger abduction/adduction; hypothenar muscles). The radial nerve injury causes wrist drop. Median nerve injury produces thenar and lateral finger sensory loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ulnar nerve = medial epicondyle groove; elbow dislocation → ulnar nerve stretch; medial 1.5 digits + medial forearm sensory loss + intrinsic weakness. COMLEX: Cubital tunnel syndrome (SDL 14 Obj 3) involves same nerve at same location; elbow dislocation acutely recreates this anatomy with traction rather than compression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at elbow: post-medial epicondyle course creates identical vulnerability in both chronic cubital tunnel syndrome and acute elbow dislocation traction injury","whyNot":{"C":"Median nerve injury produces t"}},{"id":"sdl25_q27","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old male sustains a terrible triad elbow injury — posterior dislocation with radial head fracture and coronoid process fracture confirmed on CT. During surgical planning, the surgeon emphasizes that a specific fracture component is the most critical destabilizer of the elbow joint, even when the fragment appears small on imaging. Which structure is this?","choices":{"A":"Lateral epicondyle fracture — lateral epicondyle avulsion disrupts the common extensor origin and lateral ulnar collateral ligament origin, which is the primary restraint against posterolateral rotatory instability of the elbow","B":"Capitellum fracture — an associated capitellum fracture indicates a high-energy pattern that disrupts the radiocapitellar articulation, which is the primary lateral column stabilizer against valgus and axial loading","C":"Olecranon fracture — olecranon comminution is the defining feature of terrible triad injury and always requires plate fixation first because the triceps pull directly destabilizes the ulnohumeral articulation","D":"Coronoid process fracture — the coronoid is the primary anterior buttress preventing posterior ulnar translation, and even a small coronoid fragment removal destabilizes the elbow against posterior dislocation forces","E":"Radial styloid fracture — the radial styloid is the key lateral stabilizer of the forearm and its fracture predisposes to proximal radioulnar joint instability rather than distal radius shortening"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The coronoid process is the critical anterior buttress of the ulna that prevents posterior translation of the ulnohumeral joint. In the terrible triad, even a small (Regan-Morrey Type I or II) coronoid fragment, if excised or not repaired, allows recurrent posterior elbow instability. Surgical management prioritizes coronoid fixation or reconstruction first, then radial head fixation or arthroplasty, then lateral collateral ligament repair. The radial head provides lateral column support but the coronoid is the key to preventing re-dislocation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Terrible triad = dislocation + radial head fracture + coronoid fracture; coronoid = anterior buttress; surgical priority = coronoid → radial head → LCL repair. COMLEX: Even small coronoid fragments (Regan-Morrey Type I) have clinical significance in the setting of elbow instability; stiffness remains the most common post-operative complication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve at elbow: the medial anatomy of the elbow including the coronoid and medial epicondyle shares surgical planes with the ulnar nerve requiring careful protection during terrible triad repair"},{"id":"sdl25_q28","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 20-year-old male college football player sustains his first anterior glenohumeral dislocation and undergoes successful closed reduction. He is immobilized in a sling for 4 weeks and completes physical therapy. Over the next year he has two more dislocations with progressively less force. MRI confirms a Bankart lesion and a Hill-Sachs lesion. What is the most appropriate next management step?","choices":{"A":"Repeat conservative management with a longer immobilization period of 8 to 12 weeks in internal rotation to allow the Bankart lesion to heal without surgery, as recurrence after two dislocations does not yet meet operative criteria","B":"Surgical stabilization including Bankart repair with labral reattachment to the anteroinferior glenoid rim and anteroinferior capsular tightening, given failure of conservative treatment and high recurrence risk in young active patients","C":"Physical therapy exclusively focusing on posterior capsule and internal rotator strengthening for 6 additional months before considering surgery, because strengthening the dynamic stabilizers eliminates most recurrent dislocation risk","D":"Glenohumeral corticosteroid injection to reduce capsular inflammation and restore normal joint mechanics, which is the recommended intervention for recurrent instability in young athletes before surgical consultation","E":"Reverse total shoulder arthroplasty to replace the damaged glenohumeral joint surfaces, which is the definitive surgical treatment for young patients with recurrent anterior instability and associated Hill-Sachs lesions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Young patients (<25 years) with recurrent anterior glenohumeral dislocation have a high risk of re-dislocation if treated conservatively (SDL 25 reading) and should be counseled about surgical stabilization after failed conservative management. Arthroscopic Bankart repair (labral reattachment + anteroinferior capsular tightening) is the standard surgical approach. Large Hill-Sachs lesions may require remplissage or Latarjet procedure. Corticosteroids and additional PT are not appropriate for recurrent instability. Reverse shoulder arthroplasty is for rotator cuff arthropathy in elderly patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Young patient + recurrent anterior dislocation → Bankart repair; Hill-Sachs + Bankart lesions = classic pair in recurrent anterior instability; glenoid bone loss >20% → Latarjet coracoid transfer. COMLEX: TUBS = Traumatic, Unidirectional, Bankart lesion, Surgical treatment; AMBRI = Atraumatic, Multidirectional, Bilateral, Rehabilitation, Inferior capsular shift.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries: rotator cuff tear occurs in >40% of patients over 40 with anterior shoulder dislocation, requiring MRI evaluation concurrent with instability workup before definitive surgical planning","whyNot":{"C":"Corticosteroids and additional","D":"Corticosteroids and additional","E":"Reverse shoulder arthroplasty "}},{"id":"sdl25_q29","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old male with a history of alcohol-use disorder is brought to the emergency department after a witnessed generalized tonic-clonic seizure. He complains of right shoulder pain and cannot lift his arm. His arm is held with the hand on his abdomen and he is unable to externally rotate the shoulder. The AP radiograph appears nearly normal. What is the diagnosis and what imaging confirms it?","choices":{"A":"Frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis) — the insidious onset and limited external rotation in this older patient with a comorbid condition is most consistent with adhesive capsulitis, confirmed by MRI arthrogram showing capsular thickening","B":"Anterior glenohumeral dislocation — the mechanism of seizure causes anterior capsule failure, and the AP radiograph typically confirms anterior displacement clearly without need for additional imaging views","C":"Acromioclavicular joint separation — the seizure causes a direct downward force on the shoulder girdle, and the Zanca view best demonstrates the degree of coracoclavicular ligament disruption and clavicle displacement","D":"Rotator cuff tear with pseudoparalysis — seizure-related muscle contractions avulse the supraspinatus from the greater tuberosity, and ultrasound of the subacromial space confirms the full-thickness tear causing apparent weakness","E":"Posterior glenohumeral dislocation — classic after seizures and electrocution; the AP view may look nearly normal, and an axillary lateral or Velpeau view confirms it and shows a reverse Hill-Sachs lesion anteriorly"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Posterior glenohumeral dislocation is classically missed because the AP radiograph may appear nearly normal or show only a \"light bulb sign\" (internal rotation of the humeral head). The arm is held in internal rotation (hand on belly) and external rotation is impossible — the classic locked shoulder of posterior dislocation. Seizures, lightning strikes, and electrocution are the classic atraumatic mechanisms (bilateral symmetric muscle contractions). The axillary lateral view confirms the diagnosis and shows the reverse Hill-Sachs (anterior humeral head compression). Reduction requires longitudinal traction + anterior-directed force, then immobilize in external rotation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior dislocation = seizure, lightning, electrocution; arm in internal rotation, cannot externally rotate; AP may look normal (lightbulb sign); axillary lateral confirms; immobilize in EXTERNAL rotation. COMLEX: Posterior dislocation is the \"great masquerader\"; always obtain axillary lateral view in any shoulder with locked internal rotation after seizure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries: posterior dislocation can avulse the lesser tuberosity via subscapularis traction, just as anterior dislocation avulses the greater tuberosity via supraspinatus/infraspinatus traction in patients over 40"},{"id":"sdl25_q30","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 23-year-old female volleyball player sustains a first-time anterior shoulder dislocation reduced in the emergency department. After reduction, she develops numbness over her lateral deltoid and cannot maintain her arm in extension when the shoulder is passively extended and released. Which nerve is injured and what specific test confirms the motor deficit?","choices":{"A":"Axillary nerve injury — the deltoid extension lag test confirms motor deficit: the shoulder is passively extended and the patient cannot hold the position because the deltoid (primary extensor at this range) is denervated","B":"Suprascapular nerve injury — confirmed by the empty can test showing weakness of supraspinatus-mediated abduction and the internal rotation lag sign showing infraspinatus deficit in external rotation arc","C":"Radial nerve injury — confirmed by wrist drop and loss of active extension at the wrist, thumb, and fingers, with sensory loss over the dorsal forearm and hand rather than the lateral deltoid region","D":"Musculocutaneous nerve injury — confirmed by loss of elbow flexion and forearm supination with sensory loss over the lateral forearm (lateral antebrachial cutaneous nerve distribution) rather than the shoulder","E":"Long thoracic nerve injury — confirmed by scapular winging on the wall push-up test due to serratus anterior denervation, which presents with lateral winging when the arm is forward flexed against resistance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Axillary nerve injury is confirmed by the deltoid extension lag sign: the shoulder is passively extended to end range and the patient cannot actively hold the position. This tests deltoid function (specifically the posterior deltoid for extension) while avoiding supraspinatus compensation. Sensory loss over the lateral deltoid (regimental badge area) confirms the diagnosis. The suprascapular nerve supplies supraspinatus and infraspinatus (not deltoid). The radial nerve injury produces wrist drop. The long thoracic nerve serves serratus anterior.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Axillary nerve = surgical neck humerus/shoulder dislocation; deltoid extension lag test = most specific motor test; sensory = lateral deltoid. COMLEX: Assess axillary nerve before AND after reduction; dead arm syndrome = transient axillary nerve compression resolving within minutes from brief subluxation during athletic activity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries: axillary nerve injury post-dislocation must be distinguished from rotator cuff tear as a cause of deltoid weakness, as both present with limited shoulder function but require different rehabilitation approaches","whyNot":{"B":"The suprascapular nerve suppli"}},{"id":"sdl25_q31","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old male unrestrained front-seat passenger is brought in after a head-on MVC. He has right hip pain with the limb held in flexion, adduction, and internal rotation. X-ray confirms a posterior hip dislocation without associated fracture. He is hemodynamically stable. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","choices":{"A":"Emergent open reduction in the operating room for all posterior hip dislocations regardless of associated fractures, because closed reduction risks sciatic nerve traction injury and inadequate acetabular visualization","B":"CT scan of the pelvis before any reduction attempt to rule out associated acetabular fractures, because unrecognized intra-articular fragments during closed reduction cause iatrogenic femoral head cartilage damage","C":"Apply femoral skeletal traction overnight and schedule reduction in the morning operating room to allow muscle relaxation, which reduces the force required for reduction and minimizes sciatic nerve traction risk","D":"Urgent closed reduction under conscious sedation as soon as possible, preferably within the first few hours, using the Allis method, as the odds of AVN rise about 5.6-fold when reduction is delayed beyond 12 hours","E":"Discharge with non-weight-bearing crutches and outpatient orthopedic referral within 3 to 5 days, because isolated posterior hip dislocation without fracture can be safely managed as an elective reduction procedure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Posterior hip dislocation requires urgent closed reduction, preferably within the first few hours (SDL 25 reading); time to reduction is the most critical determinant of AVN risk, and hips reduced after 12 hours have about 5.6 times the odds of AVN (course text). The Allis method: patient supine, assistant stabilizes the pelvis, surgeon applies axial traction with hip and knee at 90° flexion, then gentle internal→external rotation to reduce. A post-reduction CT is obtained after reduction (not before, unless the patient is going directly to OR) to check for intra-articular fragments and associated fractures. Delaying for CT or OR scheduling dramatically increases AVN risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip dislocation → reduce as soon as possible, preferably within the first few hours (Allis or Stimson methods); post-reduction CT to assess for intra-articular fragments; AVN follow-up with MRI (AVN can appear as late as 2 years). COMLEX: Posterior hip dislocation = CP8 named condition; 10-20% sciatic nerve injury rate; assess foot drop pre- and post-reduction; pelvis/sacral somatic dysfunction common after high-energy hip trauma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: medial circumflex femoral artery disruption in posterior hip dislocation directly bridges the mechanism of AVN and explains the 6-hour reduction urgency window","whyNot":{"C":"Delaying for CT or OR scheduli"}},{"id":"sdl25_q32","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old male undergoes successful closed reduction of a posterior hip dislocation sustained in an MVC. Post-reduction CT confirms concentric reduction with a small posterior acetabular wall fragment and no intra-articular loose bodies. Hip stability is confirmed through full range of motion. Over the next 2 years, which complication remains the greatest concern for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Avascular necrosis (AVN) of the femoral head — disruption of the posterior capsule and retinacular vessels (branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery) during dislocation can cause ischemia that manifests as AVN months to 2 years after injury","B":"Sciatic nerve palsy that is permanent and will not recover — sciatic nerve injuries from hip dislocation uniformly progress to complete motor and sensory loss that fails to recover regardless of reduction timing or decompression","C":"Heterotopic ossification at the posterior acetabulum requiring prophylaxis — indomethacin is required for all posterior hip dislocations because heterotopic ossification predictably limits hip flexion to less than 90 degrees within 6 months","D":"Post-traumatic hip osteoarthritis developing within 6 months — cartilage damage from the dislocation produces symptomatic OA within 6 months requiring total hip arthroplasty in all patients under 35 years of age","E":"Deep vein thrombosis from enforced bed rest — immobility after hip dislocation causes DVT in more than 80% of patients, making anticoagulation with therapeutic heparin the most urgent management priority post-reduction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AVN of the femoral head is the most feared long-term complication after hip dislocation. The medial circumflex femoral artery supplies the femoral head retinacular vessels, which are disrupted during posterior dislocation. AVN may not manifest clinically or radiographically for months to 2 years, requiring serial MRI surveillance. Sciatic nerve injuries from hip dislocation can recover partially or fully. Heterotopic ossification occurs but is not universal. Post-traumatic OA develops over years, not months.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AVN after hip dislocation = medial circumflex femoral artery (retinacular branches); onset 1-2 years after injury; follow with MRI; core decompression or THA if symptomatic. COMLEX: Protected weight-bearing post-reduction; serial follow-up for 2 years; hip rehabilitation OMM can address surrounding soft-tissue dysfunction without loading the compromised femoral head.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: femoral head blood supply via medial circumflex femoral artery is the shared anatomic basis for AVN in both hip dislocation and femoral neck fracture contexts","whyNot":{"D":"Post-traumatic OA develops ove"}},{"id":"sdl25_q33","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has a posterior hip dislocation closed-reduced under general anesthesia. The hip is stable through the range of motion, but the post-reduction AP pelvis shows the right hip joint space wider than the left. Which interpretation and next step best match the course reading?","choices":{"A":"Expected capsular swelling after reduction; begin full weight bearing without restriction and repeat films in 6 weeks","B":"Recurrent posterior dislocation; repeat the Allis maneuver at the bedside without imaging and then place an abduction brace","C":"Nonconcentric reduction from soft tissue or bone fragment interposition; obtain CT and plan arthroscopy or arthrotomy","D":"Early avascular necrosis of the femoral head; obtain an MRI now, since joint widening is the first sign of collapse","E":"A normal variant of hip anatomy; compare with old films and discharge if the patient can walk without pain today"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Current Diagnosis and Treatment in Orthopedics chapter states that after reduction an AP pelvis is obtained, and that soft tissue or bone fragment interposition shows as widening of the joint space compared with the opposite side. Indications for surgery include irreducible dislocations, nonconcentric reductions, open dislocations, ipsilateral femoral neck fractures, and an unstable hip, especially with a posterior wall fracture. CT can identify loose bodies or other fractures, and hip arthroscopy may be a safer alternative to arthrotomy for removing loose bodies. Asymmetric widening is not expected swelling, and unrestricted weight bearing would be inappropriate. A widened but located joint is not a redislocation. AVN can appear as late as 2 years after injury and is not shown by acute joint space widening. Asymmetry should not be dismissed as a normal variant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Post-reduction hip checklist: concentric reduction on AP pelvis, CT for loose bodies and acetabular/femoral head fractures, sciatic nerve exam before and after reduction. COMLEX: A new nerve deficit after reduction warrants exploration for nerve entrapment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — avascular necrosis after hip injury","whyNot":{"B":"A widened but located joint is","D":"AVN can appear as late as 2 ye"}},{"id":"sdl25_q34","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old obese male (BMI 44) trips stepping off a curb and presents with a grossly unstable knee. X-ray confirms a tibiofemoral dislocation. He is hemodynamically stable and the dislocation is reduced. Post-reduction ABI on the affected limb is 0.71. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Close observation with serial pulse checks every 4 hours for 72 hours, because an ABI between 0.7 and 0.9 represents mild flow reduction from post-reduction edema and does not require immediate vascular imaging","B":"Immediate surgical exploration of the popliteal fossa without vascular imaging, because any ABI below 1.0 mandates emergent operative vascular repair to prevent limb-threatening ischemia after knee dislocation","C":"Apply a knee immobilizer and obtain MRI of the knee ligaments before vascular evaluation, because multiligamentous injury documentation is required for surgical planning before addressing vascular status","D":"Repeat the ABI measurement in 6 hours after the swelling resolves from initial reduction, because edema-related ABI reductions routinely normalize within 6 hours and do not require immediate CTA","E":"CT angiography of the lower extremity to evaluate for popliteal artery injury, because an ABI below 0.9 is the threshold mandating vascular imaging regardless of the presence or absence of distal pulses"},"correct":"E","explanation":"An ABI below 0.9 after knee dislocation mandates CT angiography regardless of pulse presence. The popliteal artery is tethered at the knee and commonly injured in knee dislocations, sometimes with initially preserved distal pulses from collateral flow. Intimal injuries can cause delayed thrombosis. Vascular status must be reassessed for 72 hours post-reduction. Low-energy dislocations in obese patients are increasingly recognized, and the same vascular urgency applies. MRI evaluation waits until vascular status is confirmed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee dislocation + ABI <0.9 → CT angiography; popliteal artery injury can present with normal pulses initially (intimal tear); monitor vascular status 72 hours. COMLEX: Low-energy knee dislocation in morbidly obese patients = emerging clinical pattern; same ABI threshold and vascular urgency as high-energy dislocations applies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL injuries: multiligamentous knee instability after dislocation involves the same ligamentous structures as isolated ACL/PCL injuries but with greater severity and obligate vascular risk assessment requirement","whyNot":{"C":"MRI evaluation waits until vas"}},{"id":"sdl25_q35","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old male motorcycle rider is brought to the emergency department after a high-speed crash with a traumatic knee dislocation that spontaneously reduced before EMS arrival. CTA is normal. Physical examination reveals gross anteroposterior and mediolateral instability consistent with multiligamentous disruption including the ACL, PCL, and posterolateral complex. He is a competitive cyclist. What is the appropriate surgical management recommendation?","choices":{"A":"Conservative management with hinged knee bracing and physical therapy is equivalent to surgery for all knee dislocations and is the preferred approach because surgical complication rates exceed re-dislocation risk in braced patients","B":"Surgical reconstruction of all injured ligamentous structures is recommended for young, active patients, because a complete knee dislocation tears multiple supporting ligaments and bracing alone cannot restore stability","C":"Only the ACL and PCL require reconstruction after complete knee dislocation, because the collateral ligaments predictably heal with conservative management and do not contribute meaningfully to long-term instability","D":"Surgical reconstruction should be deferred for at least 6 months to allow spontaneous ligamentous healing and scar formation, which often restores sufficient stability without requiring operative intervention in young patients","E":"Only elderly or sedentary patients benefit from surgical reconstruction of knee dislocation; young athletes universally recover full function with an accelerated physical therapy protocol without operative intervention"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Young, active patients with complete knee dislocation and multiligamentous injury should undergo surgical reconstruction of all injured structures. The knee cannot be stabilized adequately by bracing alone when the ACL, PCL, and lateral complex are all disrupted. Early surgical reconstruction (typically staged with initial spanning external fixation if needed) optimizes functional outcomes and return to sport. The spontaneous reduction before ED arrival does not diminish the severity — multiligamentous instability defines the injury pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee dislocation = multiligamentous injury requiring reconstruction in young active patients; staged approach common; neurovascular assessment before any surgical intervention. COMLEX: Knee dislocation = CP8 named condition; peroneal nerve injury (foot drop) assessed separately from vascular injury; CPM and early PT post-operatively.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL injuries: isolated ligament reconstruction techniques from ACL/PCL repair are combined in knee dislocation surgery, which requires multiligamentous reconstruction in a single or staged operative setting","whyNot":{"A":"The knee cannot be stabilized "}},{"id":"sdl25_q36","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman sustains a traumatic knee dislocation in a motor vehicle collision. The dislocation is reduced and her initial ABI is 0.95. Four hours after reduction, her leg becomes progressively pale, cool, and painful with passive stretch of her toes. What do these findings indicate and what is the immediate management?","choices":{"A":"Progressive pallor and coolness are expected post-reduction findings from reactive edema compressing superficial veins, and the appropriate management is elevation and compression dressing with reassessment in 24 hours","B":"Cold extremity and pain with passive stretch after knee dislocation indicate peroneal nerve injury causing loss of normal pain signaling, requiring only splinting in neutral ankle position to prevent foot drop contracture","C":"These findings indicate normal post-reduction inflammation and should be observed for 72 hours without intervention, as current guidelines recommend conservative vascular monitoring before any surgical exploration after knee dislocation","D":"Ischemia time exceeding 4 hours is associated with poor prognosis and amputation risk; these findings indicate evolving vascular occlusion or compartment syndrome requiring emergent vascular repair plus fasciotomy to prevent irreversible limb ischemia","E":"Pale and cool extremity after knee dislocation reduction indicates venous obstruction from hemarthrosis, which responds to aspiration of the joint effusion and repeat Doppler ultrasound to confirm venous patency"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Progressive pallor, coolness, and pain with passive stretch 4 hours after reduction represent a vascular emergency. Ischemia beyond 4 hours after knee dislocation dramatically worsens prognosis and amputation risk. Pain with passive stretch of the toes is also an early compartment syndrome sign. This pattern requires immediate surgical vascular repair (typically popliteal artery bypass or interposition graft) plus four-compartment fasciotomy. An initial ABI of 0.95 can normalize transiently due to vasospasm, then worsen from intimal tear thrombosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ischemia >4 hours after knee dislocation = poor prognosis; emergent vascular repair + fasciotomy; pain with passive stretch = compartment syndrome sign. COMLEX: Monitor for 72 hours even with initially normal vascular exam; compartment syndrome of the leg is a named complication of knee dislocation vascular repair requiring clinical vigilance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL injuries: the popliteal fossa neurovascular bundle passes posterior to both the ACL and PCL attachment regions, explaining the proximity that makes it vulnerable in complete knee dislocation with multiligamentous disruption"},{"id":"sdl25_q37","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old female gymnast sustains a lateral patellar dislocation that spontaneously reduces when she extends her knee. MRI confirms a complete MPFL tear at the femoral attachment, severe trochlear dysplasia (flat trochlear groove), and patella alta. Which factor most significantly increases her risk of recurrent dislocation?","choices":{"A":"Patient age over 40 years is the strongest predictor of recurrent patellar dislocation, because older patients have greater quadriceps atrophy and VMO weakness that reduces dynamic patellar stabilization during activity","B":"Prior ACL reconstruction is a major risk factor for recurrent patellar dislocation because the graft harvest creates a lateral retinacular imbalance that shifts the patellar tracking vector toward the lateral compartment","C":"Obesity with BMI over 30 is the primary structural risk factor for recurrent lateral patellar dislocation because increased weight increases the Q-angle and the lateral force vector on the patella with every step","D":"Isolated lateral retinaculum tightness without any bony dysplasia is the primary anatomic risk factor for recurrence, because lateral retinacular tightness is the single most important structure preventing medial patellar reduction","E":"Trochlear dysplasia — a flat or convex trochlear groove provides minimal bony constraint to lateral displacement, leaving the patella dependent on soft-tissue restraints that are already disrupted by the injury"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Trochlear dysplasia is the strongest anatomic risk factor for recurrent patellar dislocation. A flat or convex trochlear groove fails to provide the bony constraint that normally stabilizes the patella during flexion. Combined with MPFL disruption and patella alta, trochlear dysplasia creates the highest-risk triad for recurrence. Surgical management in this patient may include MPFL reconstruction and trochleoplasty. Young age (not older age) is actually a risk factor for recurrence. Prior ACL reconstruction and obesity are not classic risk factors for patellar instability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Patellar instability risk triad = trochlear dysplasia + patella alta + MPFL tear; elevated TT-TG distance = indication for tibial tubercle medialization. COMLEX: CP8 named condition; Dejour classification grades trochlear dysplasia A-D; Grade B-D dysplasia most likely to require trochleoplasty in addition to MPFL reconstruction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: trochlear morphology is the key bony determinant of both patellofemoral syndrome (contact stress) and patellar dislocation (bony constraint deficit), sharing the same anatomic framework","whyNot":{"A":"Young age (not older age) is a","C":"Prior ACL reconstruction and o"}},{"id":"sdl25_q38","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old male soccer player plants his foot and pivots, feeling his patella \"pop out\" and then immediately reduce. On examination he has medial patellar pole tenderness, a moderate hemarthrosis, and positive apprehension sign with lateral patellar pressure. What is the biomechanical explanation for why the patella spontaneously reduced when he extended his knee?","choices":{"A":"Extension tightens the quadriceps mechanism and pulls the patella proximally and centrally into the trochlear groove; as the knee extends, rising patellar tendon tension realigns the patella medially within the groove","B":"Medial retinaculum contraction actively generates sufficient force to pull the displaced patella back into the trochlear groove during knee extension, providing the primary active stabilization of the medially displaced patella","C":"Gravity acts on the laterally displaced patella when the patient stands upright, producing a medially directed force vector that passively returns the patella to the midline position of the trochlear groove on weight-bearing","D":"Hamstring relaxation during knee extension releases the posterolateral tightening that maintained the lateral dislocation, allowing the medial retinaculum tension alone to centralize the patella into the trochlear groove","E":"VMO spasm following lateral dislocation generates an acute medially directed contractile force that pulls the patella rapidly back across the trochlear groove, explaining the audible clunk heard at the moment of spontaneous reduction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Spontaneous patellar reduction with knee extension occurs because quadriceps tension increases as the knee extends. This proximal and central traction pulls the patella back into the trochlear groove. As the knee approaches full extension, the tightened quadriceps vector becomes increasingly perpendicular to the patella, providing the centralization force. This is why lateral patellar dislocations almost always reduce spontaneously with extension — the quadriceps mechanism acts as the reduction force without manual assistance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Patellar dislocation spontaneously reduces with knee extension via quadriceps centralization; hemarthrosis + medial patellar pole tenderness + positive apprehension = classic post-dislocation triad; X-ray after spontaneous reduction confirms osteochondral fragment risk. COMLEX: Medial patellar pole tenderness = MPFL femoral attachment site; check sunrise view for osteochondral injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: quadriceps force vector and Q-angle mechanics that produce patellofemoral stress in PFS are the same biomechanical framework that drives lateral patellar dislocation and spontaneous reduction with extension"},{"id":"sdl25_q39","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old male has sustained three lateral patellar dislocations over 18 months despite completing a structured physical therapy program including VMO strengthening, taping, and bracing. MRI shows a complete MPFL tear at the femoral attachment. Imaging shows a laterally positioned tibial tubercle that pulls the patella laterally. What is the most appropriate surgical intervention?","choices":{"A":"Isolated lateral retinacular release alone, which reduces the lateral tethering force on the patella and corrects the imbalanced Q-angle effect without requiring MPFL reconstruction or bony realignment surgery","B":"MPFL reconstruction combined with a tibial tubercle transfer that moves the patellar tendon insertion medially, correcting both the soft-tissue restraint deficit and the lateral quadriceps pull on the patella","C":"Total knee replacement for end-stage patellofemoral arthritis, which is the definitive treatment for recurrent patellar instability in young patients who have failed conservative management and MPFL reconstruction","D":"Patellectomy to eliminate the unstable patella entirely, which resolves recurrent lateral dislocation by removing the dislocating structure and has acceptable long-term functional outcomes in young active patients","E":"Quadriceps tendon repair with medial retinaculum reefing alone, which provides sufficient medial stabilization in all patients regardless of tibial tubercle position or the number of prior dislocations"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course reading for SDL 25 (Current Diagnosis & Treatment in Orthopedics) notes that MPFL tears are complete in over 50% of patellar dislocations, most often at the femoral attachment, and that operative repair is best for chronic, recurrent dislocation. The SDL 4 reading (Practical Office Orthopedics) states that an MPFL reconstruction can help stabilize patellae prone to lateral tracking, as can a tibial tubercle transfer that moves the patellar tendon insertion medially to change the angle of tracking. With a torn MPFL and a laterally placed tubercle, combining the two addresses both problems. A lateral release alone loosens only the lateral retinaculum and leaves the torn MPFL and the lateral pull untreated; total knee replacement is not a treatment for instability in a young patient; excision of the patella costs strength and causes pain; and quadriceps tendon repair treats a different injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recurrent lateral patellar dislocation after failed rehabilitation → operative repair (course SDL 25 reading); MPFL reconstruction restores the medial restraint, and a tibial tubercle transfer moves the patellar tendon insertion medially to correct lateral tracking (SDL 4 reading). COMLEX: MPFL tears are complete in over 50% of dislocations, most often at the femoral attachment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: TT-TG distance is the same measurement used to evaluate patellofemoral syndrome malalignment, bridging PFS diagnosis to surgical planning for recurrent lateral patellar instability","whyNot":{"A":"A lateral release alone loosen","D":"A lateral release alone loosen","E":"A lateral release alone loosen"}},{"id":"sdl25_q40","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old male basketball player lands awkwardly from a jump, sustaining obvious ankle deformity. X-ray confirms talar dislocation from the mortise. The skin over the lateral malleolus is tented and beginning to blanch. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","choices":{"A":"Obtain MRI before any reduction attempt to fully characterize all soft-tissue and osteochondral injuries prior to deciding between closed and open reduction techniques in the operating room","B":"Open reduction and internal fixation in the operating room is required before any closed reduction is attempted, because tented skin indicates soft-tissue interposition that will block closed reduction","C":"Immediate closed reduction — tented, blanching skin signals imminent pressure necrosis; reduce under procedural sedation, splint, elevate, and confirm mortise congruency on post-reduction imaging","D":"Apply a compression dressing and elevate the ankle overnight while awaiting orthopedic consultation in the morning, because the overlying skin blanching reflects normal post-injury inflammatory vasospasm rather than ischemia","E":"Perform bedside reduction only after general anesthesia is established in the operating room, because adequate muscle relaxation is required for all ankle dislocations and procedural sedation is insufficient"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ankle dislocations are orthopedic emergencies requiring immediate reduction. The ankle should never be left in a dislocated position — skin tenting with blanching signals imminent pressure necrosis and risk of skin breakdown/open dislocation. Procedural sedation is appropriate for ED reduction. MRI is not a pre-reduction study in this emergency. The post-reduction priority is confirming mortise congruency on X-ray, assessing neurovascular status, and determining if persistent instability requires operative stabilization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ankle dislocation = immediate reduction; never leave dislocated; skin tenting = emergency; post-reduction: splint, elevate, mortise/AP/lateral views, neurovascular check. COMLEX: Ankle dislocation = CP8 named condition; persistent instability after reduction → CRIF or ORIF; assess for associated distal fibula fracture on post-reduction films.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: the same anterolateral ligamentous complex (ATFL, CFL) that fails in lateral ankle sprains forms the primary restraint against talar dislocation, with mortise stability dependent on intact syndesmosis and medial structures","whyNot":{"A":"MRI is not a pre-reduction stu","E":"Procedural sedation is appropr"}},{"id":"sdl25_q41","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 51-year-old woman has a trimalleolar ankle fracture-dislocation. It is reduced promptly in the emergency department, splinted, and elevated. Post-reduction radiographs show that the talus will not stay centered in the mortise, and the soft tissues are badly swollen with fracture blisters. Which management does the course reading describe for this situation?","choices":{"A":"Closed reduction with external fixation, since the ankle is unstable after reduction and the skin does not yet allow ORIF","B":"Immediate open reduction and internal fixation through the blistered skin, since any delay increases the risk of arthritis","C":"Leave the ankle in its current position in a splint for 6 weeks, since repeated manipulation further damages the soft tissue","D":"Below-knee amputation, since a trimalleolar fracture-dislocation with blisters cannot be salvaged once swelling develops","E":"Weight bearing as tolerated in a walking boot, since loading the ankle pushes the talus back into the center of the mortise"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ankle section of the Current Diagnosis and Treatment in Orthopedics chapter states that a dislocated ankle should first be reduced, splinted, elevated, and imaged after reduction. If the ankle is still unstable after reduction, either closed reduction with external fixation or ORIF (if the soft tissues allow) should be performed, and an ankle should never be left dislocated. With severe swelling and fracture blisters, external fixation holds the reduction until the soft tissues recover. ORIF through compromised skin ignores the soft tissue condition. Leaving the talus out of position for 6 weeks would allow malunion and arthritis; a lateral talar shift of 1 mm cuts tibiotalar contact by 40%. Amputation is not indicated. Weight bearing will not hold an unstable mortise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ankle fracture goals: reduce dislocations promptly, restore joint surfaces, hold reduction until healed, mobilize early; in trimalleolar ORIF the lateral malleolus is usually fixed first. COMLEX: Open fracture-dislocation → IV antibiotics and urgent irrigation and debridement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 8 — trimalleolar ankle fractures and the posterior malleolus","whyNot":{"B":"ORIF through compromised skin ","C":"Leaving the talus out of posit"}},{"id":"sdl25_q42","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old male sustains a severe ankle inversion injury in a trail-running fall. He presents with the foot inverted and internally rotated in a locked position that will not correct with gentle manipulation. X-ray shows simultaneous dislocation of both the talocalcaneal and talonavicular joints without a talar neck fracture. What is the most likely diagnosis and predominant direction?","choices":{"A":"Lateral subtalar dislocation from an eversion mechanism is the most common pattern, accounting for 70% of subtalar dislocations, where the calcaneus and forefoot displace laterally while the talus remains in the ankle mortise","B":"Superior subtalar dislocation from an axial compression mechanism is the most common pattern, where the calcaneus is driven superiorly against the talar body during a fall from height onto the heel with foot in neutral position","C":"Anterior subtalar dislocation from a plantarflexion mechanism is the most frequent presentation, where the calcaneus displaces anteriorly on the talus while the talonavicular joint simultaneously subluxes dorsally","D":"Rotatory subtalar dislocation from a combined axial load and twisting mechanism is the most common pattern, where the calcaneus rotates on the talar axis producing a spiral dissociation of both subtalar and midtarsal joints simultaneously","E":"Medial subtalar dislocation — the talocalcaneal and talonavicular joints dislocate without talar neck fracture; an inversion mechanism displaces the calcaneus and forefoot medially in approximately 85% of subtalar dislocations"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Subtalar dislocation involves simultaneous dislocation of the talocalcaneal (subtalar) and talonavicular joints with an intact talar neck — distinct from tibiotalar dislocation. Medial subtalar dislocation (foot inverted and internally rotated) accounts for approximately 85% of cases from inversion injury. Lateral subtalar dislocation (eversion mechanism) accounts for 15% and has worse prognosis due to associated open injury risk. Management is closed reduction under sedation, NWB short leg cast for 6 weeks. Open subtalar dislocations have high infection risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Subtalar dislocation = talocalcaneal + talonavicular simultaneous dislocation with intact talar neck; medial 85% (inversion), lateral 15% (eversion); reduce under sedation, NWB cast 6 weeks. COMLEX: Distinguish from tibiotalar dislocation on X-ray by noting talar position within mortise; subtalar joint is a CP8-adjacent structure in foot and ankle OMM.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: severe inversion mechanism from ankle sprain can progress to subtalar dislocation when ligamentous disruption extends from the lateral ankle ligaments to involve the talocalcaneal interosseous ligament and talonavicular capsule"}]};
